        <dc:title>OpenOOD: Benchmarking Generalized Out-of-Distribution Detection</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection is vital to safety-critical machine learning applications and has thus been extensively studied, with a plethora of methods developed in the literature. However, the field currently lacks a unified, strictly formulated, and comprehensive benchmark, which often results in unfair comparisons and inconclusive results. From the problem setting perspective, OOD detection is closely related to neighboring fields including anomaly detection (AD), open set recognition (OSR), and model uncertainty, since methods developed for one domain are often applicable to each other. To help the community to improve the evaluation and advance, we build a unified, well-structured codebase called OpenOOD, which implements over 30 methods developed in relevant fields and provides a comprehensive benchmark under the recently proposed generalized OOD detection framework. With a comprehensive comparison of these methods, we are gratified that the field has progressed significantly over the past few years, where both preprocessing methods and the orthogonal post-hoc methods show strong potential.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2022-10-13</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>OpenOOD</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07242</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-15 07:42:00</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2210.07242 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2210.07242</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2210.07242</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_9">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Accepted by NeurIPS 2022 Datasets and Benchmarks Track. Codebase: https://github.com/Jingkang50/OpenOOD&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: très bon papier, bonne overview de tout ce qui existe comme méthode OOD post-hoc quelque soit le domaine.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Closely related topics that can improve OOD: anomaly detection (AD), open set recognition (OSR), model uncertainty, data augmentation&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;OSR&lt;/strong&gt; is used in classification tasks where the model must not only recognize known classes but also detect and reject inputs from entirely unknown classes. &lt;em&gt;ex: divide the categories of existing datasets into two parts, called closed and open set. Machine learning models are trained only on the closed set. When testing, the models are evaluated on the entire test set and need to separate open set samples from closed set samples.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;OOD&lt;/strong&gt; detection focuses more broadly on identifying whether an input comes from a different data distribution than the one the model was trained on, often as a binary in/out decision rather than classifying unknowns. &lt;em&gt;ex: consider an entire dataset as in-distribution (ID), and then collect several datasets that are disconnected from any ID categories as OOD datasets. Near-OOD datasets only have semantic shift compared with ID datasets, while far-OOD further contains obvious covariate (domain) shift.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;AD:&lt;/strong&gt; is usually unsupervised or semi-supervised and aims to identify rare or abnormal data points that deviate significantly from the norm, regardless of their class or distribution. Note that AD does not have multiple classes in the training set. &lt;em&gt;ex: While the training set only contains defect-free images, the test set contains both normal images and anomalous images with various types of defects.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OSR and OOD detection are interchangeable in some literature due to their identical motivation to identify samples with semantic shift compared to training distribution. The gap between OSR and OOD detection is just getting smaller.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;OpenOOD&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
implements 36 methods (4 from AD, 3 from OSR, 22 from OOD detection, 6 from model uncertainty plus data augmentation) under the recently proposed generalized OOD detection framework.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
provides 9 benchmarks (1 from AD, 4 from OSR, 4 from OOD detection) to comprehensively evaluate OOD detection methods.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Both preprocessing methods and the orthogonal post-hoc methods show strong potential.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Different methods:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;classification-based&lt;/strong&gt; (loss function, classifier architecture, post-hoc processing techniques)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;density-based&lt;/strong&gt; (model the in-distribution with probabilistic models) =&amp;gt; good perf, easy theory.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;distance-based method&lt;/strong&gt; (compute distance in the high-dimension space such as feature space and gradient space to distinguish ID and OOD).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;reconstruction-based methods&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;XC5BA823&quot; width=&quot;808&quot; height=&quot;251&quot;&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Insights&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;nbsp;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
1) Simple preprocessing methods can achieve the best score among the benchmark
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
2) Extra data seems not necessary or requires further exploration
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
3) Post-hoc methods make significant progress and are generally no worse than methods that require training.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Metrics:&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;nbsp;we consider ID samples as positive&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
1) &lt;strong&gt;FPR@95&lt;/strong&gt; measures the false positive rate (FPR) when the true positive rate (TPR) is equal to 95%. Lower scores indicate better performance.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
2) &lt;strong&gt;AUROC&lt;/strong&gt; measures the area under the ROC curve. The area under the ROC curve can be interpreted as the probability that a positive ID example will have a higher detection score than a negative OOD example.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
3) &lt;strong&gt;AUPR&lt;/strong&gt; measures the area under the Precision-Recall (PR) curve. The PR curve is created by plotting precision versus recall.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Main results:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Data Augmentation is the Most Effective Method Type&lt;/strong&gt;: Techniques such as PixMix or Mixup expose the model to interpolated, mixed or modified data, which do not exactly resemble the known classes. This pushes the model to learn to give a more faithful uncertainty when an image doesn't fit well with what it knows.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Result: when a real OOD image arrives, the model is better prepared to say “hmm, that doesn't fit in any box”, instead of forcing a misclassification. Data augmentation methods can help achieve great results but the computational cost is a bit higher&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Extra Data Seems Not Necessary&lt;/strong&gt;: , &quot;extra data&quot; refers to external datasets that are not part of the original training set, and that are used to help the model learn how to better distinguish between in-distribution and out-of-distribution (OOD) inputs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Post-Hoc Methods Outperform Training in General&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;nbsp;For OOD and OSR methods without extra data
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Post-Hoc Methods are Making Progress&lt;/strong&gt;: In general, recent post-hoc methods have better performance compared to previous methods, suggesting the direction of inference-only methods is promising and enjoy progress.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;OSR Benchmarks Aligns with OOD Detection Benchmarks&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Best pots-hoc method: KNN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
