        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_19"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_17"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
                <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>ViM: Out-Of-Distribution with Virtual-logit Matching</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Most of the existing Out-Of-Distribution (OOD) detection algorithms depend on single input source: the feature, the logit, or the softmax probability. However, the immense diversity of the OOD examples makes such methods fragile. There are OOD samples that are easy to identify in the feature space while hard to distinguish in the logit space and vice versa. Motivated by this observation, we propose a novel OOD scoring method named Virtual-logit Matching (ViM), which combines the class-agnostic score from feature space and the In-Distribution (ID) class-dependent logits. Speciﬁcally, an additional logit representing the virtual OOD class is generated from the residual of the feature against the principal space, and then matched with the original logits by a constant scaling. The probability of this virtual logit after softmax is the indicator of OOD-ness. To facilitate the evaluation of large-scale OOD detection in academia, we create a new OOD dataset for ImageNet1K, which is human-annotated and is 8.8× the size of existing datasets. We conducted extensive experiments, including CNNs and vision transformers, to demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed ViM score. In particular, using the BiT-S model, our method gets an average AUROC 90.91% on four difﬁcult OOD benchmarks, which is 4% ahead of the best baseline. Code and dataset are available at https://github.com/haoqiwang/vim.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2022-03-21</dc:date>
        <z:language>en</z:language>
        <z:shortTitle>ViM</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2203.10807</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-15 10:00:56</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2203.10807 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2203.10807</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2203.10807</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_19">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: CVPR 2022&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Most of the existing OOD detection algorithms depend on single input source: the &lt;strong&gt;feature&lt;/strong&gt;, the &lt;strong&gt;logit&lt;/strong&gt;, or the &lt;strong&gt;softmax probability&lt;/strong&gt;. However, the immense diversity of the OOD examples makes such methods fragile. There are OOD samples that are easy to identify in the feature space while hard to distinguish in the logit space and vice versa. =&amp;gt; novel OOD scoring method named ViM, which combines the class-agnostic score from feature space and the In-Distribution (ID) class-dependent logits. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;An additional logit representing the virtual OOD class is generated from the residual of the feature against the principal space, and then matched with the original logits by a constant scaling. The probability of this virtual logit after softmax is the indicator of OOD-ness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To be specific, for feature x, (1) extract the residual x^P⊥ of x against the principal subspace P; (2) convert the norm ||x^P⊥|| to a virtual logit by rescaling; and (3) output the softmax probability of the virtual logit as the ViM score.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;FHNKIRTH&quot; width=&quot;483&quot; height=&quot;476&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;ViM is a &lt;strong&gt;scoring method&lt;/strong&gt; for OOD (out-of-distribution) detection. Its goal is to assign a score to each input indicating whether it’s likely &lt;strong&gt;in-distribution (ID)&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;out-of-distribution (OOD)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br&gt;It relies on two elements:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
the &lt;strong&gt;logits&lt;/strong&gt; (the raw outputs of the model before softmax),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
and the &lt;strong&gt;features&lt;/strong&gt; extracted by the network.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&amp;gt; &lt;strong&gt;Steps of the ViM Score&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Extracting the residual against a principal subspace&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The method considers the feature space learned by the model.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It performs &lt;strong&gt;PCA (Principal Component Analysis)&lt;/strong&gt; on the training features to identify a &lt;strong&gt;principal subspace&lt;/strong&gt; where ID samples typically lie. /.\ ViM doen’t do a exactly a PCA but something mathematically equivalent (via an SVD on the features)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For a new sample, it computes the &lt;strong&gt;residual&lt;/strong&gt;, which is the part of the feature that cannot be projected into this subspace.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
=&amp;gt; The larger the residual, the more &quot;different&quot; the sample is from the training data → possible OOD signal.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Creating a virtual logit for an OOD class&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This &lt;strong&gt;residual&lt;/strong&gt; is transformed into a kind of &lt;strong&gt;virtual logit&lt;/strong&gt;, calibrated so that its average matches the average of the real logits.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This creates a &lt;strong&gt;virtual OOD class&lt;/strong&gt;, whose logit is based on this residual.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Computing the ViM score&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This virtual OOD logit is added to the standard logits.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A &lt;strong&gt;softmax&lt;/strong&gt; is applied over all logits (real classes + virtual OOD class).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
=&amp;gt; The &lt;strong&gt;ViM score&lt;/strong&gt; is simply the &lt;strong&gt;softmax probability assigned to the virtual OOD class&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
