        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_31"/>
        <dc:title>Out-of-Distribution Generalization in Natural Language Processing: Past, Present, and Future</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Machine learning (ML) systems in natural language processing (NLP) face significant challenges in generalizing to out-of-distribution (OOD) data, where the test distribution differs from the training data distribution. This poses important questions about the robustness of NLP models and their high accuracy, which may be artificially inflated due to their underlying sensitivity to systematic biases. Despite these challenges, there is a lack of comprehensive surveys on the generalization challenge from an OOD perspective in natural language understanding. Therefore, this paper aims to fill this gap by presenting the first comprehensive review of recent progress, methods, and evaluations on this topic. We further discuss the challenges involved and potential future research directions. By providing convenient access to existing work, we hope this survey will encourage future research in this area.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-12</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>Out-of-Distribution Generalization in Natural Language Processing</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2023.emnlp-main.276/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-15 14:59:00</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>4533–4559</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
           <bib:Conference><dc:title>EMNLP 2023</dc:title></bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_120">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: mainly focuses on OOD generalization which is different than OOD detection. However, they list different&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; data augmentation techniqu&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;es&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; that are used in OOD generalization and that could also be efficient to boost OOD detection as shown in previous paper (data aug improves OOD detection). Morever, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;A full survey of current available OOD datasets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; can be found in Appendix A.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper aims to provide a comprehensive overview of the current state of research in &lt;strong&gt;OOD generalization&lt;/strong&gt; for natural language understanding, highlighting key methodologies, advancements, and unique challenges.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Most related surveys in NLP focus on measuring and improving model robustness against adversarial attacks or providing causal explanations. &amp;nbsp;Among them &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Measure and improve robustness in NLP models: A survey 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; is the most relevant review to this paper, but their work does not differentiate between data-level variance and shortcut features and also not discuss LLMs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Out-of-Distribution (OOD) Detection vs. OOD Generalization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;OOD Detection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;focuses on identifying data points that deviate from the training distribution, ensuring models abstain from making unreliable predictions. It operates under the assumption that models should flag inputs with&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;semantic shifts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., unseen categories) or&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;covariate shifts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., domain differences) that fall outside their training scope&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;.&amp;nbsp;For instance, autonomous driving systems use OOD detection to reject unfamiliar scenarios that could lead to unsafe decisions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;OOD Generalization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;aims to improve model performance on unseen distributions&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;without explicit detection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;, enabling robust predictions across domains. This involves training strategies like&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;data augmentation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;or&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;domain-invariant representations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;to handle covariate shifts (e.g., lighting variations in images) while maintaining accuracy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;.&amp;nbsp;For example, pre-trained models leverage diverse datasets to generalize better across domains, though they remain vulnerable to extreme distributional shift&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We focus on 2 perspectives of OOD generalization:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
the data distribution, which is model-independent
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
the feature distribution, which is model-oriented
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;Y6E5BG97&quot; width=&quot;840&quot; height=&quot;301&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;WQQXJNYT&quot; width=&quot;430&quot; height=&quot;231&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;5.1 Data Augmentation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Semi-fact Data Augmentation&lt;/strong&gt;: involves substituting part of the content or introducing perturbations to the original data, primarily focusing on enhancing the diversity without altering the semantic meaning or label&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Synonym substitution,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
 manipulating tokens within sentences, 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
adversarial perturbations. 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(A rationale-centric framework for human-in-the-loop machine learning 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; incorporated semi-fact data augmentation for improving the OOD robustness of PLMs in sentiment analysis.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Counterfactual data augmentation (CDA):&lt;/strong&gt; is widely adopted to mitigate bias in neural NLP tasks by operating on biased text
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;6 Large Language Models&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;OOD Definition: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Redefining out-of-distribution (OOD) detection for large language models (LLMs) is critical due to their&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;non-public pre-trained corpora&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;, which complicates identifying OOD data. Current approaches focus on two strategies:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Synthetic Data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;: Artificially generated examples designed to challenge LLMs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Temporal Distribution Shifts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;: Using real-world data collected after 2021 (e.g., post-ChatGPT's training cutoff) as OOD test sets.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;A third OOD scenario involves&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;open-set label shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;, where test data includes&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;unseen classes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;absent in training. While LLMs like ChatGPT show promise in handling novel classes via&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;in-context learning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;, they still struggle to fully resolve these challenges, requiring further innovation for robust OOD generalization.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;OOD Detection: &lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:relation rdf:resource="http://arxiv.org/abs/2105.03075"/>
    </bib:Memo>
    <z:Attachment rdf:about="#item_31">
