        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Out-of-Distribution Detection with Deep Nearest Neighbors</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection is a critical task for deploying machine learning models in the open world. Distance-based methods have demonstrated promise, where testing samples are detected as OOD if they are relatively far away from in-distribution (ID) data. However, prior methods impose a strong distributional assumption of the underlying feature space, which may not always hold. In this paper, we explore the efficacy of non-parametric nearest-neighbor distance for OOD detection, which has been largely overlooked in the literature. Unlike prior works, our method does not impose any distributional assumption, hence providing stronger flexibility and generality. We demonstrate the effectiveness of nearest-neighbor-based OOD detection on several benchmarks and establish superior performance. Under the same model trained on ImageNet-1k, our method substantially reduces the false positive rate (FPR@TPR95) by 24.77% compared to a strong baseline SSD+, which uses a parametric approach Mahalanobis distance in detection. Code is available: https://github.com/deeplearning-wisc/knn-ood.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2022-12-08</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2204.06507</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-15 15:03:32</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2204.06507 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2204.06507</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2204.06507</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_33">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: 15 pages, 4 figures, accepted in ICML 2022&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;CODE available&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
distance-based OOD methods demonstrated promise. Distance-based methods leverage feature embeddings extracted from a model, and operate under the assumption that the test OOD samples are relatively far away from the ID data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
However, prior OOD detection methods often rely on &lt;strong&gt;parametric assumptions&lt;/strong&gt; (like features being Gaussian-distributed), which &lt;strong&gt;don’t hold in practice&lt;/strong&gt;, especially in complex real-world data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Main gap in the literature:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nearest neighbor (KNN) methods&lt;/strong&gt; for OOD detection are simple and flexible but &lt;strong&gt;largely underexplored&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;(and simply using the nearest neighbor distance derived from the feature embedding of a standard classification model is not performant).&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Prior work focuses on &lt;strong&gt;Mahalanobis-based distances&lt;/strong&gt; which assume Gaussian distributions in the embedding space — often invalid in deep feature representations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors propose a &lt;strong&gt;non-parametric k-Nearest Neighbors (KNN)&lt;/strong&gt; method for OOD detection, using the &lt;strong&gt;distance between a test input and its nearest neighbors in the ID training set&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Extract &lt;strong&gt;normalized feature embeddings&lt;/strong&gt; from the &lt;strong&gt;penultimate layer&lt;/strong&gt; of a trained model (e.g., ResNet, ViT).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Compute the k-th nearest neighbor (KNN) distance between the embedding of test input and the embeddings of the training set
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
If the distance exceeds a threshold (learned from ID data), classify the input as &lt;strong&gt;OOD&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Advantages of this method&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(1) &lt;strong&gt;No distributional assumption&lt;/strong&gt; on features.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(2) OOD-agnostic (i.e., the distance threshold is estimated on the ID data only,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(3) easy-to-use (i.e., no need to calculate the inverse of the covariance matrix which can&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;be numerically unstable),&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(4) model-agnostic (i.e., the testing procedure is applicable to different model architectures and training losses) =&amp;gt; Works with any backbone (CNNs, ViTs).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Can be used &lt;strong&gt;with or without contrastive learning&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Variants:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;KNN&lt;/strong&gt;: trained with standard cross-entropy.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;KNN+&lt;/strong&gt;: trained with supervised contrastive learning (SupCon) to improve feature quality.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Backbones:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ResNet-18&lt;/strong&gt; (used for CIFAR-10 and CIFAR-100 experiments)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ResNet-50&lt;/strong&gt; (used for large-scale ImageNet-1k)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ViT-B/16&lt;/strong&gt; (Vision Transformer, for additional evaluation)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;All these models are used with both:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Standard CE loss&lt;/strong&gt; (for simple KNN),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SupCon loss&lt;/strong&gt; (for KNN+).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Experiments:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Datasets&lt;/strong&gt;: CIFAR-10, ImageNet, and several challenging OOD datasets (e.g., LSUN, Textures, Places, iNaturalist).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Metrics&lt;/strong&gt;: FPR@95, AUROC, ID accuracy, inference time.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;R2368DIX&quot; width=&quot;825&quot; height=&quot;277&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Performance highlights:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;KNN+ outperforms parametric baselines&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., Mahalanobis, Energy, SSD+) in OOD detection.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;KNN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; (simple) outperforms a little bit &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Mahalanobis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; on average but not in every cases.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;KNN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; can be further boosted by &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;ReACT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; method&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
On CIFAR-10, &lt;strong&gt;KNN+ achieves FPR95 as low as 2.42%&lt;/strong&gt;, and reduces false positives by up to &lt;strong&gt;24.77%&lt;/strong&gt; compared to SSD+ on ImageNet.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Robust across training losses&lt;/strong&gt; and scales well to large datasets with fast inference (via Faiss library).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A compact and normalized feature space is the key to the success of the nearest neighbor approach for OOD detection.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
KNN outperforms the parametric approach, and scales well to the large-scale dataset (milisec with modern implementations of approximate nearest neighbor search).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Using the &lt;strong&gt;penultimate layer’s&lt;/strong&gt; feature is &lt;strong&gt;better&lt;/strong&gt; than using the &lt;strong&gt;projection head&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Feature normalization is critical
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Using k-th and averaged k nearest nerighbors’ distance has similar performance: using k-th NN distance has a stronger theoretical interpretation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Contrastive learning adds training cost&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., 500+ epochs for SupCon).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nearest neighbor search scales with training set size&lt;/strong&gt; — mitigated with approximate search (e.g., Faiss).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Needs careful choice of &lt;strong&gt;k&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;threshold λ&lt;/strong&gt;, though the paper provides validation strategies.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Simple KNN matches or outperforms Mahalanobis and other baselines&lt;/strong&gt;, even &lt;em&gt;without&lt;/em&gt; contrastive learning.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
