        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Types of Out-of-Distribution Texts and How to Detect Them</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Despite agreement on the importance of detecting out-of-distribution (OOD) examples, there is little consensus on the formal definition of OOD examples and how to best detect them. We categorize these examples by whether they exhibit a background shift or a semantic shift, and find that the two major approaches to OOD detection, model calibration and density estimation (language modeling for text), have distinct behavior on these types of OOD data. Across 14 pairs of in-distribution and OOD English natural language understanding datasets, we find that density estimation methods consistently beat calibration methods in background shift settings, while performing worse in semantic shift settings. In addition, we find that both methods generally fail to detect examples from challenge data, highlighting a weak spot for current methods. Since no single method works well across all settings, our results call for an explicit definition of OOD examples when evaluating different detection methods.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2021-12-14</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2109.06827</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-16 09:23:50</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2109.06827 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2109.06827</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2109.06827</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_50">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: EMNLP 2021&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper provides an in-depth analysis of OOD detection in NLP, categorizing OOD examples into &lt;strong&gt;semantic shift&lt;/strong&gt; (entirely novel classes) vs &lt;strong&gt;background shift&lt;/strong&gt; (different domain or style, same labels). It compares two approaches: (1) &lt;strong&gt;Calibration-based&lt;/strong&gt; methods using classifier confidence (e.g. Maximum Softmax Probability), and (2) &lt;strong&gt;Density-based&lt;/strong&gt; methods using language model probability (perplexity) as an OOD score. Key findings include: density (LM perplexity) methods consistently outperform confidence-based methods when OOD differs only in background/topic, but perform worse on semantic shifts (novel classes)​. Moreover, both approaches struggle on “challenge” sets (adversarial or highly confusing inputs), indicating a need for clearer definitions and benchmarks​. &lt;em&gt;Models:&lt;/em&gt; GPT-2 for perplexity; BERT-based classifiers for confidence scores. &lt;em&gt;Type:&lt;/em&gt; OOD detection (analysis paper). &lt;em&gt;NLP-specific:&lt;/em&gt; Yes. &lt;em&gt;Benchmarks:&lt;/em&gt; 14 pairs of datasets (e.g. SNLI vs. ANLI, SQuAD vs. NewsQA, etc., covering various shift types). &lt;em&gt;Key result:&lt;/em&gt; Highlighted that &lt;strong&gt;no single method excels on all OOD types&lt;/strong&gt; – e.g. language model likelihood is robust to distributional/style shifts​, while calibrated discriminative models work better for unknown labels – underscoring the importance of defining expected OOD scenario.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We categorize examples by whether they exhibit a&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;background&lt;/strong&gt; shift
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;semantic&lt;/strong&gt; shift,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We note that the two types of shifts may happen simultaneously in the real world, and our categorization is based on the most prominent type of shift.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;and find that the 2 major approaches to OOD detection are:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;model calibration&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;density estimation&lt;/strong&gt; (language modeling for text)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;have distinct behavior on these types of OOD data.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In practice, an example is considered OOD due to various reasons, e.g:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
being rare
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
out-of-domain
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
adversarial
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This broad range of distribution shifts makes it unreasonable to expect a detection algorithm to work well for arbitrary OOD examples without assumptions on the test distribution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this paper, we categorize OOD examples by common types of distribution shifts in NLP problems inspired by &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Likelihood ratios for out-of-distribution detection 2019) and (Generalized odin: Detecting out-of-distribution image without learning from out-of-distribution data 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;background shift&lt;/strong&gt; dominates when the domain or the style of the text changes e.g. from news to tweets. &lt;em&gt;An example of background shift is a sentiment classification corpus with reviews from IMDB versus GoodReads where phrases indicating positive reviews (e.g. “best”, “beautifully”) are roughly the same while the background phrases change significantly (e.g. “movie” vs “book”).&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;semantic shift&lt;/strong&gt; dominates when unseen classes occur at test time, as in open-set classification. &lt;em&gt;Semantic shift happens when we encounter unseen classes at test time, e.g. a dialogue system for booking flight tickets receiving a request for meal vouchers or a question-answering system handling unanswerable questions.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;calibration methods&lt;/strong&gt; that use the model’s prediction confidence as the score. &lt;em&gt;Calibration methods rely on p(y | x) estimated by a classifier, thus they are more influenced by the semantic features which are correlated&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;with the label.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MSP
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;density estimation method&lt;/strong&gt;s that fit a distribution of the training inputs&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
the token perplexity (PPL)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
While there are many works on density estimation methods using flow-based models in computer vision, there is limited work experimenting with density estimation methods for OOD detection on text. &lt;em&gt;Density estimation methods are sensitive to all components of the input, including both background and semantic features, even in situations where distribution shifts are predominately driven by one particular type.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Datasets:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1) Toy Datasets&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The authors generate &lt;strong&gt;toy datasets&lt;/strong&gt; using a &lt;strong&gt;Gaussian Mixture Model (GMM)&lt;/strong&gt; for simulating &lt;strong&gt;in-distribution (ID)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;out-of-distribution (OOD)&lt;/strong&gt; samples.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These datasets are &lt;strong&gt;synthetically created&lt;/strong&gt; and are specifically designed to evaluate the &lt;strong&gt;Semantic Shift&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Background Shift&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Semantic Shift&lt;/strong&gt;: Involves shifting the &lt;strong&gt;semantic features&lt;/strong&gt; between ID and OOD data. The generated examples help assess how well a model detects shifts in semantic meaning between two distributions (e.g., news vs. sports).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Background Shift&lt;/strong&gt;: Focuses on changes in &lt;strong&gt;background features&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., shifting the genre while keeping sentiment words the same) and simulates how well models can handle such shifts in data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2) Real Datasets&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The authors also evaluate the OOD detection methods on &lt;strong&gt;real-world datasets&lt;/strong&gt; to better understand the performance of the models in practical scenarios.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;1. &lt;strong&gt;Semantic Shift&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;News Category Dataset (2018)&lt;/strong&gt;: This dataset, which includes data from HuffPost, is used for evaluating semantic shifts. The authors use the &lt;strong&gt;top 40 ID classes&lt;/strong&gt; by frequency as in-distribution (ID) examples and the &lt;strong&gt;remaining 36 classes&lt;/strong&gt; as out-of-distribution (OOD) examples. This shift involves moving classes in increments of 10, resulting in different semantic information between ID and OOD.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DBpedia Ontology Classification Dataset (2015)&lt;/strong&gt;: This dataset includes 14 non-overlapping classes from DBPedia 2014. The authors use &lt;strong&gt;the first 4 classes&lt;/strong&gt; as ID and the &lt;strong&gt;rest&lt;/strong&gt; as OOD, to generate semantic shifts by moving classes to the OOD set.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2. &lt;strong&gt;Background Shift&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SST-2 (Stanford Sentiment Treebank)&lt;/strong&gt;: A binary sentiment classification dataset, used for evaluating background shifts in sentiment classification.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;IMDB Dataset&lt;/strong&gt;: This movie review dataset is used alongside &lt;strong&gt;SST-2&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Yelp Polarity&lt;/strong&gt; to evaluate domain-specific shifts (e.g., from movie reviews to business reviews).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Yelp Polarity Dataset&lt;/strong&gt;: This dataset contains reviews from different businesses and represents a domain shift compared to &lt;strong&gt;SST-2&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;IMDB&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SNLI (Stanford Natural Language Inference)&lt;/strong&gt; A dataset for &lt;strong&gt;Natural Language Inference (NLI)&lt;/strong&gt; tasks, used to evaluate background shifts between different genres of text. SNLI includes sentence pairs labeled as &lt;strong&gt;entailment&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;contradiction&lt;/strong&gt;, or &lt;strong&gt;neutral&lt;/strong&gt; and comes from multiple genres.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MNLI (Multi-Genre Natural Language Inference)&lt;/strong&gt;: Similar to &lt;strong&gt;SNLI&lt;/strong&gt;, but contains more diverse examples sourced from various genres (e.g., fiction, news, and government). This dataset is used to evaluate &lt;strong&gt;background shifts&lt;/strong&gt; in NLI tasks, where the genre shifts between the &lt;strong&gt;training&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;test data&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;RTE (Recognizing Textual Entailment)&lt;/strong&gt;:A dataset for &lt;strong&gt;NLI&lt;/strong&gt; tasks with examples sourced from &lt;strong&gt;different domains&lt;/strong&gt;. The task is to determine whether one sentence entails, contradicts, or is neutral to another. This dataset introduces &lt;strong&gt;domain shifts&lt;/strong&gt;, making it useful for evaluating background shifts in NLI tasks.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
These datasets are used to simulate &lt;strong&gt;semantic shifts&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;background shifts&lt;/strong&gt;. For instance, a shift in &lt;strong&gt;background features&lt;/strong&gt; could involve the &lt;strong&gt;genre&lt;/strong&gt; of text (e.g., movie reviews vs. book reviews), while a &lt;strong&gt;semantic shift&lt;/strong&gt; would involve changing the &lt;strong&gt;category or topic&lt;/strong&gt; of text (e.g., sports articles vs. news articles).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;The datasets mentioned in the paper are primarily used for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;classification and NLI tasks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Sentiment classification&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., SST-2, IMDB, Yelp)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Topic classification&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., News Category Dataset, DBpedia Ontology Classification)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Natural language inference (NLI)&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., SNLI, MNLI)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;Rq&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;: NLI: the goal is to determine the relationship between a premise and a hypothesis. Specifically, NLI involves classifying the relationship between the two into one of three categories: Entailment, Contradiction, Neutral&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3) Challenge Datasets&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Models trained on standard datasets often exploit&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;surface-level features&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., premise-hypothesis overlap in NLI tasks) or&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;spurious correlations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;to achieve strong in-distribution (ID) performance. However, these shortcuts fail when tested on out-of-distribution (OOD) data where such patterns no longer hold.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Challenge datasets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;are specifically crafted to expose these flaws by presenting OOD examples that force models to rely on&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;meaningful task understanding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;rather than superficial heuristics.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The authors classify challenge datasets into three categories, focusing on two types here:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Human-Generated Challenge Data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;c-IMDB&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Crowdsourced counterfactual edits to IMDB reviews, where minimal changes flip sentiment labels. This tests models relying on&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;semantic feature correlations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;by creating a distribution shift between ID (IMDB) and OOD (c-IMDB). =&amp;gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Semantic shift&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Rule-Based Challenge Data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;HANS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Template-based NLI examples with high premise-hypothesis overlap but no entailment, exposing models’ reliance on&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;syntactic heuristics&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;over semantic understanding. =&amp;gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Background shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Stress Test Dataset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Evaluates NLI robustness through sub-categories like:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Semantic Shifts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Antonym substitutions, numerical reasoning tests.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Background Shifts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Negation additions, spelling errors, word overlap, and length mismatches to test resilience to superficial patterns.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Near OOD/ Far OOD and semantic/background shift are they the same ?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Models:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;RoBERTa (Liu et al., 2019)&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Model&lt;/strong&gt;: RoBERTa is fine-tuned on the &lt;strong&gt;in-distribution (ID) data&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Method&lt;/strong&gt;: The &lt;strong&gt;Maximum Softmax Probability (MSP)&lt;/strong&gt; method is used for OOD detection. This involves computing the model's prediction probabilities on ID data and then using those probabilities as an OOD score.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Model Variants&lt;/strong&gt;: The authors experiment with &lt;strong&gt;RoBERTaBase&lt;/strong&gt; (110M parameters) and &lt;strong&gt;RoBERTaLarge&lt;/strong&gt; (355M parameters), evaluating their performance in OOD detection.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;GPT-2 (Radford et al., 2019)&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Model&lt;/strong&gt;: GPT-2 is also fine-tuned on the &lt;strong&gt;ID data&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Method&lt;/strong&gt;: For &lt;strong&gt;density estimation&lt;/strong&gt;, GPT-2 is used in conjunction with &lt;strong&gt;perplexity (PPL)&lt;/strong&gt; as an OOD score. Perplexity measures the uncertainty of the model's prediction, which helps detect OOD data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Model Variants&lt;/strong&gt;: The authors test &lt;strong&gt;GPT-2Small&lt;/strong&gt; (117M parameters) and &lt;strong&gt;GPT-2Medium&lt;/strong&gt; (345M parameters).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;RoBERTa&lt;/strong&gt; is fine-tuned with &lt;strong&gt;MSP&lt;/strong&gt; because it is a &lt;strong&gt;discriminative model&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;MSP&lt;/strong&gt; leverages the &lt;strong&gt;probabilities&lt;/strong&gt; of its classifications to determine OOD samples.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GPT-2&lt;/strong&gt; is fine-tuned with &lt;strong&gt;PPL&lt;/strong&gt; because it is a &lt;strong&gt;generative model&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;perplexity&lt;/strong&gt; is a natural method for evaluating how well it predicts sequences of text, which aligns with its generative nature.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Across 14 pairs of ID and OOD English NLU (natural language understanding) datasets,&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; we find that &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;density estimation methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; consistently &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;beat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; calibration methods in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;background shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; settings. We further see the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;opposite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;semantic shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; pairs, with the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;calibration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; method consistently yielding &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;higher performance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
we find that both methods generally fail to detect examples from challenge data, highlighting a weak spot for current methods.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Since &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;no single method works well across all settings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, our results call for an explicit definition of OOD examples when evaluating different detection methods.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
