        <dc:title>Generalized Out-of-Distribution Detection: A Survey</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection is critical to ensuring the reliability and safety of machine learning systems. For instance, in autonomous driving, we would like the driving system to issue an alert and hand over the control to humans when it detects unusual scenes or objects that it has never seen during training time and cannot make a safe decision. The term, OOD detection, first emerged in 2017 and since then has received increasing attention from the research community, leading to a plethora of methods developed, ranging from classification-based to density-based to distance-based ones. Meanwhile, several other problems, including anomaly detection (AD), novelty detection (ND), open set recognition (OSR), and outlier detection (OD), are closely related to OOD detection in terms of motivation and methodology. Despite common goals, these topics develop in isolation, and their subtle differences in definition and problem setting often confuse readers and practitioners. In this survey, we first present a unified framework called generalized OOD detection, which encompasses the five aforementioned problems, i.e., AD, ND, OSR, OOD detection, and OD. Under our framework, these five problems can be seen as special cases or sub-tasks, and are easier to distinguish. We then review each of these five areas by summarizing their recent technical developments, with a special focus on OOD detection methodologies. We conclude this survey with open challenges and potential research directions.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-01-23</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>Generalized Out-of-Distribution Detection</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2110.11334</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-16 12:40:23</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2110.11334 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2110.11334</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2110.11334</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_54">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Feel free to comment on our Overleaf manuscript: https://www.overleaf.com/9899719915wmccvdtwpkct#c25192&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Encompasses the 5 problems (=sub-topics) : anomaly detection (AD), novelty detection (ND), open set recognition (OSR), outlier detection (OD), OOD detection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
semantic shift: &amp;nbsp;OOD samples are drawn from different classes (= different labels)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
covariate shift: OOD samples from a different domain
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;4ULP5L65&quot; width=&quot;861&quot; height=&quot;272&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;4 groups of methodology:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
1) classification based methods: methods that largely rely on classifiers
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
2) density-based methods: detecting OOD by modeling data density;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
3) distance-based methods: using distance metrics (usually in the feature space) to identify OODs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
4) reconstruction-based methods: methods featured by reconstruction techniques.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;OOD Detection: Methodology&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;3UZ6AVGX&quot; width=&quot;873&quot; height=&quot;339&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;3 OOD Detection: Methodology&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3.1 Classification-based Methods&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;3.1.1 Output-based Methods&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;a. Post-hoc Detection&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Temperature scaling and input perturbation: ODIN &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Enhancing the reliability of out-of-distribution image detection in neural networks 2017)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Energy methods : Energy&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Energy-based out-of-distribution detection 2020) Mood (Mood: Multi-level out-of-distribution detection 2021), JointEnergy (Can multi-label classification networks know what they don't know? 2021) , SHE (Out-of-distribution detection based on in-distribution data patterns memorization with modern hopfield energy 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mahalanobis distance &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(A simple unified framework for detecting out-of-distribution samples and adversarial attacks 2018)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Gram Matrix&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Detecting out-of-distribution examples with gram matrices 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ReACT, NMD &amp;nbsp;one fundamental cause of the overconfidence issue on OOD data has been revealed that&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;using mismatched BatchNorm statistics—that are estimated on ID data yet blindly applied to the OOD data in testing—can trigger abnormally high unit activations. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(React: Out-of-distribution detection with rectified activations 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
DICE&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Dice: Leveraging sparsification for out-of-distribution detection 2022), ASH (Extremely simple activation shaping for out-of-distribution detection 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;b. Training-based Method (requires model re-training)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
designing a confidence-estimating branch &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Learning confidence for out-of-distribution detection in neural networks 2018)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
designing a confidence-estimating class&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Energy-based open-world uncertainty modeling for confidence calibration 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ensembling with leaving-out strategy &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Out-of-distribution detection using an ensemble of self supervised leave-out classifiers 2018)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
adversarial training&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Certifiably adversarially robust detection of out-of-distribution data 2020, Robust out-of-distribution detection for neural networks 2020, &amp;nbsp;Why relu networks yield high-confidence predictions far away from the training data and how to mitigate the problem 2019, Novelty detection via blurring 2019, Atom: Robustifying out-of-distribution detection using outlier mining 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
stronger data augmentation&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(On mixup training: Improved calibration and predictive uncertainty for deep neural networks 2019, Cutmix: Regularization strategy to train strong classifiers with localizable features 2019, Improved regularization of convolutional neural networks with cutout 2017,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
better uncertainty modeling&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;, Augmix: A simple data processing method to improve robustness and uncertainty 2019, Pixmix: Dreamlike pictures comprehensively improve safety measures 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
pretext trainig&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Csi: Novelty detection via contrastive learning on distributionally shifted instances 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
input level manipulation&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Enhancing the reliability of out-of-distribution image detection in neural networks 2017, Watermarking for out-of-distribution detection 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
utilizing feature or statistics from the intermediate-layer features &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Mood: Multi-level out-of-distribution detection 2021, Neural mean discrepancy for efficient out-of-distribution detection 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
G-ODIN (ODIN extension) Especially, to enhance the sensitivity to covariate shift, some methods &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;focus on the hidden representations in the middle layers of neural networks&lt;/span&gt; &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Generalized odin: Detecting out-of-distribution image without learning from out-of-distribution data 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;overconfidence issue can be mitigated through Logit Normalization (LogitNorm)&lt;/span&gt;, a simple fix to the common crossentropy loss by enforcing a constant vector norm on the logits in training (&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Mitigating neural network overconfidence with logit normalization 2022&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;several works attempt to use information in the label space for OOD detection:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;arrange the large semantic space into a hierarchical taxonomy of known classes. Under the&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;redesigned label architecture, top-down classification strategy &amp;nbsp;and group softmax training are&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;demonstrated effective&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Mos: Towards scaling out-of-distribution detection for large semantic space 2021, Fine-grain inference on out-of-distribution data with hierarchical classification 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
take the image features from language-image pre-training models &amp;nbsp;to better detect novel classes, where the image encoding space also contains rich information from the language space
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3.1.2 Methods with Outlier Exposure&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;a. Real Outliers&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Outlier exposure utilizes a set of collected outlier (OOD) samples during training to help models learn to distinguish between ID and OOD data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;OE (Outlier Exposure)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Outlier exposure for OOD detection 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;MCD (Maximum Confidence Discrepancy 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;MixOE (Mixing Outliers for OOD detection 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;The outlier exposure approaches impose a strong assumption on the availability of OOD training data, which can be infeasible in practice.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(241, 152, 55, 0.5)&quot;&gt;TO COMPLETE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;b. Data Generation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Generative models can be used to synthesize outlier (OOD) data samples to augment training data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;DOE (Data Outlier Exposure 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;ATOL (Adversarial Outlier Learning 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;VOS (Virtual Outlier Synthesis 2020) (NPOS Non-parametric outlier synthesis 2023), &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3.1.3 Gradient-based Methods&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
These methods utilize gradients to detect OOD samples. The primary idea is that the gradients behave differently for ID and OOD data, and this discrepancy can be exploited for OOD detection.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ODIN&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Enhancing the reliability of out-of-distribution image detection in neural networks 2017)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; Gradient-based input perturbation is used to increase the softmax score for ID data, thus amplifying the difference between ID and OOD data.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Maybe since it is easier to perturb text than image ODIN would work for text?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GradNorm&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Gradient Norm-based OOD Detection 2020&lt;/span&gt;)&lt;/em&gt;: This method calculates the gradient norm and uses it as a scoring function to distinguish ID from OOD data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Recent research &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(How useful are gradients for ood detection really? 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; demonstrates that while gradient-based methods are effective, their success does not necessarily depend on gradients, but rather on the magnitude of learned feature embeddings and predicted output distribution.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3.1.4 Bayesian Models&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bayesian Neural Networks&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;nbsp;These models estimate the uncertainty of predictions by incorporating priors and posterior distributions over the network parameters. They are used to detect OOD data by identifying high uncertainty in the model's predictions. &lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;However, the obvious shortcomings of inaccurate predictions and high computational costs of of Bayesian NN &amp;nbsp;prevent them from wide adoption in practice. Recent works attempt several less principled approximations&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MC-Dropout&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Monte Carlo Dropout for OOD Detection 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;A simpler approach that uses dropout at test time to approximate posterior distributions. This technique can help in identifying OOD samples by observing how much uncertainty is associated with each prediction.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Deep Ensembles&lt;/strong&gt;: An ensemble of models is used, and the variance in their predictions is used as a measure of uncertainty. Higher variance suggests an OOD sample. &lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;These methods are less competitive for OOD uncertainty estimation.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3.1.5 OOD Detection for Foundation Models&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The success of Foundation models is largely attributed to extensive pre-training on large-scale datasets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
well-pretrained models can significantly enhance OOD detection &lt;em&gt;(Exploring the limits of out-of-distribution detection 2021, Using pre-training can improve model robustness and uncertainty &amp;nbsp;2019, Pretrained transformers improve out-of-distribution robustness 2020)&lt;/em&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;However, adapting (tuning) these models for downstream tasks with specific semantic (label) space in the&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;training data remains a challenge, as simple approaches such as linear probing, prompt tuning &amp;nbsp;and adaptor-style fine-tuning methods do not have good results on OOD detection&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
examines how fine-tuned vision-language models are performed &lt;em&gt;(How does fine-tuning impact out-of-distribution detection for vision-language models? 2024)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;provides a systematic study on pretraining strategies on OOD detection&lt;/span&gt; &lt;em&gt;(Can pre-trained networks detect familiar out-of-distribution data? 2023)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;text-image (CLIP) methods:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;(Locoop: Few-shot out-of-distribution detection via prompt learning 2023) (LSA: Likelihood-aware semantic alignment for full-spectrum out-of-distribution detection 2023)&lt;/em&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;The strong zero-shot learning capabilities of models like CLIP also open avenues for zero-shot OOD detection&lt;/span&gt; &lt;em&gt;(ZOC: Zero-shot out-of-distribution detection based on the pre-trained model clip 2022) &lt;/em&gt;but ZOC computationally expensive,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MCM &lt;em&gt;(Delving into out-of-distribution detection with vision-language representations 2022)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
CLIPN &amp;nbsp;&lt;em&gt;(Clipn for zero-shot ood detection: Teaching clip to say no 2023)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3.2 Density-based Methods&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Explicitly model the in-distribution with some probabilistic models, and flag test data in low-density regions as OOD.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Flow-based methods can also be used for probabilistic modeling &lt;em&gt;(Deep residual flow for out of distribution detection 2020) (Revisiting flow generative models for out-of-distribution detection 2021)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
some works find that probabilistic models sometimes assign a higher likelihood for the OOD sample. &lt;em&gt;(Why &lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;normalizing flows fail to detect out-of-distribution&lt;/span&gt; data 2020)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SEM &lt;em&gt;(Full-spectrum out-of-distribution detection 2023)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Overall, generative models can be prohibitively challenging to train and optimize, and the performance can often lag behind the classification based approaches&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3.3 Distance-based Methods&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Idea: the testing OOD samples should be relatively far away from the centroids or prototypes of in-distribution classes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
mahalanobis distance &lt;em&gt;(A simple fix to mahalanobis distance for improving near-ood detection 2021)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Unlike Mahalanobis, the non-parametric approach does not impose any distributional assumption about the underlying feature space, hence providing stronger simplicity, flexibility, and generality. &lt;em&gt;(Out-of-distribution detection with deep nearest neighbors (2022)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For distance functions, some works use cosine similarity between test sample features and class features to determine OOD samples &lt;em&gt;(Hyperparameter-free out-of-distribution detection using cosine similarity 2020) (A boundary based out-of-distribution classifier for generalized zero-shot learning 2020)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;other distances between the input’s embedding and the class centroids:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
leverage distances with radial basis function kernel (Uncertainty estimation using a single deep deterministic neural network 2020)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Euclidian distance &lt;em&gt;(Feature space singularity for out-of-distribution detection 2020)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
geodesic distance &lt;em&gt;(Igeood: An information geometry approach to out-of-distribution detection 2022)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
the feature norm in the orthogonal complement space of the principal space is shown effective on OOD detection &lt;em&gt;(Vim: Out-of-distribution with virtual-logit matching 2022)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CIDER &lt;/strong&gt;explores the usability of the embeddings in the hyperspherical space, where inter-class dispersion and inner-class compactness can be encouraged.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3.4 Reconstruction-based Methods&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;core idea: the encoder-decoder framework trained on the ID data usually yields different outcomes for ID and OOD samples.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Reconstruction based models with pixel-level comparison seem not a popular solution in OOD detection
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reconstructing with hidden features is shown as a promising alternative &lt;em&gt;(Rethinking reconstruction autoencoder-based out-of-distribution detection 2022):&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MoodCAT &lt;em&gt;(Out-of-distribution detection with semantic mismatch under masking 2022) &lt;/em&gt;masks a random portion of the input image and identifies OOD samples using the quality of the classification-based reconstruction results.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
READ &lt;em&gt;(Read: Aggregating reconstruction error into out-of-distribution detection 2023)&lt;/em&gt; combines inconsistencies from a classifier and an autoencoder by transforming the reconstruction error of raw pixels to the latent space of the classifier.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MOOD &lt;em&gt;(Rethinking out-of-distribution (ood) detection: Masked image modeling is all you need 2023) &lt;/em&gt;shows that masked image modeling for pretraining is beneficial to OOD detection tasks compared to contrastive training and classic classifier training.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3.5 Theoretical Analysis&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Limitations of Deep Generative Models (DGMs) in OOD contexts.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;em&gt;(Understanding failures in out-of-distribution detection with deep generative models 2021) &lt;/em&gt;where DGMs frequently assign greater probabilities to OOD data compared to training data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.87)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255)&quot;&gt;GEM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.87)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255)&quot;&gt; (theoretical and empirical superiority) &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.87)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255)&quot;&gt;(Provable guarantees for understanding out-of-distribution detection 2022) =&amp;gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.87)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255)&quot;&gt;GEM Score: &amp;nbsp;We now introduce a new scoring function, Gaussian mixture based energy measurement. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Performs the same as Mahalanobis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
study establishes necessary and sufficient conditions for the learnability of OOD detection in various scenarios &lt;em&gt;(Is out-of-distribution detection learnable? 2022)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;4 Methodologies from Other Sub-tasks&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4.1 Open Set Recognition : &lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;idem as OOD so we should get inspired by this field&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Image generation techniques have been utilized to synthesize unknown samples from known classes =&amp;gt; doesn’t work
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Classification based methods
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Distance base methods&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Category-based clustering and prototyping are performed based on the visual features extracted from the classifiers.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
OOD samples can be detected by &lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;computing the distance w.r.t. clusters&lt;/span&gt; &lt;em&gt;(P-odn: Prototype-based open deep network for open set recognition 2020)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
leveraged&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; contrastive learning&lt;/span&gt; to learn more compact clusters for known classes &lt;em&gt;(Few-shot open-set recognition using meta-learning 2020), (Learning open set network with discriminative reciprocal points 2020)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
GMVAE &lt;em&gt;(Open-set recognition with gaussian mixture variational autoencoders 2021)&lt;/em&gt; extracts features using a reconstruction VAE, and models the embeddings of the train-Generalized Out-of-Distribution set as a Gaussian mixture with multiple centroids for the following distance-based operations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reconstruction-based Methods&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
use conditional Gaussian distributions by forcing different latent features to approximate class-wise Gaussian models &lt;em&gt;(Conditional gaussian distribution learning for open set recognition 2020)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
generate counterfactual images, which help the model focus more on semantics &lt;em&gt;(Counterfactual zero-shot and open-set visual recognition 2021)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
adversarial defense is also considered to enhance model robustness &lt;em&gt;(Open-set adversarial defense 2020)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;OSR research also shows that a good classifier &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Open-set recognition: A good closed-set classifier is all you need? 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &amp;nbsp;in the close-set is critical to OSR performance, which should also applicable to OOD detection tasks.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4.2 Anomaly Detection &amp;amp; Novelty Detection&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Density-based Methods&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
using gradient-based representation to characterize anomalies &lt;em&gt;(Backpropagated gradient representations for anomaly detection 2020)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Reconstruction-based Methods&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Anomaly detection using self-organizing maps-based k-nearest neighbor algorithm (2014)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Distance-based Methods
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Classification-based Methods
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4.3 Outlier Detection : &lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;All these solutions can be applied especially when OOD samples are exposed during the training stage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Density-based Methods: OD methods include Gaussian distribution, Mahalanobis distance, Gaussian mixtures and Local outlier factor (LOF)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Distance-based Methods: Outliers can be detected by neighbor counting, DBSCAN clustering, and graph-based methods
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Classification-based Methods: Isolation Forest, OC-SVM
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Datasets:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The common practice for building OOD detection benchmarks is to consider an entire dataset as indistribution (ID), and then collect several datasets that are disconnected from any ID categories as OOD datasets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
CIFAR10, CIFAR-100 from OpenOOD (with near OOD and far-OOD)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ResNet18 as the backbone network
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;QQKMIQCX&quot; width=&quot;1164&quot; height=&quot;99&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;V4WC2ZIP&quot; width=&quot;1049&quot; height=&quot;525&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;UCLJFBVR&quot; width=&quot;920&quot; height=&quot;518&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;5.3 Experimental Results and Findings&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Data Augmentation Methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; are the Most Effective: &amp;nbsp;Generally, the most effective methods are those that use model uncertainty works with data augmentation techniques&lt;/span&gt;. Including:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
PixMix (best)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
CutMix
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Ensemble &lt;em&gt;(Ensemble methods in machine learning 2000)&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mixup
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Extra Data Seems Not Necessary?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;Comparing UDG (the best from the extra-data part) with&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;KNN &amp;nbsp;(the best from the extra data-free part)&lt;/span&gt;, we found that UDG’s advantage is only in CIFAR-10 nearOOD, which is not satisfactory since a large quantity of real outlier data is required.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Post-Hoc Methods Outperform Training in General &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(/.\ when they talk about training they refer to methods where the model is fine_tuned on ID data. These methods aim to enhance the model's capability to recognize OOD data during the training phase by integrating OOD detection as part of the model's training proces)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;VOS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Fine-tunes on ID data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; using synthetic OOD samples to enhance OOD detection capabilities.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;LogitNorm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Fine-tunes on ID data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; to adjust logits for better confidence calibration and OOD detection.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;G-ODIN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;: Fine-tunes on ID data with perturbation and temperature scaling to improve OOD detection.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;CSI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;: Fine-tunes on ID data using contrastive learning to enhance the model's ability to distinguish between ID and OOD data.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;ConfBranch&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;: Fine-tunes on ID data with an added confidence-estimating branch to better detect OOD data.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Post-Hoc Methods are Making Progress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; In general, r&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;ecent post-hoc methods have had better performance than previous methods since 2021&lt;/span&gt;, indicating that the direction of inference-only methods is promising and making progress. Recent methods show improvements in performance on more realistic datasets than previous methods, which focused on toy datasets.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Some AD Methods are Good at Far-OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;: such as with DRAEM and CutPaste.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;This study: does not include settings for pure covariate shift =&amp;gt; this was a deliberate choice because most&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;current OOD detectors are more sensitive to covariate shifts than semantic shifts so in detecting semantic shift they also detect covariate shift&lt;/span&gt;. &lt;em&gt;(Full-spectrum out-of-distribution detection 2023)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitation:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Current methods in OOD detection are predominantly evaluated on smaller datasets like CIFAR. However, it has been observed that strategies effective on CIFAR may not perform as well on larger datasets like ImageNet, which has a more extensive semantic space.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;

&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;NOTE&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;The paper &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Exploring the Limits of Out-of-Distribution Detection)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;clearly shows that fine-tuning on in-distribution (ID) data significantly improves OOD detection performance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;. This finding seems at odds with the conclusions in “&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Generalized Out-of-Distribution Detection: A Survey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (2024),” which suggests that training-based methods like &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;VOS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;LogitNorm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;G-ODIN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;, and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;CSI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; who also fine-tune on ID dat (using Resnet18 as backbone) can lead to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;overfitting to ID data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;, ultimately reducing the model's ability to generalize well to unseen OOD data.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
