        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>How Good Are LLMs at Out-of-Distribution Detection?</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection plays a vital role in enhancing the reliability of machine learning (ML) models. The emergence of large language models (LLMs) has catalyzed a paradigm shift within the ML community, showcasing their exceptional capabilities across diverse natural language processing tasks. While existing research has probed OOD detection with relative small-scale Transformers like BERT, RoBERTa and GPT-2, the stark differences in scales, pre-training objectives, and inference paradigms call into question the applicability of these findings to LLMs. This paper embarks on a pioneering empirical investigation of OOD detection in the domain of LLMs, focusing on LLaMA series ranging from 7B to 65B in size. We thoroughly evaluate commonly-used OOD detectors, scrutinizing their performance in both zero-grad and fine-tuning scenarios. Notably, we alter previous discriminative in-distribution fine-tuning into generative fine-tuning, aligning the pre-training objective of LLMs with downstream tasks. Our findings unveil that a simple cosine distance OOD detector demonstrates superior efficacy, outperforming other OOD detectors. We provide an intriguing explanation for this phenomenon by highlighting the isotropic nature of the embedding spaces of LLMs, which distinctly contrasts with the anisotropic property observed in smaller BERT family models. The new insight enhances our understanding of how LLMs detect OOD data, thereby enhancing their adaptability and reliability in dynamic environments. We have released the source code at \url{https://github.com/Awenbocc/LLM-OOD} for other researchers to reproduce our results.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-04-16</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2308.10261</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-16 13:11:23</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2308.10261 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2308.10261</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2308.10261</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_60">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Accepted at COLING 2024&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: excellent papier. Très instructif. Très bien documenté et tests réels et bien menés.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;CODE provided&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This paper initiates a pioneering empirical investigation into the OOD detection capabilities of&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;open source&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;decoder based&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;LLMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; focusing on the &lt;strong&gt;LLaMA&lt;/strong&gt; series ranging from 7B to 65B in size.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We thoroughly evaluate &lt;strong&gt;commonly used OOD detectors&lt;/strong&gt;, examining their performance in both &lt;strong&gt;zero-grad&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt; scenarios.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Notably, &lt;strong&gt;we alter previous discriminative ID fine-tuning into generative fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt;, aligning the pre-training objective of LLMs with downstream tasks.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
the majority of previously prominent PLMs utilized for OOD detection adopt the encoder-based architecture, such as BERT and RoBERTa. These models are predominantly designed with a pre-training objective that focuses on &lt;strong&gt;sentence classification. &lt;/strong&gt;However, LLMs commonly adopt the decoder-based architecture and are trained with the autoregressive objective.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The hidden states of LLMs are specialized for next token prediction, which could influence their performance in OOD detection.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
we elaborate on how to integrate &lt;strong&gt;posthoc&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;OOD detectors&lt;/strong&gt; into &lt;strong&gt;decoder-style&lt;/strong&gt; LLMs, which has not been addressed in existing literature.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Utilizing s&lt;strong&gt;entence representations&lt;/strong&gt; yielded by pretrained language models (PLMs) to &lt;strong&gt;derive OOD confidence scores&lt;/strong&gt; has been the de facto method for textual OOD detection. Specifically, PLMs are &lt;strong&gt;first fine-tuned on the ID data&lt;/strong&gt; and then OOD detectors are applied on the sentence representations generated by PLMs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2 types of OOD instances:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;far-OOD&lt;/strong&gt; where ID and OOD data come from different domains
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;near-OOD&lt;/strong&gt; where ID and OOD data come from the same&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;domain but with different classes (harder to recognize).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;1. Introduction&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;1. Discriminative vs. generative fine-tuning.&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper: &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Is fine-tuning needed? pre-trained language models are near perfect for out-of-domain detection (2023) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;show that OOD detectors progressively lose efficacy as the training of ID tasks continues (trade-off between achieving higher accuracy on ID tasks and ensuring effective OOD detection). &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;generative fine-tuning demonstrates greater resilience&lt;/strong&gt; to the issue of ID overfitting when compared to discriminative finetuning.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2. LLM-based far- vs. near-OOD detection&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Our results consistently demonstrate that LLMs are natural far-OOD detectors: &lt;strong&gt;LLMs of all scales&lt;/strong&gt; achieve near-&lt;strong&gt;perfect&lt;/strong&gt; OOD performance in &lt;strong&gt;far-OOD &lt;/strong&gt;scenarios &lt;strong&gt;without &lt;/strong&gt;requiring any&lt;strong&gt; fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt;. However, when it comes to near-OOD detection, only the 65B model is able to achieve satisfactory performance without any fine-tuning. =&amp;gt; &lt;strong&gt;fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt; significantly &lt;strong&gt;improves&lt;/strong&gt; the &lt;strong&gt;near-OOD&lt;/strong&gt; detection capability of LLMs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3. Anisotropy vs. isotropy.&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Our experimental results suggest that the &lt;strong&gt;cosine distance function&lt;/strong&gt;, when used as a straightforward OOD detector, performs exceptionally well (explained by &lt;strong&gt;isotropic&lt;/strong&gt; representations generated by LLMs).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;2. Related Work&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Pretrained transformers improve out-of-distribution robustness (2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Pre-trained&lt;/strong&gt; Transformers have shown intrinsic superiority in handing OOD detection
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Revisiting mahalanobis distance for transformer-based out-of-domain detection (2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; show that the Gaussian distribution assumption of MD better matches the representation space of BERT and can yield the best OOD performance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Contrastive out-of-distribution detection for pretrained transformers (2021) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;show that a contrastive regularizer can further improve the sentence representation of Transformers for OOD detection.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Is fine-tuning needed? pre-trained language models are near perfect for out of-domain detection (2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;show that RoBERTa &amp;nbsp;without fine-tuning can achieve near-perfect far-OOD detection. It also show that &lt;strong&gt;discriminative&lt;/strong&gt; (classic) &lt;strong&gt;fine-tuning on ID data&lt;/strong&gt; can &lt;strong&gt;decrease&lt;/strong&gt; performance. However this is &lt;strong&gt;not the case&lt;/strong&gt; anymore with &lt;strong&gt;generative&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;fine tuning&lt;/strong&gt; as shown later in this paper (at least it is more difficult to overfit with generative fine tuning).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Probing out-of-distribution robustness of language models with parameter efficient transfer learning (2023) :&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;explore the OOD detection capability of medium-sized PLMs (such as GPT-2), as well as the impact of various ID fine-tuning techniques. &lt;em&gt;But moderate scale models examined, not extensive evaluation of decoder-based models, &amp;nbsp;neglect to undertake generative ID tuning, a crucial step to fine-tune decoder-based models for downstream ID tasks.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;3. Method&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
To better and fairly evaluate the capabilities of LLMs for OOD detection compared to prior smaller models, &lt;strong&gt;we utilize the same sentence classification task as the ID training task&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;For LLMs, we employ the embeddings of the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;last&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; token as the representation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3.1. ID Generative Fine-tuning with LLMs&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
explanation of generative fine-tuning: The aim here is to classify sentences in a known domain (ID) using an LLM. But instead of closed classification (choosing from a fixed set of classes), the LLM directly generates the class name as text. The aim is to learn how to generate the output (the label) correctly. LLMs are naturally designed to generate text, so this format aligns better with how they work.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
use of LoRA and PEFT to do so.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Regarding OOD detection methods, we focus on the prevailing &lt;strong&gt;post-hoc&lt;/strong&gt; approach.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3.2. OOD Detection with LLMs&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;there mainly exist &lt;strong&gt;two&lt;/strong&gt; categories of &lt;strong&gt;post-hoc methods&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;logits-based&lt;/strong&gt; OOD score functions : operate on the final class-related logits.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Maximum softmax probability (MSP)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Energy score (Energy)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;distance-based&lt;/strong&gt;: operate on embeddings.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mahalanobis distance (Maha)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Cosine distance (Cosine)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They explain how to &lt;strong&gt;adapt logit-based and distance based&lt;/strong&gt; method to &lt;strong&gt;generative pre-training &lt;/strong&gt;(here generative classification).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;A289TA4F&quot; width=&quot;821&quot; height=&quot;474&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;4. Experimental Setup&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4.1. Datasets&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Far OOD&lt;/u&gt;: ID and OOD samples come from different distributions (datasets) =&amp;gt; significant semantic differences.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
20 Newsgroups (20NG) (ID)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SST-2 (ID)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
RTE (OOD)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MNLI (OOD)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
TREC10 (OOD)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
IMDB (OOD)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Multi30K (OOD)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
WMT16 (OOD)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
we choose 20NG and SST-2 as two separate ID tasks and the remaining ones are recognized as OOD
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Near-OOD&lt;/u&gt;: ID and OOD samples come from the same domain (dataset) but with disjoint label sets.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
CLINC150
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;5. Findings&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;5.1. Zero-grad OOD Detection with LLMs - NO FINE TUNING&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LLMs are natural far-OOD detectors&lt;/strong&gt; (all LLMs can achieve near-perfect results)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
both &lt;strong&gt;MSP&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Energy&lt;/strong&gt; generate &lt;strong&gt;poor results&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The capability of LLMs for &lt;strong&gt;near-OOD&lt;/strong&gt; detection &lt;strong&gt;improves with their scale.&lt;/strong&gt; When the model has an exceedingly &lt;strong&gt;large number&lt;/strong&gt; of parameters, we can observe a dramatic &lt;strong&gt;performance&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;surge&lt;/strong&gt; to detect OOD inputs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;5.2. OOD Detection with Generatively Fine-tuned LLMs - FINE TUNING&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ID fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt; can &lt;strong&gt;boost OOD&lt;/strong&gt; detection: fine-tuning LLMs on in-distribution tasks can notably augment the models’ capacity to detect OOD instances, surpassing the performance of the zero-grad setting by a significant margin in most cases like in near-OOD setting and with logits-based functions (we go fro 0.95 to 0.99)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ID fine-tuning can improve the separability between ID and OOD instances.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
However, as the training continues, there is a possibility of encountering &lt;strong&gt;overfitting&lt;/strong&gt; =&amp;gt;inferior OOD performance, especially for &lt;strong&gt;logits-based&lt;/strong&gt; methods
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Generative fine-tuning generalizes better compare to disriminative fine-tuning =&amp;gt; &lt;/strong&gt;generative fine-tuning tends to be less overfit on the ID task and all OOD detectors consistently perform better than discriminative fine-tuning, especially for distance-based OOD detectors
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
we compare encoder-based and decoder-based Transformers and observe impressive &lt;strong&gt;improvement&lt;/strong&gt; on &lt;strong&gt;decoder-based&lt;/strong&gt; LLMs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Cosine distance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; is a data-&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;efficient OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; detector and is &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;superior to other methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Why? BERT’s sentence representations are concentrated within a narrow cone, resulting in substantial challenges for tasks involving semantic matching. Nevertheless, we discover that this concern does not apply to LLMs. The representations generated by off-the-shelf LLMs inherently exhibit isotropy, enabling Cosine distance to excel in OOD detection.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Distance-based&lt;/strong&gt; OOD detection methods are &lt;strong&gt;superior&lt;/strong&gt; to &lt;strong&gt;logits-based&lt;/strong&gt; ones.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Our findings unveil that a &lt;strong&gt;simple cosine distance OOD detector&lt;/strong&gt; demonstrates &lt;strong&gt;superior efficacy&lt;/strong&gt;, outperforming other OOD detectors, especially in the few-shot setting.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Cosine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; similarity (distance based) &amp;gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Mahalanobis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &amp;nbsp;distance (distance based) &amp;gt;&amp;gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Energy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; (logit base) &amp;gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;MSP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; (logit base)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We provide an intriguing explanation for this phenomenon by highlighting the &lt;strong&gt;isotropic&lt;/strong&gt; nature of the &lt;strong&gt;embedding spaces of LLMs,&lt;/strong&gt; which distinctly contrasts with the &lt;strong&gt;anisotropic&lt;/strong&gt; property observed in smaller &lt;strong&gt;BERT&lt;/strong&gt; family models.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Similarly, we find that LLMs can also achieve perfect far-OOD detection performance without fine-tuning.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:relation rdf:resource="http://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07242"/>
    </bib:Memo>
