        <dc:relation rdf:resource="http://arxiv.org/abs/2308.10261"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Probing Out-of-Distribution Robustness of Language Models with Parameter-Efficient Transfer Learning</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>As the size of the pre-trained language model (PLM) continues to increase, numerous parameter-efficient transfer learning methods have been proposed recently to compensate for the tremendous cost of fine-tuning. Despite the impressive results achieved by large pre-trained language models (PLMs) and various parameter-efficient transfer learning (PETL) methods on sundry benchmarks, it remains unclear if they can handle inputs that have been distributionally shifted effectively. In this study, we systematically explore how the ability to detect out-of-distribution (OOD) changes as the size of the PLM grows or the transfer methods are altered. Specifically, we evaluated various PETL techniques, including fine-tuning, Adapter, LoRA, and prefix-tuning, on three different intention classification tasks, each utilizing various language models with different scales.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-06-14</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2301.11660</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-17 08:53:44</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2301.11660 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2301.11660</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2301.11660</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_80">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: *SEM 2023&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: pas le papier le plus utile mais en gros plus la taille du decoder only LLM augmente et meilleure est la détection OOD, Le fine-tuning avec LoRa améliore les résultats si le modèle est petit mais pas si le modèle est grand. Trouve des résultats différents de &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;“How Good Are LLMs at Out-of-Distribution Detection?”&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (qui trouve que cosine similarity est meilleur)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;PLM = Pre-trained Language Model&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;PETL = parameter-efficient transfer learning&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Explore how the ability to &lt;strong&gt;detect OOD&lt;/strong&gt; changes as the &lt;strong&gt;size of the PLM grows&lt;/strong&gt; OR the &lt;strong&gt;transfer methods are altered&lt;/strong&gt;. We evaluate &lt;strong&gt;4 PETL techniques&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
full fine-tuning (upper bound)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Adapter
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
LoRA,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
prefix-tuning
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
train only the last single layer perceptron (lower bound)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
on &lt;strong&gt;3 intention classification tasks,&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;each utilizing &lt;strong&gt;various language models&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;strong&gt;different scales &lt;/strong&gt;(explore &lt;strong&gt;large&lt;/strong&gt; PLM or auto-regressive ie &lt;strong&gt;decoder&lt;/strong&gt; only PLM) :&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
GPT2-S, M, L, XL
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
GPT-Neo
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
GPT-J
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2 Datasets:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
CLINC150
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Banking77
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;With 2 scenarios:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
far-OOD setting (CLINC OOD)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
close-OOD setting (Banking)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;4 OOD detection methods:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Logit-based (require additional training of the classification layer.)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MSP
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Energy
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Representation-based&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mahalanobis distance
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Cosine Similarity
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In many works, &lt;strong&gt;hidden representation&lt;/strong&gt;-based OOD evaluation, generally &lt;strong&gt;surpass&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;logit-based&lt;/strong&gt; evaluations (hypothesis: hidden representations have more copious information than the logit value):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Contrastive out-of-distribution detection for pretrained transformers (2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Enhancing the generalization for intent classification and out-of-domain detection in SLU (2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Revisiting mahalanobis distance for transformer-based out-of-domain detection (2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, they showed here the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;opposite&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (which is also opposite from what the showed in this paper: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;How Good Are LLMs at Out-of-Distribution Detection?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;) they state that:&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;in auto-regressive PLMs, logit-based evaluations (i.e., MSP and Energy) outperform representation-based methods (i.e., Mahalanobis distance and cosine similarity), as shown in Table 1.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/em&gt;Then they give they own conjecture on why this is happening.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(1)&lt;/span&gt; As the size of the PLM increases, the performance improves without any update of model parameters. However, it is still challenging to use it without supervision since their performances still lag far behind compared to the finetuned small PLM (i.e., BERT-base).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;876TI7HU&quot; width=&quot;715&quot; height=&quot;314&quot;&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;UFWFWR3V&quot; width=&quot;616&quot; height=&quot;506&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(2)&lt;/span&gt; PETLs outperform full fine-tuning with sufficiently large PLMs &amp;nbsp;(i.e., GPT-J) in both IND and OOD metrics.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The capability to detect far-OOD inputs (far from the training distribution) improves proportionally as the size grows, while the ability to identify close-OOD input improves rather trivially
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Empirically, we confirmed that &lt;strong&gt;LoRA&lt;/strong&gt; is the most &lt;strong&gt;stable&lt;/strong&gt; during learning and that &lt;strong&gt;prefix-tuning&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;fluctuates&lt;/strong&gt; severely according to learning.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(3) &lt;/span&gt;Lastly, leveraging the information of the last hidden representation, which is the most prevailing method for bi-directional PLM in recent OOD detection, does not transfer well in auto-regressive PLM, requiring a novel representation extracting technique.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:relation rdf:resource="http://arxiv.org/abs/2104.08812"/>
        <dc:relation rdf:resource="http://arxiv.org/abs/2210.11034"/>
    </bib:Memo>
