                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Cho</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Hyunsoo</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Park</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Choonghyun</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Kang</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Jaewook</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Yoo</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Kang Min</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Kim</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Taeuk</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Lee</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Sang-goo</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_84"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_86"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_85"/>
        <dc:relation rdf:resource="#item_80"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Enhancing Out-of-Distribution Detection in Natural Language Understanding via Implicit Layer Ensemble</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection aims to discern outliers from the intended data distribution, which is crucial to maintaining high reliability and a good user experience. Most recent studies in OOD detection utilize the information from a single representation that resides in the penultimate layer to determine whether the input is anomalous or not. Although such a method is straightforward, the potential of diverse information in the intermediate layers is overlooked. In this paper, we propose a novel framework based on contrastive learning that encourages intermediate features to learn layer-specialized representations and assembles them implicitly into a single representation to absorb rich information in the pre-trained language model. Extensive experiments in various intent classification and OOD datasets demonstrate that our approach is significantly more effective than other works.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2022-10-20</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2210.11034</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-17 09:11:29</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2210.11034 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2210.11034</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2210.11034</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_84">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: EMNLP Findings 2022&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: difficile à mettre en oeuvre en pratique je pense. A l’air complexe comme ça à première vue. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Most current OOD detection methods rely only on the &lt;strong&gt;last layer&lt;/strong&gt; of a language model, ignoring potentially useful information in &lt;strong&gt;intermediate layers&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previous work shows that middle layers encode rich linguistic and semantic information, but combining them via &lt;strong&gt;naive or explicit ensembles&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., averaging or concatenating outputs) is often suboptimal and computationally expensive.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors propose &lt;strong&gt;LaCL (Layer-agnostic Contrastive Learning)&lt;/strong&gt;, which:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Uses &lt;strong&gt;contrastive learning&lt;/strong&gt; to enrich each intermediate layer with diverse, specialized features.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Applies a &lt;strong&gt;global compression layer (GCL)&lt;/strong&gt; to fuse these intermediate representations into a single, low-dimensional vector — an &lt;strong&gt;implicit ensemble&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Introduces a &lt;strong&gt;correlation regularization (CR) loss&lt;/strong&gt; to &lt;strong&gt;encourage diversity&lt;/strong&gt; between layers.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Uses &lt;strong&gt;cosine similarity&lt;/strong&gt; between test inputs and training embeddings to detect OOD inputs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Unlike traditional methods, &lt;strong&gt;LaCL does not require scoring each layer separately&lt;/strong&gt;, making it &lt;strong&gt;more efficient and accurate&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Across &lt;strong&gt;9 OOD scenarios&lt;/strong&gt; (both close-OOD and far-OOD using datasets like CLINC150, SNIPS, Banking77):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LaCL outperforms all baseline and state-of-the-art OOD methods&lt;/strong&gt; (including SimCSE, MirrorBERT, ConSERT).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It achieves &lt;strong&gt;lower false positive rates (FPR@95)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;higher AUROC&lt;/strong&gt;, especially in &lt;strong&gt;challenging close-OOD settings&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Ablation studies confirm that each component (contrastive loss, GCL, CR) contributes to performance.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The current model architecture depends on the number of layers in the transformer, which means:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hyperparameters must be manually tuned&lt;/strong&gt; for different backbones.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Although promising for general use, &lt;strong&gt;LaCL has only been tested on OOD detection&lt;/strong&gt; — its generalization to other NLP or vision tasks remains unexplored.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Some &lt;strong&gt;misclassifications happen due to keyword over-reliance or non-standard input (e.g., slang, typos)&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
