    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://arxiv.org/abs/2111.12797">
        <z:itemType>preprint</z:itemType>
        <dc:publisher>
           <foaf:Organization><foaf:name>arXiv</foaf:name></foaf:Organization>
        </dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Sun</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Yiyou</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Guo</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Chuan</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Li</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Yixuan</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_98"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_100"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_99"/>
        <dc:relation rdf:resource="http://arxiv.org/abs/2204.06507"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>ReAct: Out-of-distribution Detection With Rectified Activations</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Out-of-distribution (OOD) detection has received much attention lately due to its practical importance in enhancing the safe deployment of neural networks. One of the primary challenges is that models often produce highly confident predictions on OOD data, which undermines the driving principle in OOD detection that the model should only be confident about in-distribution samples. In this work, we propose ReAct--a simple and effective technique for reducing model overconfidence on OOD data. Our method is motivated by novel analysis on internal activations of neural networks, which displays highly distinctive signature patterns for OOD distributions. Our method can generalize effectively to different network architectures and different OOD detection scores. We empirically demonstrate that ReAct achieves competitive detection performance on a comprehensive suite of benchmark datasets, and give theoretical explication for our method's efficacy. On the ImageNet benchmark, ReAct reduces the false positive rate (FPR95) by 25.05% compared to the previous best method.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2021-11-24</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>ReAct</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2111.12797</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-17 13:32:03</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2111.12797 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2111.12797</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2111.12797</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_98">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: NeurIPS 2021&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Why this method? What problem does it address?&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Problem:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Modern neural networks tend to be &lt;strong&gt;overconfident&lt;/strong&gt; on out-of-distribution (OOD) inputs — giving &lt;strong&gt;high-confidence predictions for unknown data&lt;/strong&gt;. This is dangerous in real-world applications like autonomous driving or healthcare.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Gap in the literature:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Most prior OOD methods try to tweak output scores (e.g., softmax, energy) but &lt;strong&gt;ignore internal activations&lt;/strong&gt;, which may contain strong OOD signals. The authors show that &lt;strong&gt;OOD data triggers abnormally high activations&lt;/strong&gt; in certain units — a phenomenon not previously well exploited. &lt;em&gt;What high activations means here? This is the result after a &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;ReLU&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;layer: h=ReLU(Wx+b) &amp;nbsp;The values of &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;h &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;(e.g., in the penultimate layer) are unusually large when the input is OOD.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;What is the proposed method (ReAct)?&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ReAct (Rectified Activations):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;simple, post hoc&lt;/strong&gt; technique applied &lt;strong&gt;after training&lt;/strong&gt;. It works by &lt;strong&gt;truncating overly large activations&lt;/strong&gt; in the &lt;strong&gt;penultimate layer&lt;/strong&gt; of a network:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;ReAct(x;c)=min⁡(x,c)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Where &lt;code&gt;c&lt;/code&gt; is a threshold (e.g., 90th percentile of activation values on in-distribution data). This reduces spiky activations on OOD inputs while preserving most of the ID structure.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Compatible with any scoring function:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;ReAct can be plugged into:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Max softmax (MSP),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ODIN,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Energy score.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It works by &lt;strong&gt;reducing OOD logits more than ID logits&lt;/strong&gt;, making scores more separable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;What are the results?&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Benchmarks:&lt;/strong&gt; CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, ImageNet (ID), tested against multiple OOD datasets (iNaturalist, Textures, Places, SUN, etc.).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Models used:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ResNet-50&lt;/strong&gt; (ImageNet)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ResNet-18&lt;/strong&gt; (CIFAR)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MobileNet-v2&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Also tested with &lt;strong&gt;WeightNorm&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;GroupNorm&lt;/strong&gt; networks
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Training and Implementation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No retraining required&lt;/strong&gt; — ReAct is fully &lt;strong&gt;post hoc&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use the &lt;strong&gt;penultimate layer activations&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Choose threshold &lt;code&gt;c&lt;/code&gt; based on a chosen percentile (e.g., 90%) of ID data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Can be implemented in 1 line: &lt;code&gt;activations = torch.clamp(activations, max=c)&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Requires a &lt;strong&gt;carefully chosen threshold&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;code&gt;c&lt;/code&gt; (e.g., percentile tuning).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Works best on &lt;strong&gt;deep layers&lt;/strong&gt; (penultimate), not early layers.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Theoretical assumptions&lt;/strong&gt; are based on Gaussian/skewed activation distributions — may not generalize to all settings.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Does not address OOD detection for &lt;strong&gt;non-image&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;multi-modal&lt;/strong&gt; models yet.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Seems to work only for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;logit based&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; method and not so well for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;representation based&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; methods: &amp;nbsp;Note that Mahalanobis [29 ] estimates OOD score using feature representations instead of the model output f (x), hence is less compatible with ReAct.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
