        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>OpenOOD v1.5: Enhanced Benchmark for Out-of-Distribution Detection</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Out-of-Distribution (OOD) detection is critical for the reliable operation of open-world intelligent systems. Despite the emergence of an increasing number of OOD detection methods, the evaluation inconsistencies present challenges for tracking the progress in this field. OpenOOD v1 initiated the unification of the OOD detection evaluation but faced limitations in scalability and scope. In response, this paper presents OpenOOD v1.5, a significant improvement from its predecessor that ensures accurate and standardized evaluation of OOD detection methodologies at large scale. Notably, OpenOOD v1.5 extends its evaluation capabilities to large-scale data sets (ImageNet) and foundation models (e.g., CLIP and DINOv2), and expands its scope to investigate full-spectrum OOD detection which considers semantic and covariate distribution shifts at the same time. This work also contributes in-depth analysis and insights derived from comprehensive experimental results, thereby enriching the knowledge pool of OOD detection methodologies. With these enhancements, OpenOOD v1.5 aims to drive advancements and offer a more robust and comprehensive evaluation benchmark for OOD detection research.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-12-16</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>OpenOOD v1.5</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2306.09301</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-18 12:49:35</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2306.09301 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2306.09301</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2306.09301</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_104">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Accepted by DMLR. See code at https://github.com/Jingkang50/OpenOOD/ and leaderboard at https://zjysteven.github.io/OpenOOD/&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: This paper is also interesting because it also explains how thet performed their experiments such that the experiments are comparable between each other. Indeed, some OOD methods require hyperparameter tuning and they explain how they did do that it is the same between different methods.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Computer Vision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OpenOOD v1.5 extends its evaluation capabilities to large-scale data sets (ImageNet) and foundation models (e.g., CLIP and DINOv2), and expands its scope to investigate full-spectrum OOD detection which considers semantic and covariate distribution shifts at the same time.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Focus on OOD detection in the context of multi-class image classification,&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Semantic Shift
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Covariate Shift
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Semantic Shift&lt;/strong&gt;: The &lt;strong&gt;meaning&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;class&lt;/strong&gt; of the data changes, but the features may still be similar to the ID data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Covariate Shift&lt;/strong&gt;: The &lt;strong&gt;distribution of input features&lt;/strong&gt; changes (e.g., lighting, noise, scale), but the label distribution remains the same.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Near-OOD&lt;/strong&gt;: The OOD data is &lt;strong&gt;close to&lt;/strong&gt; the ID data, often caused by subtle &lt;strong&gt;covariate shifts&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., slightly different images but from the same object class).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Far-OOD&lt;/strong&gt;: The OOD data is &lt;strong&gt;far from&lt;/strong&gt; the ID data, with &lt;strong&gt;major differences&lt;/strong&gt; in both feature distribution and potentially class semantics.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(46, 168, 229, 0.5)&quot;&gt;are semantic and covariate shift special cases of far OOD and near OOD?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Benchmarks&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;6IIJ466R&quot; width=&quot;784&quot; height=&quot;265&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They also add &lt;strong&gt;Full-spectrum benchmarks : &lt;/strong&gt;For full-spectrum benchmarks we consider the large-scale settings of ImageNet-200 and ImageNet-1K.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Most of the OOD data sets considered in OOD detection literature fall into our far-OOD category, meaning that the more difficult near-OOD detection was less emphasized than our benchmarks do
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Methods:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We prioritize methods that were published in toptier machine learning conferences or journals and have public implementations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;4 groups:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Post-hoc inference methods&lt;/strong&gt; (= post-processor) are applied to the base classifier to generate the “OOD score”. They only take effect at inference phase and&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; by default assume that the classifier is trained with the standard cross-entropy loss&lt;/span&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Training methods&lt;/strong&gt; involve training-time regularization. Most of them assume no access to auxiliary&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OOD training data &lt;strong&gt;(w/o outlier data&lt;/strong&gt;), while some methods do &lt;strong&gt;(w/ outlier data).&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We also consider several &lt;strong&gt;data augmentation&lt;/strong&gt; methods.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;9U99Q93I&quot; width=&quot;1007&quot; height=&quot;459&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;9ZPXGY56&quot; width=&quot;1001&quot; height=&quot;312&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;A8F2DZVN&quot; width=&quot;682&quot; height=&quot;385&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Mean of each line of Table 3: &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Post-hoc Inference Methods&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;OpenMAx: 83.54&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;MSP: 83.86&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;TempScale: 84.40&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;ODIN: 83.34&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;MDS: 75.27&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;MDSEns: 72.69&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;RMDS: 84.49&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Gram: 72.57&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;EBO: 84.34&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;OpenGAN: 70.42&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;GradNorm: 75.65&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4eb31c&quot;&gt;ReAct: 85.00&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;MLS: 84.52&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;KLM: 81.92&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;VIM: 84.03&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4eb31c&quot;&gt;KNN: 84.75&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;DICE: 82.79&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;RankFeat: 68.27&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;ASH: 83.13&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;SHE: 82.69&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt; Training Methods (w/o Outlier Data)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;ConfBranch: 81.68&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4eb31c&quot;&gt;RotPred: 85.76&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;G-ODIN: 83.49&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;CSI: 78.67&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;ARPL: 81.88&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;MOS: 78.68&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4eb31c&quot;&gt;VOS: 85.78&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4eb31c&quot;&gt;LogitNorm: 85.26&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;CIDER: 83.93&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4eb31c&quot;&gt;NPOS: 86.40&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Training Methods (w/ Outlier Data)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4eb31c&quot;&gt;OE: 87.96&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;MCD: 84.81&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;UDG: 81.11&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;MixOE: 84.92&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;EFR7FAQV&quot; width=&quot;1017&quot; height=&quot;434&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;There is no single winner&lt;/strong&gt; that always outperforms others across multiple data sets&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For example, ReAct &amp;nbsp;and ASH are extremely powerful on ImageNet but less competitive on CIFAR.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In contrast, methods that yield remarkable performance on small data sets (KNN, DICE) do not show clear advantage on large data sets.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Nevertheless, we can see that the methods : (see mean of each line) are approximately good for a lot of datasets. &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;data augmentations&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;help&lt;/strong&gt; with OOD detection in both standard and full-spectrum setting: the performance gain is amplified when they are combined with powerful post-processors. For example, AugMix + ASH or AugMix + MSP are the current best score among all methods. The results indicate that the effects from data augmentations and post-processors are complementary. &lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ff666680&quot;&gt;Data augmentations are helpful especially when combined with approriate post-processors and when performing near-OOD detection. Rk: data augmentation is applied on ID data during the training of the model. &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Near-OOD remains more challenging than far-OOD &lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Vision transformers do not outperform ResNets. &lt;/strong&gt;(ViT do not show noticeable improvements over ResNets for OOD detection)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Training methods excel at small data sets.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Post-hoc methods are more effective for large-scale settings.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Outlier data helps in certain cases: &lt;/strong&gt;mainly when the test OOD samples are similar to the training ones. For instance, OE yields the highest near-OOD AUROC on CIFAR-100 because the used OOD training set is similar to one of the near-OOD test sets. In contrast, it does not seem to be beneficial for detecting far-OOD samples
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;ZMKR46E9&quot; width=&quot;922&quot; height=&quot;536&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;GXPUU2WU&quot; width=&quot;896&quot; height=&quot;430&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;4I5M2HWI&quot; width=&quot;728&quot; height=&quot;309&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Full-Spectrum Detection&lt;/strong&gt; refers to the ability of an &lt;strong&gt;Out-of-Distribution (OOD) detection model&lt;/strong&gt; to effectively identify OOD samples while maintaining accuracy when there are &lt;strong&gt;covariate shifts&lt;/strong&gt; within the &lt;strong&gt;in-distribution (ID) data&lt;/strong&gt;. The performance drop suggests that existing OOD detectors can be sensitive to the non-semantic covariate shift and are likely to flag covariate-shifted ID samples as OOD.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Data augmentations continue to help in full-spectrum settings, especially when combined with powerful post-hoc methods.&lt;/strong&gt; (AugMix + SHE is the current best approach in terms of full-spectrum near-OOD AUROC on ImageNet-1K). In general our results demonstrate that “data augmentation+ post-processor” is promising for both standard and full-spectrum OOD detection.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Foundation model&lt;/strong&gt;s (= large pre-trained models that can be adapted for a wide range of tasks). &amp;nbsp;In particular, zero-shot foundation models generalize significantly better when facing covariate-shifted samples when it comes to OOD generalization. This motivates us to see whether the same advantage exists when foundation models handle semantic-shifted samples, i.e., in the context of OOD detection. &lt;em&gt;Compared to task-specific supervised models, zero-shot CLIP shows significant improvements in far-OOD detection in both standard and full-spectrum settings. However, the comparison for near-OOD detection is more nuanced and inconclusive. The paper suggests that it is too early to draw conclusions about its performance in this area. Additionally, most existing OOD detectors are not compatible with zero-shot CLIP, and the only detector specifically designed for CLIP (Ming et al., 2022a) uses a simple design that is equivalent to the basic MSP detector.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
All the OOD detection methods considered in our work are &lt;strong&gt;discriminative&lt;/strong&gt;. OOD detection approaches that are based on generative modeling are not currently included in OpenOOD because &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;generative methods have not yet demonstrated scalability on ImageNet-level data and in general are less competitive than discriminative methods. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(Deep residual flow for out of distribution detection 2020, Why normalizing flows fail to detect out-of-distribution data 2020, Do deep generative models know what they don't know? 2018, Input complexity and out-of-distribution detection with likelihood-based generative models 2019)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
certain methods may pose &lt;strong&gt;privacy or security risks&lt;/strong&gt;: methods such as &lt;strong&gt;KNN&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;SHE&lt;/strong&gt; rely on the extracted representation of training samples to compute the OOD score, making it vulnerable to privacy attacks &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(Privacy leakage of adversarial training models in federated learning systems 2022, Adversarial attacks on foundational vision models 2023) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;showed that OOD detectors enlarge the attack surface of deep learning systems, and existing methods can easily be compromised by adversarial attacks.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4eb31c&quot;&gt;In the future v2 release we plan to further expand the scope of OpenOOD beyond image classification and include more application scenarios such as object detection, semantic segmentation, and natural language processing tasks.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Related work:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
No true state-of-the-art? ood detection methods are inconsistent across datasets (2021) made the observation that “OOD detection methods are inconsistent across data sets” from experiments on 3 small data sets (CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100, and SVHN) with 3 specific post-hoc methods (MSP, ODIN, and MDS).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:relation rdf:resource="http://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07242"/>
    </bib:Memo>
    <z:Attachment rdf:about="#item_105">
        <z:itemType>attachment</z:itemType>
        <dc:title>Snapshot</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2306.09301</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-18 12:49:36</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <z:linkMode>1</z:linkMode>
        <link:type>text/html</link:type>
    </z:Attachment>
    <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2021/hash/3941c4358616274ac2436eacf67fae05-Abstract.html">
        <z:itemType>conferencePaper</z:itemType>
        <dcterms:isPartOf>
            <bib:Journal>
                <prism:volume>34</prism:volume>
                <dc:title>Advances in Neural Information Processing Systems</dc:title>
            </bib:Journal>
        </dcterms:isPartOf>
        <dc:publisher>
            <foaf:Organization>
               <foaf:name>Curran Associates, Inc.</foaf:name>
            </foaf:Organization>
        </dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Fort</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Stanislav</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Ren</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Jie</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Lakshminarayanan</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Balaji</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_111"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_110"/>
        <dc:title>Exploring the Limits of Out-of-Distribution Detection</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Near out-of-distribution detection (OOD) is a major challenge for deep neural networks. We demonstrate that large-scale pre-trained transformers can significantly improve the state-of-the-art (SOTA) on a range of near OOD tasks across different data modalities. For instance, on CIFAR-100 vs CIFAR-10 OOD detection, we improve the AUROC from 85% (current SOTA) to more than 96% using Vision Transformers pre-trained on ImageNet21k. On a challenging genomics OOD detection benchmark, we improve the AUROC from 66% to 77% using transformer and unsupervised pre-training.  To further improve performance, we explore the few-shot outlier exposure setting where a few examples from outlier classes may be available; we show that  pre-trained transformers are particularly well-suited for outlier exposure, and that the AUROC of OOD detection on CIFAR-100 vs CIFAR-10  can be improved to 98.7% with just 1 image per OOD class, and 99.46% with 10 images per OOD class. For multi-modal image-text pre-trained transformers such as CLIP, we explore a new way of using just the names of outlier classes as a sole source of information without any accompanying images, and show that this outperforms previous SOTA on standard OOD benchmark tasks.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2021</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>Neural Information Processing Systems</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2021/hash/3941c4358616274ac2436eacf67fae05-Abstract.html</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-18 13:42:23</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>7068–7081</bib:pages>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_111">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Why this method? What gap does it address?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper addresses the &lt;strong&gt;challenging problem of detecting near-OOD (Out-of-Distribution) samples&lt;/strong&gt;, which are inputs that are semantically similar to in-distribution (ID) classes — and thus harder to distinguish.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Literature gap:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Prior OOD detection methods perform well on &lt;strong&gt;far-OOD tasks&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., CIFAR-100 vs SVHN) but &lt;strong&gt;struggle with near-OOD&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., CIFAR-100 vs CIFAR-10).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Underexplored potential of pre-trained transformers&lt;/strong&gt;, especially &lt;strong&gt;Vision Transformers (ViTs)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;multi-modal models&lt;/strong&gt; like CLIP, for OOD detection.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What is the proposed approach?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors investigate how &lt;strong&gt;large-scale pretraining&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;transformer-based architectures&lt;/strong&gt; affect OOD detection — especially near-OOD.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They explore &lt;strong&gt;three main strategies&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fine-tuning pre-trained Transformers (ViT, BERT):&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Fine-tune on ID data.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use simple OOD scores: max softmax (MSP), Mahalanobis distance.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Leads to &lt;strong&gt;well-separated embeddings&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;improved OOD scores.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Few-shot outlier exposure:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Add &lt;strong&gt;1–10 labeled OOD examples per class&lt;/strong&gt; during training.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Improves&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;AUROC significantly (e.g., from 96% → 99.5% on CIFAR benchmarks).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Works in both vision and genomics domains.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Zero-shot OOD detection with CLIP:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Uses &lt;strong&gt;class names only&lt;/strong&gt; (no images) as candidate labels.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Leverages CLIP’s &lt;strong&gt;image-text alignment&lt;/strong&gt; to separate in/out-distribution.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Achieves AUROC up to &lt;strong&gt;99.95%&lt;/strong&gt; on CIFAR vs SVHN.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;What are the results?&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Vision (CIFAR-100 vs CIFAR-10):&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Baseline SOTA AUROC:&lt;/strong&gt; 85%
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ViT pre-trained on ImageNet-21k + fine-tuned:&lt;/strong&gt; 96%
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;+ Few-shot (10 images/class):&lt;/strong&gt; 99.46%
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;+ CLIP zero-shot (class names only):&lt;/strong&gt; 94.7%
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Genomics:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Baseline (1D CNN):&lt;/strong&gt; 66%
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;BERT pre-trained &amp;amp; fine-tuned:&lt;/strong&gt; 77.5%
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;+ Few-shot outlier exposure:&lt;/strong&gt; 88.5%
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitations&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Requires access to &lt;strong&gt;large pre-trained models&lt;/strong&gt; (ViT, CLIP, BERT) — compute intensive.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Few-shot exposure&lt;/strong&gt; assumes availability of some labeled OOD examples (may not be realistic in all settings).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Improvements are &lt;strong&gt;less pronounced without fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
CLIP-style zero-shot OOD detection &lt;strong&gt;depends heavily on semantic label quality&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Key Contributions&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 102, 102, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Demonstrated that pre-trained transformers dramatically improve near-OOD detection across modalities.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Introduced &lt;strong&gt;few-shot outlier exposure&lt;/strong&gt; as a practical and effective strategy.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Proposed &lt;strong&gt;zero-shot OOD detection with text-only prompts&lt;/strong&gt; for CLIP.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;The paper &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;clearly shows that fine-tuning on in-distribution (ID) data significantly improves OOD detection performance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;. &amp;nbsp;This finding seems at odds with the conclusions in “&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Generalized Out-of-Distribution Detection: A Survey&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (2024),” which suggests that training-based methods like &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;VOS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;LogitNorm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;G-ODIN&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;, and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;CSI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; who also fine-tune on ID data (using Resnet18 as backbone) can lead to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;overfitting to ID data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;, ultimately reducing the model's ability to generalize well to unseen OOD data.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
