        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_128"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>TweepFake: about Detecting Deepfake Tweets</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>The recent advances in language modeling significantly improved the generative capabilities of deep neural models: in 2019 OpenAI released GPT-2, a pre-trained language model that can autonomously generate coherent, non-trivial and human-like text samples. Since then, ever more powerful text generative models have been developed. Adversaries can exploit these tremendous generative capabilities to enhance social bots that will have the ability to write plausible deepfake messages, hoping to contaminate public debate. To prevent this, it is crucial to develop deepfake social media messages detection systems. However, to the best of our knowledge no one has ever addressed the detection of machine-generated texts on social networks like Twitter or Facebook. With the aim of helping the research in this detection field, we collected the first dataset of \real deepfake tweets, TweepFake. It is real in the sense that each deepfake tweet was actually posted on Twitter. We collected tweets from a total of 23 bots, imitating 17 human accounts. The bots are based on various generation techniques, i.e., Markov Chains, RNN, RNN+Markov, LSTM, GPT-2. We also randomly selected tweets from the humans imitated by the bots to have an overall balanced dataset of 25,572 tweets (half human and half bots generated). The dataset is publicly available on Kaggle. Lastly, we evaluated 13 deepfake text detection methods (based on various state-of-the-art approaches) to both demonstrate the challenges that Tweepfake poses and create a solid baseline of detection techniques. We hope that TweepFake can offer the opportunity to tackle the deepfake detection on social media messages as well.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2021-5-13</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>TweepFake</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2008.00036</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 10:07:59</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2008.00036 [cs]</dc:description>
        <bib:pages>e0251415</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:1932-6203">
        <prism:volume>16</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>PLOS ONE</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1371/journal.pone.0251415</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>5</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>PLoS ONE</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 1932-6203</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_130">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;My Comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Overview of the Paper: &lt;strong&gt;TweepFake: Detecting Deepfake Tweets&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Motivation and Literature Gap:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The rise of &lt;strong&gt;deepfake technologies&lt;/strong&gt; has raised concerns not only in the context of video and audio manipulation but also in the realm of &lt;strong&gt;text generation&lt;/strong&gt; on social media platforms. While deepfake technologies have been widely studied in media like videos and audio, &lt;strong&gt;textual deepfakes&lt;/strong&gt;, such as those found in tweets, have not received as much attention. The paper aims to fill this gap by introducing &lt;strong&gt;TweepFake&lt;/strong&gt;, a &lt;strong&gt;new dataset of deepfake tweets&lt;/strong&gt; that provides the first step toward detecting &lt;strong&gt;deepfake content&lt;/strong&gt; in the form of social media messages.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The motivation behind this research is to develop effective detection methods for identifying &lt;strong&gt;deepfake tweets&lt;/strong&gt; that can manipulate public opinion or spread misinformation on platforms like Twitter. The authors recognize that &lt;strong&gt;current detection techniques&lt;/strong&gt; have limitations when applied to social media text, and their goal is to create a robust solution.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Methods Used to Detect Deepfakes:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors use a combination of &lt;strong&gt;machine learning (ML)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;deep learning (DL)&lt;/strong&gt; models to &lt;strong&gt;detect deepfake tweets&lt;/strong&gt;. They evaluate various text classifiers and deep learning techniques to assess which models are best suited for distinguishing between &lt;strong&gt;human-written tweets&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;machine-generated deepfake tweets&lt;/strong&gt;. Here are the main models tested:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Traditional Machine Learning Models&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Logistic Regression&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;strong&gt;TF-IDF&lt;/strong&gt; (Term Frequency-Inverse Document Frequency).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Support Vector Machine (SVM)&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;strong&gt;TF-IDF&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Naive Bayes&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;strong&gt;TF-IDF&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Deep Learning Models&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Recurrent Neural Networks (RNN)&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM)&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Gated Recurrent Units (GRU)&lt;/strong&gt;, including &lt;strong&gt;CHAR GRU&lt;/strong&gt;, which operates at the character level to capture subtle patterns in deepfake text.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Transformer-based Models&lt;/strong&gt; (Best performing for deepfake detection):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;BERT (Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers)&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;RoBERTa (Robustly Optimized BERT Pretraining Approach)&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;XLNet&lt;/strong&gt;, which combines autoregressive models and BERT-style pretraining.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Data Augmentation-based Methods&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AugMix&lt;/strong&gt;, a method that combines various augmentations to generate diverse training samples, enhancing model robustness.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Baseline Models&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;BoW + TF-IDF&lt;/strong&gt;, a basic approach that uses bag-of-words combined with TF-IDF features.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Results:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The results showed that &lt;strong&gt;transformer-based models&lt;/strong&gt; such as &lt;strong&gt;RoBERTa&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;XLNet&lt;/strong&gt; performed the &lt;strong&gt;best&lt;/strong&gt; in detecting deepfake tweets, achieving nearly &lt;strong&gt;90% accuracy&lt;/strong&gt;. These models outperformed simpler machine learning models like &lt;strong&gt;Logistic Regression&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Naive Bayes&lt;/strong&gt;, which were not as effective in distinguishing between human-written and machine-generated text.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Character-level models&lt;/strong&gt; like &lt;strong&gt;CHAR GRU&lt;/strong&gt; also performed well, particularly with tweets generated by &lt;strong&gt;GPT-2&lt;/strong&gt;, a sophisticated deepfake text generator. The use of &lt;strong&gt;data augmentation&lt;/strong&gt; methods, especially &lt;strong&gt;AugMix&lt;/strong&gt;, improved detection rates, especially when combined with &lt;strong&gt;post-processors&lt;/strong&gt; like &lt;strong&gt;MSP&lt;/strong&gt; (Maximum Softmax Probability) and &lt;strong&gt;ASH&lt;/strong&gt; (Activation Shaping).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The paper also identified the difficulty in detecting &lt;strong&gt;GPT-2-generated tweets&lt;/strong&gt;, which were particularly challenging to distinguish from human-written tweets due to the &lt;strong&gt;high quality of the text generation&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Limitations:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GPT-2-generated tweets&lt;/strong&gt; remain difficult to detect due to the &lt;strong&gt;high fluency and coherence&lt;/strong&gt; of the text generated by the model. This makes it challenging for detectors to reliably differentiate between human-written and machine-generated content.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The dataset, while comprehensive, is &lt;strong&gt;limited to specific types of deepfake generation techniques&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., Markov Chains, RNNs, and GPT-2), and may not cover all possible methods of generating deepfake text.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Character-level models&lt;/strong&gt;, although effective, may still have limitations when dealing with very subtle or advanced deepfake text that mimics human writing too closely.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;Conclusion:&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper introduces &lt;strong&gt;TweepFake&lt;/strong&gt;, the first dataset designed for detecting &lt;strong&gt;deepfake tweets&lt;/strong&gt;, and provides a robust baseline for future research in this area. By testing various &lt;strong&gt;machine learning&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;deep learning models&lt;/strong&gt;, including state-of-the-art transformer-based architectures, the authors demonstrate that &lt;strong&gt;RoBERTa&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;XLNet&lt;/strong&gt; are the most effective models for &lt;strong&gt;deepfake text detection&lt;/strong&gt;. While data augmentation and &lt;strong&gt;post-processors&lt;/strong&gt; like &lt;strong&gt;MSP&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;ASH&lt;/strong&gt; improve detection, challenges remain, especially when dealing with advanced models like &lt;strong&gt;GPT-2&lt;/strong&gt;. This work paves the way for further exploration into &lt;strong&gt;detecting textual deepfakes&lt;/strong&gt; in social media and highlights the importance of improving detection methods to combat the spread of &lt;strong&gt;misinformation&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;automated manipulation&lt;/strong&gt; onli&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
