        <dc:title>GLUE-X: Evaluating Natural Language Understanding Models from an Out-of-distribution Generalization Perspective</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Pre-trained language models (PLMs) are known to improve the generalization performance of natural language understanding models by leveraging large amounts of data during the pre-training phase. However, the out-of-distribution (OOD) generalization problem remains a challenge in many NLP tasks, limiting the real-world deployment of these methods. This paper presents the first attempt at creating a unified benchmark named GLUE-X for evaluating OOD robustness in NLP models, highlighting the importance of OOD robustness and providing insights on how to measure the robustness of a model and how to improve it. The benchmark includes 13 publicly available datasets for OOD testing, and evaluations are conducted on 8 classic NLP tasks over 21 popularly used PLMs, including GPT-3 and GPT-3.5. Our findings confirm the need for improved OOD accuracy in NLP tasks, as significant performance degradation was observed in all settings compared to in-distribution (ID) accuracy.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-05-22</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>GLUE-X</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2211.08073</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 11:26:59</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2211.08073 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2211.08073</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2211.08073</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_132">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Accepted to ACL-23 Findings&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;Yang et al. (2022) presented &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;GLUE-X,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt; a benchmark based on GLUE and then conducted a thorough evaluation of the OOD robustness of language models by training on in-distribution (ID) sets and then testing on OOD sets. The OOD robustness of ChatGPT &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;cannot be evaluated by GLUE and GLUE-X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;benchmarks since it may include the entire GLUE datasets in its training data.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The benchmark includes 15 publicly available datasets for OOD testing, and evaluations are conducted on 8 classic NLP tasks over 21 popularly used PLMs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We introduce the GLUE-X benchmark for evaluating the OOD performance of PLMs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;GLUE is to evaluate Natural Language Understanding &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;(NLU)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Specifically, GLUE-X focuses on &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;domain generalization&lt;/span&gt;, where a model trained on a source domain can be directly generalized to target domains without any labeled or unlabeled data from target domains.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Analysis of 2 main factors affecting the cross-domain generalization performance, we asses:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;21&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;pre-trained language model&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;different training strategies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
linear probing
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
fine-tuning
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
linear probing then fine-tuning method (LP-FT)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Objective&lt;/strong&gt;: This paper presents &lt;strong&gt;GLUE-X&lt;/strong&gt;, an extended benchmark designed to evaluate the out-of-distribution (OOD) performance of pre-trained language models (PLMs) across multiple domains and tasks. GLUE-X aims to fill a gap in evaluating OOD robustness, which has not been adequately addressed in existing benchmarks for natural language understanding (NLU). While some existing evaluations cover only a few tasks or datasets, &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;GLUE-X provides a unified framework that includes multiple datasets across 8 text classification tasks.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Method&lt;/strong&gt;: GLUE-X uses &lt;strong&gt;domain generalization&lt;/strong&gt; techniques, where &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;a model trained on multiple source domains is tested on target domains without access to any target domain data&lt;/span&gt;. The benchmark evaluates 21 different pre-trained language models on standard NLU tasks, using a variety of &lt;strong&gt;fine-tuning strategies&lt;/strong&gt;, such as &lt;strong&gt;linear probing&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;standard fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt;, and a combination of both. Additionally, the paper also introduces post-hoc analyses to assess the model's reasoning during inference.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;I2VU45VC&quot; width=&quot;386&quot; height=&quot;460&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;REYJ3DBE&quot; width=&quot;678&quot; height=&quot;492&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;ZI6H2FZ8&quot; width=&quot;750&quot; height=&quot;505&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;These models represent both &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;discriminative&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;generative&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; model architectures.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Here no particular method is used&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Results&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
significant &lt;strong&gt;performance decay&lt;/strong&gt; when evaluating models on OOD data compared to in-domain (ID) data. The paper shows that &lt;strong&gt;none of the models&lt;/strong&gt; outperform human-level performance across the board, even the best-performing model had a considerable performance gap from human annotators.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;no single backbone architecture&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; consistently outperforms others across all tasks,&lt;/span&gt; emphasizing the complex nature of OOD performance in NLP tasks. The study also suggests that using a &lt;strong&gt;large amount of test data&lt;/strong&gt; helps reduce performance fluctuations, and that the OOD performance is often correlated with the &lt;strong&gt;ID performance&lt;/strong&gt;, though this varies across tasks.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;in terms of the &lt;strong&gt;tuning strategy&lt;/strong&gt;, we show that&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;linear probing and then fine-tuning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; can slightly improve the OOD performance compared to standard fine-tuning.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitations&lt;/strong&gt;: GLUE-X currently only addresses text classification tasks and does not include tasks such as &lt;strong&gt;machine translation&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;summarization&lt;/strong&gt;, or &lt;strong&gt;dialogue systems&lt;/strong&gt;. Furthermore, it acknowledges that extending the benchmark to more real-world datasets from various domains remains essential for improving its generalizability and utility.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Future Work&lt;/strong&gt;: The authors propose the continuous development of GLUE-X, emphasizing the importance of a unified evaluation system for OOD generalization, and aim to incorporate additional domains and tasks to improve the robustness and applicability of the benchmark.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The results demonstrate that while PLMs perform well on in-domain tasks, their &lt;strong&gt;OOD robustness&lt;/strong&gt; remains a significant challenge and requires further advancements in training and testing strategies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
