        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_139"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_141"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_140"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>How Close is ChatGPT to Human Experts? Comparison Corpus, Evaluation, and Detection</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>The introduction of ChatGPT has garnered widespread attention in both academic and industrial communities. ChatGPT is able to respond effectively to a wide range of human questions, providing fluent and comprehensive answers that significantly surpass previous public chatbots in terms of security and usefulness. On one hand, people are curious about how ChatGPT is able to achieve such strength and how far it is from human experts. On the other hand, people are starting to worry about the potential negative impacts that large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT could have on society, such as fake news, plagiarism, and social security issues. In this work, we collected tens of thousands of comparison responses from both human experts and ChatGPT, with questions ranging from open-domain, financial, medical, legal, and psychological areas. We call the collected dataset the Human ChatGPT Comparison Corpus (HC3). Based on the HC3 dataset, we study the characteristics of ChatGPT's responses, the differences and gaps from human experts, and future directions for LLMs. We conducted comprehensive human evaluations and linguistic analyses of ChatGPT-generated content compared with that of humans, where many interesting results are revealed. After that, we conduct extensive experiments on how to effectively detect whether a certain text is generated by ChatGPT or humans. We build three different detection systems, explore several key factors that influence their effectiveness, and evaluate them in different scenarios. The dataset, code, and models are all publicly available at https://github.com/Hello-SimpleAI/chatgpt-comparison-detection.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-01-18</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>How Close is ChatGPT to Human Experts?</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2301.07597</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 12:00:29</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2301.07597 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2301.07597</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2301.07597</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_139">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: https://github.com/Hello-SimpleAI/chatgpt-comparison-detection&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Summary of the Paper: &quot;How Close is ChatGPT to Human Experts? Comparison Corpus, Evaluation, and Detection&quot;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Motivation and Literature Gap:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper addresses the growing concerns about &lt;strong&gt;large language models (LLMs)&lt;/strong&gt; like &lt;strong&gt;ChatGPT&lt;/strong&gt; and their impact on society, particularly in terms of generating misleading or harmful content. While LLMs such as ChatGPT have demonstrated significant improvements in human-like text generation, there are concerns about their potential misuse in areas such as fake news, plagiarism, and social security issues. Existing research has not extensively compared &lt;strong&gt;ChatGPT's responses&lt;/strong&gt; to those of &lt;strong&gt;human experts&lt;/strong&gt; in different domains (e.g., medical, legal, financial), nor have detection systems been adequately developed to distinguish ChatGPT-generated content from human-written content.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Methodology:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors created the &lt;strong&gt;Human ChatGPT Comparison Corpus (HC3)&lt;/strong&gt;, which consists of &lt;strong&gt;nearly 40,000 questions&lt;/strong&gt; and corresponding answers from both &lt;strong&gt;human experts&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;ChatGPT&lt;/strong&gt;. This dataset covers multiple domains, including &lt;strong&gt;open-domain&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;finance&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;medicine&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;law&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;psychology&lt;/strong&gt;. Using this corpus, the authors conducted a &lt;strong&gt;comprehensive comparison&lt;/strong&gt; between ChatGPT and human responses based on several key aspects:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Human Evaluation&lt;/strong&gt;: A &lt;strong&gt;Turing Test&lt;/strong&gt; and a &lt;strong&gt;Helpfulness Test&lt;/strong&gt; were used to assess how well humans could distinguish between ChatGPT and human responses.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Linguistic Analysis&lt;/strong&gt;: Differences in writing style, vocabulary usage, and sentiment were analyzed to identify specific patterns in ChatGPT's text compared to human writing.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ChatGPT Detection Systems&lt;/strong&gt;: The authors developed and tested three &lt;strong&gt;detection models&lt;/strong&gt; to classify whether a piece of text was generated by ChatGPT or a human.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Key Results:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Human vs. ChatGPT Responses&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Human responses&lt;/strong&gt; tend to be more &lt;strong&gt;subjective&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;divergent&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;colloquial&lt;/strong&gt;, using more emotional and nuanced language.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ChatGPT responses&lt;/strong&gt; are more &lt;strong&gt;organized&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;neutral&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;objective&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;formal&lt;/strong&gt;, with a clear structure that defines concepts and provides detailed answers.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ChatGPT often &lt;strong&gt;fabricates information&lt;/strong&gt; when it does not know the answer to a question, especially in specialized domains like &lt;strong&gt;law&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;medicine&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Detection Accuracy&lt;/strong&gt;: The authors trained &lt;strong&gt;RoBERTa-based models&lt;/strong&gt; and tested them on various datasets. The results showed that &lt;strong&gt;sentence-level detection&lt;/strong&gt; was more challenging than full-text detection, with the performance varying based on &lt;strong&gt;data sources&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., &lt;strong&gt;finance&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;open QA&lt;/strong&gt; performed better than &lt;strong&gt;wiki&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;medical datasets&lt;/strong&gt;).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Helpfulness&lt;/strong&gt;: In the &lt;strong&gt;Helpfulness Test&lt;/strong&gt;, ChatGPT's answers were often rated as &lt;strong&gt;more helpful&lt;/strong&gt; than human responses, particularly in domains like &lt;strong&gt;finance&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;psychology&lt;/strong&gt;, where its answers were more concrete and detailed.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Limitations:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Data Imbalance&lt;/strong&gt;: The collected data from different sources is not sufficiently balanced, and there is a need for more &lt;strong&gt;varied&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;diverse data&lt;/strong&gt; to improve the evaluation's accuracy and robustness.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ChatGPT's General Style&lt;/strong&gt;: The analysis was based on &lt;strong&gt;general prompts&lt;/strong&gt; for ChatGPT. Special instructions (e.g., “Pretend to be Shakespeare”) could potentially lead to ChatGPT responses that bypass the detectors or skew the findings.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Language Model Focus&lt;/strong&gt;: The study primarily used &lt;strong&gt;English&lt;/strong&gt; data, and the conclusions from the &lt;strong&gt;Chinese&lt;/strong&gt; dataset might not always align due to ChatGPT's focus on the English language.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This work introduces the &lt;strong&gt;HC3 dataset&lt;/strong&gt;, providing a &lt;strong&gt;valuable resource&lt;/strong&gt; for analyzing and comparing human and ChatGPT responses. It offers insights into the &lt;strong&gt;linguistic characteristics&lt;/strong&gt; of ChatGPT-generated content and reveals important gaps between human and machine-generated responses. The study also highlights the challenges of detecting ChatGPT-generated text, particularly in specific domains and granularities, providing a solid foundation for future research and the development of &lt;strong&gt;AI content detection tools&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Let me know if you need more details on specific parts of the paper!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
