        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_152"/>
        <dc:title>The Art of Abstention: Selective Prediction and Error Regularization for Natural Language Processing</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>In selective prediction, a classifier is allowed to abstain from making predictions on low-confidence examples. Though this setting is interesting and important, selective prediction has rarely been examined in natural language processing (NLP) tasks. To fill this void in the literature, we study in this paper selective prediction for NLP, comparing different models and confidence estimators. We further propose a simple error regularization trick that improves confidence estimation without substantially increasing the computation budget. We show that recent pre-trained transformer models simultaneously improve both model accuracy and confidence estimation effectiveness. We also find that our proposed regularization improves confidence estimation and can be applied to other relevant scenarios, such as using classifier cascades for accuracy–efficiency trade-offs. Source code for this paper can be found at https://github.com/castorini/transformers-selective.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2021-08</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>The Art of Abstention</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2021.acl-long.84/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 14:27:50</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>1040–1051</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
           <bib:Conference><dc:title>ACL-IJCNLP 2021</dc:title></bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_225">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt; bof, papier pas super interessant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Selective prediction&lt;/strong&gt; (classifier abstain from making predictions on low confidence examples) has rarely been examined in &lt;strong&gt;NLP&lt;/strong&gt; tasks.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Here the study is about&lt;strong&gt; multiclass classification&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Selective prediction &lt;/strong&gt;is closely related to &lt;strong&gt;confidence estimation&lt;/strong&gt;, as well as &lt;strong&gt;OOD detection&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We construct a selective classifier by &lt;strong&gt;pairing a standard classifier with a confidence estimator&lt;/strong&gt;. The confidence estimator measures how confident the model is for a certain example, and instructs the classifier to abstain on uncertain ones
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2 choices of confidence estimators:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;softmax response (SR):&lt;/strong&gt; interprets the output of the final softmax layer as a probability distribution and the highest probability as confidence.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
 &lt;strong&gt;Monte-Carlo dropout (MC-dropout)&lt;/strong&gt;: repeats the inference process multiple times, each time with a different dropout mask, and treats the negative variance of maximum probability as confidence. =&amp;gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt; but is MC dropout (as well as ensemble methods) computationally expensive and therefore not applicable in practice? &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;YES&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;By using different dropout masks, MC-dropout is equivalent to using an ensemble for confidence estimation, but does not require actually training and storing multiple models. Nevertheless, compared to SR, the inference cost of MC-dropout is multiplied by R, which can be a problem when model inference is expensive.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;New metric: reversed pair proportion (RPP)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Confidence Estimators&lt;/strong&gt; explored: &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
softmax response
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
difference between probabilities of the top two classes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MC-dropout (because Gal and Ghahramani (2016) argue that “softmax outputs are often erroneously interpreted as model confidence”). 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Error Regularization&lt;/strong&gt;: SR and MC-dropout are often used directly as the confidence estimator. We propose a simple &lt;strong&gt;regularization trick&lt;/strong&gt; that can be easily applied at &lt;strong&gt;training&lt;/strong&gt; (or &lt;strong&gt;fine-tuning &lt;/strong&gt;for pre-trained models) time and can &lt;strong&gt;improve the effectiveness&lt;/strong&gt; of the induced confidence estimators (add regularizer to training loss cf formula in the paper) 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;MC-dropout, despite having a solid theoretical foundation, has difficulties &lt;/span&gt;matching the effectiveness of simple SR in practice
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
