           <foaf:Organization><foaf:name>arXiv</foaf:name></foaf:Organization>
        </dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Yuan</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Lifan</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Chen</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Yangyi</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Cui</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Ganqu</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Gao</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Hongcheng</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Zou</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Fangyuan</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Cheng</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Xingyi</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Ji</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Heng</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Liu</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Zhiyuan</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Sun</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Maosong</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_156"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_158"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_157"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Cryptography and Security</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>BOSS: Revisiting Out-of-distribution Robustness in NLP: Benchmark, Analysis, and LLMs Evaluations</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>This paper reexamines the research on out-of-distribution (OOD) robustness in the field of NLP. We find that the distribution shift settings in previous studies commonly lack adequate challenges, hindering the accurate evaluation of OOD robustness. To address these issues, we propose a benchmark construction protocol that ensures clear differentiation and challenging distribution shifts. Then we introduce BOSS, a Benchmark suite for Out-of-distribution robustneSS evaluation covering 5 tasks and 20 datasets. Based on BOSS, we conduct a series of experiments on pre-trained language models for analysis and evaluation of OOD robustness. First, for vanilla fine-tuning, we examine the relationship between in-distribution (ID) and OOD performance. We identify three typical types that unveil the inner learning mechanism, which could potentially facilitate the forecasting of OOD robustness, correlating with the advancements on ID datasets. Then, we evaluate 5 classic methods on BOSS and find that, despite exhibiting some effectiveness in specific cases, they do not offer significant improvement compared to vanilla fine-tuning. Further, we evaluate 5 LLMs with various adaptation paradigms and find that when sufficient ID data is available, fine-tuning domain-specific models outperform LLMs on ID examples significantly. However, in the case of OOD instances, prioritizing LLMs with in-context learning yields better results. We identify that both fine-tuned small models and LLMs face challenges in effectively addressing downstream tasks. The code is public at \url{https://github.com/lifan-yuan/OOD_NLP}.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-10-26</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>Revisiting Out-of-distribution Robustness in NLP</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2306.04618</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 14:46:23</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2306.04618 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2306.04618</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2306.04618</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_156">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Accepted to NeurIPS 2023 Dataset and Benchmark Track. Code is available at \url{https://github.com/lifan-yuan/OOD_NLP}&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;CODE available&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We propose a benchmark construction protocol that ensures clear differentiation and challenging distribution shifts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We introduce BOSS, a Benchmark suite for Out-of-distribution robustneSS evaluation covering 5 tasks and 20 datasets. Based on BOSS, we conduct a series of experiments on pretrained language models for analysis and evaluation of OOD robustness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;1 Introduction&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There exist multiple definitions of OOD&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Types of out-of-distribution texts and how to detect them 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;classifies distribution shifts into &quot;semantic shift&quot; and &quot;&lt;strong&gt;background shift&lt;/strong&gt;&quot;. Here we use &quot;background shift&quot; as OOD.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Dive into deep learning 2023) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;defines three types of distribution shifts: &lt;strong&gt;covariate shift,&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;label shift&lt;strong&gt;,&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;concept shift.&lt;/strong&gt; Here we use “covariate shift” and “concept shift” as OOD.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Benchmark&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;UT65JQGT&quot; width=&quot;737&quot; height=&quot;377&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We observe a lack of standardized OOD benchmark suites tailored for NLP since there is no single uniform set of datasets for evaluation. Two main from previous datasets:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(1) The selected OOD datasets may come from similar distributions as the ID dataset,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(2) The challenge stemming from distribution shifts is limited, deviating from the expectation for difficulty posed by benchmark construction principles =&amp;gt; overestimation of the OOD robustness.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We establish a protocol for selecting both ID and OOD datasets:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(1) ID datasets should be large and diverse for comprehensive knowledge;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(2) Selection for OOD datasets should prioritize distinct distributions and dissimilarity, regarding text sources and semantics;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(3) Challenging distribution shifts should be prioritized based on performance degradation, to ensure that the benchmark stands the test of time.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We compile BOSS, a more holistic and challenging NLP Benchmark suite for OOD robustneSS evaluation. Unlike existing benchmarks which only consider single task types such as classification &amp;nbsp;or reading comprehension, BOSS covers:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;sentiment analysis,&lt;/strong&gt; (SA)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;toxic detection,&lt;/strong&gt; (TD)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;natural language inference&lt;/strong&gt; for &lt;strong&gt;classification&lt;/strong&gt;, (NLI)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;name entity recognition&lt;/strong&gt; for structured &lt;strong&gt;prediction&lt;/strong&gt;, (NER)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;extractive question answering&lt;/strong&gt; for reading &lt;strong&gt;comprehension&lt;/strong&gt;. (EQA)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We establish &lt;strong&gt;one&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;ID&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;three&lt;/strong&gt; corresponding &lt;strong&gt;OOD&lt;/strong&gt; datasets for each task.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Analysis&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There is a lack of analysis of models’ learning behaviors regarding ID performance and OOD generalization in NLP. Thus, we investigate the correlation between the performance on ID and OOD datasets using the BOSS benchmark. Evaluate setttings:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
model scale
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
training steps
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
available training samples
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
tunable parameters.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;3 typical categories of ID-OOD correlation are observed:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
monotonic linear positive correlation,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
monotonic piecewise linear positive correlation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
non-monotonic V-shaped correlation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Evaluations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We evaluate five representative methods on BOSS. We also consider recent LLMs:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
LLaMA
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
OpenAI text-davinci-003
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OpenAI gpt-3.5-turbo&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We include two relatively smaller models for comparison.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
T0-3B
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
T5-3B
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;We evaluate different scnearios:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt; Zero-shot inference&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;classifies unseen data without task-specific training,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;in-context learning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;uses examples within the prompt to guide predictions,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;few-shot fine-tuning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;minimally updates a model with small labeled datasets, and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;full-data fine-tuning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;extensively retrains the model on comprehensive labeled data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;2 BOSS Benchmark&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;The motivation is to address the lack of systematic evaluation benchmarks for OOD robustness in NLP by creating a standardized framework that overcomes issues like dataset similarity and limited distribution shifts, ensuring rigorous assessment across diverse tasks.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We aim to establish a standard benchmark for rigorous evaluation of OOD robustness in NLP.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rules to construct the datasets:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The ID dataset should provide sufficient knowledge for models to handle the task. &lt;em&gt;It is necessary for the&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;ID datasets to possess the following characteristics: (1) Sufficiently large size; (2) Diversity, which is achieved through collection from multiple sources or the inclusion of several subtypes (i.e., styles, topics, levels of formality, et al)&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Datasets within a given task should originate from diverse distributions for a holistic evaluation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
OOD shifts should be challenging to provide an accurate assessment of progress in OOD robustness. To quantify the challenge, we train a model on the ID dataset and test it on all candidate datasets. These datasets represent different OOD data and are used to simulate how the model would perform on data that is not from the same distribution as its training data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2 3 Dataset selection&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The dataset selection process described in this section outlines how the authors selected &lt;strong&gt;ID&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;OOD&lt;/strong&gt; datasets for evaluating &lt;strong&gt;sentiment analysis&lt;/strong&gt; models (the other task are described in appendix.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. &lt;strong&gt;Candidate Datasets Collection&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The authors start by collecting sentiment analysis datasets from three major sources: &lt;strong&gt;Paperswithcode&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Kaggle&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;ACL Anthology&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pre-2010 datasets&lt;/strong&gt; are filtered out since they are considered largely resolved due to the advent of &lt;strong&gt;pre-trained language models&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This results in &lt;strong&gt;seven remaining datasets&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Amazon&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DSC&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Dynasent&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;IMDb&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SemEval&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SST&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Yelp&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. &lt;strong&gt;Dataset Processing&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Given the &lt;strong&gt;inconsistency in the number of classes&lt;/strong&gt; across datasets, they convert all datasets into a &lt;strong&gt;three-class classification setting&lt;/strong&gt; to standardize them for comparison.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. &lt;strong&gt;Probing Experiments&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors conduct two probing experiments to guide the &lt;strong&gt;ID&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;OOD&lt;/strong&gt; dataset selection:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Semantic Similarity&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They use &lt;strong&gt;SimCSE&lt;/strong&gt; (a supervised version of &lt;strong&gt;RoBERTa-large&lt;/strong&gt;) to assess the &lt;strong&gt;semantic similarity&lt;/strong&gt; between each pair of datasets. The datasets are encoded into &lt;strong&gt;embeddings&lt;/strong&gt;, and then the cosine similarity between their centroid representations is calculated.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Datasets that are too semantically similar to each other are avoided, as they would not represent challenging distribution shifts.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;ID Model Performance&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They train a &lt;strong&gt;T5-large model&lt;/strong&gt; on each potential &lt;strong&gt;ID dataset&lt;/strong&gt; and evaluate it on all the candidate datasets.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The performance drop on the OOD datasets indicates how challenging they are for the model.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;4. &lt;strong&gt;Dataset Selection Process&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;For ID Dataset Selection&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They exclude &lt;strong&gt;DSC&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;IMDb&lt;/strong&gt; because these are binary classification datasets and cannot handle a neutral class, which is often necessary for OOD evaluation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SemEval&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;SST&lt;/strong&gt; are excluded due to their smaller sample sizes (fewer than 10k samples per class).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The remaining datasets are evaluated for &lt;strong&gt;dataset size&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;semantic diversity&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;strong&gt;Amazon&lt;/strong&gt; is chosen as the &lt;strong&gt;ID dataset&lt;/strong&gt; because it contains reviews from 29 distinct product categories, offering more diversity than &lt;strong&gt;Yelp&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;For OOD Dataset Selection&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A &lt;strong&gt;T5-large model&lt;/strong&gt; is trained on the &lt;strong&gt;Amazon dataset&lt;/strong&gt; and tested on the other candidate datasets.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They look for datasets with &lt;strong&gt;low semantic similarity&lt;/strong&gt; to Amazon and a &lt;strong&gt;large performance degradation&lt;/strong&gt; when the model is tested on them.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dynasent&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;SemEval&lt;/strong&gt; are included in the OOD benchmark because:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Dynasent&lt;/strong&gt; is an adversarial dataset, and &lt;strong&gt;SemEval&lt;/strong&gt; is a &lt;strong&gt;Twitter dataset&lt;/strong&gt;—both are valuable due to their low semantic similarity to Amazon and significant performance drop on the ID model.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For &lt;strong&gt;movie review datasets&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;SST&lt;/strong&gt; is selected as the OOD dataset due to its lower semantic similarity to &lt;strong&gt;IMDb&lt;/strong&gt; and its larger performance drop on the ID model.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;5. &lt;strong&gt;Final Selection of Datasets&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After the experiments, the authors end up with the following benchmark for &lt;strong&gt;sentiment analysis&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ID Dataset&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;strong&gt;Amazon&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;OOD Datasets&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;strong&gt;Dynasent&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;SemEval&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;SST&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These three OOD datasets are chosen because they represent &lt;strong&gt;distinct, challenging distribution shifts&lt;/strong&gt; that the model is expected to handle when deployed in real-world scenarios&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;It is worth noting that for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;IMDb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;SST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;, the widely used ID-OOD dataset pair in sentiment analysis, the SimCSE score demonstrates one of the highest levels among dataset pairs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;2.4 BOSS&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;BOSS comprises five essential NLP tasks: sentiment analysis (SA), toxic detection (TD), natural language inference (NLI), name entity recognition (NER), and extractive question answering (EQA). These tasks represent diverse practical applications and provide comprehensive coverage for evaluating models’ capabilities&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;EZFPUPXD&quot; width=&quot;480&quot; height=&quot;161&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;3 Analysis of OOD Robustness : &lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;analyze under vanilla fine-tuning,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Previous research primarily concentrates on method comparisons without delving into models’ learning behaviors. Therefore, we first analyze the models’ learning mechanism by assessing the correlation between ID and OOD performance. We manipulate the ID performance of models by varying their scale, training steps, available training samples, and tunable parameters.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We observe that the correlation between ID and OOD performance on datasets of the five tasks is inconsistent, but can be broadly categorized into 3 types:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;CKLHSJEG&quot; width=&quot;723&quot; height=&quot;397&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Type I.&lt;/strong&gt; This is the most prevalent type of correlation observed across all ID-OOD pairs for sentiment analysis, name entity recognition, and the majority for toxic detection. Here OOD performance is positively and linearly correlated with ID performance.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Type II.&lt;/strong&gt; This category is observed on ID-OOD pairs for extractive question answering. The correlation between OOD performance and ID performance is positive and linear, with a notable difference in the slope before and after the turning point. Specifically, OOD performance demonstrates slow growth until the turning point, after which a minor increase in ID performance yields a substantial improvement in OOD performance.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Type III&lt;/strong&gt;. The V-shaped fitted lines shown in Figure 2c mainly occurs on ID-OOD pairs of NLI tasks. In the first&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;stage, OOD performance experiences worsening performance degradation during the distribution shift. However, in the second stage, the ID-OOD correlation becomes positive.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;4 Evaluation of OOD Robustness&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4.1 Robustness-enhanced Methods&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors evaluate &lt;strong&gt;robustness-enhanced methods&lt;/strong&gt; for improving model performance across different tasks summarized by &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Measure and improve robustness in NLP models: A survey 2021).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; They categorize these methods into four groups:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Data-driven methods&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;EDA (Easy Data Augmentation)&lt;/strong&gt;: Uses simple transformations to generate additional training data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Model and training-based methods&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;FreeLB (Free Large-Batch)&lt;/strong&gt;: Stabilizes training with large batches.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Label Smoothing&lt;/strong&gt;: Regularizes the model by smoothing true labels.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Focal Loss&lt;/strong&gt;: Focuses on hard-to-classify examples, addressing class imbalance.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Inductive-prior-based methods&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Model Ensemble&lt;/strong&gt;: Combines predictions from multiple models to improve robustness.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They exclude &lt;strong&gt;causal intervention methods&lt;/strong&gt;, which are typically used in &lt;strong&gt;low-resource scenarios&lt;/strong&gt;. The selected methods are applied to &lt;strong&gt;DeBERTa-base models&lt;/strong&gt; for &lt;strong&gt;Named Entity Recognition (NER)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;T5-base models&lt;/strong&gt; for other tasks.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Takeaway 1:&lt;/strong&gt; The vanilla fine-tuning (empirical risk minimization) remains a strong baseline. Despite existing methods outperforming vanilla fine-tuning on certain datasets they show limited superiority or can potentially harm model performance.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Takeaway 2: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD detection methods show consistent patterns of success or failure across different datasets, but no single method performs well universally on all datasets within the same NLP task.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To summarize,current approaches fall short of meeting the expectations in enhancing the OOD robustness of models, highlighting the urgent demand for more advanced improvement techniques.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4.2 Large Language Models&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The authors evaluate &lt;strong&gt;five prominent language models (LLMs)&lt;/strong&gt; for their robustness and performance across different tasks. The models they consider are:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LLaMA-7B and LLaMA-13B&lt;/strong&gt; (LLaMA-series)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;OpenAI text-davinci-003&lt;/strong&gt; (referred to as &lt;strong&gt;Davinci3&lt;/strong&gt;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;GPT-3.5-turbo&lt;/strong&gt; (referred to as &lt;strong&gt;Turbo&lt;/strong&gt;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;T0-3B&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;T5-3B&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;Evaluation Setup:&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero-shot inference&lt;/strong&gt; is performed on all models using task instructions, as it is a &lt;strong&gt;general&lt;/strong&gt; evaluation approach that doesn't rely on task-specific fine-tuning.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For &lt;strong&gt;T5-3B&lt;/strong&gt;, the authors explore additional paradigms:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;5-shot learning&lt;/strong&gt;: Using five examples to fine-tune the model.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Full-data fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt;: Fine-tuning the model on the entire dataset.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;In-context learning&lt;/strong&gt;: Selecting exemplars from the &lt;strong&gt;ID dataset&lt;/strong&gt; to provide context during inference.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For the &lt;strong&gt;other LLMs (LLaMA-series, Davinci3, and Turbo)&lt;/strong&gt;, the authors apply &lt;strong&gt;in-context learning (ICL)&lt;/strong&gt; in two variants:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ICL&lt;/strong&gt;: Context is selected from the &lt;strong&gt;ID dataset&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;ICL*&lt;/strong&gt;: Context is selected from the &lt;strong&gt;original training split&lt;/strong&gt; of the evaluated OOD dataset.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In summary, the authors evaluate these models using &lt;strong&gt;zero-shot inference&lt;/strong&gt; for general tasks and apply additional &lt;strong&gt;in-context learning&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt; techniques for specific models to explore different learning paradigms.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Takeaway 1:&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; Fine-tuning small models&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; is preferred when data is abundant, while &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;LLMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; are better suited for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;low-resource scenarios&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;, where there is less data available for training.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Takeaway 2:&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; In-context learning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; does not improve small models' performance, but can help larger models like &lt;strong&gt;Turbo&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;LLaMA&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;Davinci3&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Takeaway 3:&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; ID examples&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; provide more effective contexts for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;in-context learning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;compared to examples from &lt;strong&gt;OOD datasets &lt;/strong&gt;(referred to as &lt;strong&gt;ICL*&lt;/strong&gt; in the paper), though OOD contexts can be useful for specific tasks like &lt;strong&gt;EQA&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;The discussion highlights two key NLP paradigms—&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;general-purpose LLMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(costly, underperform domain-specific models on in-distribution tasks but better on OOD) and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;domain-specific fine-tuned models&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(strong ID performance but OOD weaknesses)—and emphasizes that&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;neither fully solves OOD challenges&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;. It also raises concerns about&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;data contamination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;in LLM evaluations (due to pre-training on public corpora) and advocates for&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;new datasets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;post-dating LLMs' training data to enable reliable OOD testing, especially for temporal shifts.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For LLMs, the only way to be sure about data contamination is to take data after the model has been trained. (temporal data).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;A relevant study to ours is &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;GLUE-X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &amp;nbsp;which establishes an OOD benchmark derived from the GLUE benchmark. Nevertheless, they do not establish a coherent benchmark construction protocol and primarily rely on dataset selection driven by popularity,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Our main observation is that vanilla fine-tuning (a.k.a, empirical risk minimization) remains a strong baseline, while certain methods may slightly improve OOD performance in some cases.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
we find that when provided with enough training data, fine-tuning domain-specific models remain the preferable choices for handling ID examples, while leveraging LLMs with in-context learning is superior for tackling OOD instances. In addition, we observe that the impact of in-context learning on generalization ability varies across models.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
possibility that some datasets have been included in the pretraining corpus of LLMs, so they may not be suitable to test the generalizability of recent LLMs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;we only consider five tasks in this benchmark, which is not a comprehensive collection of current NLP literature.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;The task selection in the BOSS benchmark focuses on&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;natural language understanding, structured prediction, and QA&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(core LM competencies), while excluding&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;NLG (natural language generation)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;commonsense reasoning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;due to challenges:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For &lt;strong&gt;Natural Language Generation (NLG)&lt;/strong&gt; tasks, the main challenge is &lt;strong&gt;evaluation&lt;/strong&gt;. Since &lt;strong&gt;ID models&lt;/strong&gt; may generate responses with styles that differ from reference answers when tested on &lt;strong&gt;OOD datasets&lt;/strong&gt;, existing NLG metrics, which assess predictions based on their similarity to reference answers, may not accurately reflect the quality of the generated text, especially in scenarios with a vast output space. An emerging alternative is using &lt;strong&gt;Language Models like GPT-4&lt;/strong&gt; for scoring, but this approach is &lt;strong&gt;costly&lt;/strong&gt; and lacks &lt;strong&gt;reproducibility&lt;/strong&gt; due to unpredictable updates. Given these challenges, including &lt;strong&gt;generation tasks&lt;/strong&gt; in the OOD benchmark may not be suitable.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Commonsense reasoning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;: Datasets like HellaSwag (world knowledge) and StepGame (spatial reasoning) require fundamentally different skills, making transfer via fine-tuning ineffective; such knowledge should instead come from pre-training.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;The exclusion aims to ensure&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;reliable evaluation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;task coherence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;within the benchmark.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;

&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
