               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Contrastive Novelty-Augmented Learning: Anticipating Outliers with Large Language Models</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>In many task settings, text classification models are likely to encounter examples from novel classes on which they cannot predict correctly. Selective prediction, in which models abstain on low-confidence examples, provides a possible solution, but existing models are often overly confident on unseen classes. To remedy this overconfidence, we introduce Contrastive Novelty-Augmented Learning (CoNAL), a two-step method that generates OOD examples representative of novel classes, then trains to decrease confidence on them. First, we generate OOD examples by prompting a large language model twice: we prompt it to enumerate relevant novel classes, then generate examples from each novel class matching the task format. Second, we train a classifier with a novel contrastive objective that encourages lower confidence on generated OOD examples than training examples. When trained with CoNAL, classifiers improve in their ability to detect and abstain on novel class examples over prior methods by an average of 2.3% in terms of accuracy under the accuracy-coverage curve (AUAC) and 5.5% AUROC across 4 NLP datasets, with no cost to in-distribution accuracy.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-05-26</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>Contrastive Novelty-Augmented Learning</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2211.15718</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 14:46:40</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2211.15718 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2211.15718</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2211.15718</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_160">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: ACL 2023&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper introduces &lt;strong&gt;Contrastive Novelty-Augmented Learning (CoNAL)&lt;/strong&gt;, a novel method to address &lt;strong&gt;Open-Set Selective Classification (OSSC)&lt;/strong&gt;. The problem it tackles occurs when a model faces &lt;strong&gt;out-of-distribution (OOD) examples&lt;/strong&gt; that don't belong to any known class, which is common in real-world applications where the data shifts. Standard classifiers struggle because they may still classify novel data as part of a known category. CoNAL aims to help models correctly abstain from making predictions in such situations, without sacrificing performance on known classes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;u&gt;Objective and Novelty in Literature&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The approach fills a gap in the literature, particularly addressing &lt;strong&gt;the lack of effective strategies for handling novel classes&lt;/strong&gt; during training, which could lead to &lt;strong&gt;overconfidence&lt;/strong&gt; in predictions for unseen data. Prior methods like &lt;strong&gt;Outlier Exposure (OE)&lt;/strong&gt; improve OOD detection but often reduce the model's &lt;strong&gt;in-distribution accuracy&lt;/strong&gt;, whereas CoNAL is designed to avoid this problem.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;u&gt;Methodology&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CoNAL operates in two key phases:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. &lt;strong&gt;Novelty Prompting&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This phase leverages the generative power of &lt;strong&gt;large language models (LLMs)&lt;/strong&gt;, like &lt;strong&gt;GPT-3&lt;/strong&gt;, to create examples of &lt;strong&gt;out-of-distribution (OOD)&lt;/strong&gt; data that simulate potential novel classes the classifier may encounter in real-world scenarios. The goal is to generate data that is:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Plausible&lt;/strong&gt;: The generated examples should have surface features that resemble the training data. For instance, if the task is news classification, the generated examples should also reflect news content.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Semantically novel&lt;/strong&gt;: The generated examples should correspond to classes not present in the training data, ensuring that the model is trained to recognize novel shifts in the data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;two-step process&lt;/strong&gt; for novelty prompting works as follows:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Step 1: Label Generation&lt;/strong&gt;: First, the model generates new class labels that are &lt;strong&gt;topically related but not present in the training dataset&lt;/strong&gt;. This is done by prompting the LLM (e.g., GPT-3) with the existing labels of the dataset (ID labels). The LLM then suggests potential &lt;strong&gt;novel labels&lt;/strong&gt;. For example, if the dataset deals with news, the model might generate labels like &quot;sports,&quot; &quot;business,&quot; and &quot;world&quot; based on the given instructions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Step 2: Example Generation&lt;/strong&gt;: Once the novel labels are generated, the model prompts the LLM again to generate &lt;strong&gt;novel examples&lt;/strong&gt; corresponding to each newly generated label. To make these examples plausible, the LLM is provided with a few context examples from the known classes (ID labels) of the training set. This ensures that the generated examples maintain the same style and structure as the ID examples while being semantically novel.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The output of this phase is a large set of &lt;strong&gt;novel examples&lt;/strong&gt; that are semantically distinct but still consistent with the format of the ID data. These novel examples are crucial for training the classifier to handle out-of-distribution data.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. &lt;strong&gt;Contrastive Confidence Loss (CCL)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Contrastive Confidence Loss (CCL)&lt;/strong&gt; is the second key component of CoNAL. This loss function is designed to train the classifier to have &lt;strong&gt;lower confidence&lt;/strong&gt; on novel (out-of-distribution) examples compared to known (in-distribution) examples.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;How it works&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
During training, the classifier learns to output a &lt;strong&gt;probability distribution&lt;/strong&gt; over the possible classes (ID classes). The &lt;strong&gt;confidence&lt;/strong&gt; for a given prediction is measured using &lt;strong&gt;Maximum Softmax Probability (MaxProb)&lt;/strong&gt;, which picks the class with the highest probability.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For the CCL, two types of examples are used in the loss function: &lt;strong&gt;ID examples&lt;/strong&gt; (the original training data) and &lt;strong&gt;OOD examples&lt;/strong&gt; (the novel examples generated by the novelty prompting phase).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;strong&gt;contrastive aspect&lt;/strong&gt; of the loss function encourages the classifier to be &lt;strong&gt;less confident&lt;/strong&gt; on OOD examples than on ID examples. Specifically, it penalizes cases where the confidence on an OOD example is higher than the confidence on any ID example. This ensures that when the model encounters OOD data, it adjusts its decision-making to reflect uncertainty (abstaining from a confident prediction when necessary).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Datasets:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;AGNews&lt;/strong&gt;: A dataset used for news topic classification, where the goal is to classify news articles into one of four categories: World, Sports, Business, and Science/Technology.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;TREC-10&lt;/strong&gt;: A question intent detection dataset, where the task is to classify questions into one of ten categories related to the type of question (e.g., location, description, etc.).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Emotion&lt;/strong&gt;: A dataset for emotion classification from text, specifically tweets. It is used to predict one of several emotional labels (e.g., joy, sadness, etc.) from a given tweet.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;TACRED&lt;/strong&gt;: A relation extraction dataset that involves classifying relationships between entities in a sentence. It is a highly class-imbalanced dataset, where most classes have very few examples.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;And then the CoNAL method generates OOD samples from these datasets. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper demonstrates that &lt;strong&gt;CoNAL&lt;/strong&gt; improves OOD detection and &lt;strong&gt;OSSC performance&lt;/strong&gt; across several NLP datasets (e.g., AGNews, TREC-10, Emotion, and TACRED), achieving an &lt;strong&gt;average increase of 5.5% in AUROC&lt;/strong&gt; and 2.3% in AUAC compared to the best previous methods. Notably, these improvements occur &lt;strong&gt;without sacrificing ID accuracy&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite its effectiveness, CoNAL faces challenges, particularly in datasets with &lt;strong&gt;strong OOD overconfidence&lt;/strong&gt; like TACRED, where distinguishing between known and novel relations remains difficult. Also, while the approach benefits from using large generative models, it can still suffer from issues related to &lt;strong&gt;generation noise&lt;/strong&gt; where some novel examples may be too similar to ID examples.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In conclusion, CoNAL effectively addresses the &lt;strong&gt;novelty detection problem&lt;/strong&gt; in selective classification settings, improving the ability of classifiers to handle unexpected, unseen data without compromising their performance on known classes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h5&gt;&lt;/h5&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
