    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05083">
        <z:itemType>preprint</z:itemType>
        <dc:publisher>
           <foaf:Organization><foaf:name>arXiv</foaf:name></foaf:Organization>
        </dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Lin</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Haowei</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Gu</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Yuntian</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_166"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_165"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_164"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Artificial Intelligence</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>FLatS: Principled Out-of-Distribution Detection with Feature-Based Likelihood Ratio Score</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Detecting out-of-distribution (OOD) instances is crucial for NLP models in practical applications. Although numerous OOD detection methods exist, most of them are empirical. Backed by theoretical analysis, this paper advocates for the measurement of the &quot;OOD-ness&quot; of a test case $\boldsymbol{x}$ through the likelihood ratio between out-distribution $\mathcal P_{\textit{out}}$ and in-distribution $\mathcal P_{\textit{in}}$. We argue that the state-of-the-art (SOTA) feature-based OOD detection methods, such as Maha and KNN, are suboptimal since they only estimate in-distribution density $p_{\textit{in}}(\boldsymbol{x})$. To address this issue, we propose FLatS, a principled solution for OOD detection based on likelihood ratio. Moreover, we demonstrate that FLatS can serve as a general framework capable of enhancing other OOD detection methods by incorporating out-distribution density $p_{\textit{out}}(\boldsymbol{x})$ estimation. Experiments show that FLatS establishes a new SOTA on popular benchmarks. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/linhaowei1/FLatS.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-10-08</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>FLatS</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2310.05083</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 15:02:51</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2310.05083 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2310.05083</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2310.05083</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_166">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: interesting technique but it needs the access to OOD data to work and in practice it is hard to have OOD data. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;FLatS: Principled Out-of-Distribution Detection with Feature-Based Likelihood Ratio Score&lt;/strong&gt; – &lt;em&gt;Haowei Lin, Yuntian Gu (EMNLP 2023).&lt;/em&gt; This paper proposes &lt;strong&gt;FLatS&lt;/strong&gt;, a theoretically grounded OOD scoring method for text classification​. It computes an OOD score as a &lt;strong&gt;likelihood ratio&lt;/strong&gt; between estimated out-distribution and in-distribution densities in the feature space​. The authors use pretrained language model features (from fine-tuned RoBERTa) and kNN-based density estimates. FLatS achieved &lt;strong&gt;state-of-the-art&lt;/strong&gt; results on &lt;strong&gt;intent classification benchmarks&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g. &lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;CLINC150, ROSTD, Banking77, SNIPS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #eb52f7&quot;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; by substantially lowering false positive rates​. Notably,&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; it outperforms strong baselines like Mahalanobis and kNN distance&lt;/span&gt;, improving average FPR@95 from ~17% to &lt;strong&gt;14.94%&lt;/strong&gt; (across datasets)​. &lt;em&gt;Models:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;RoBERTa&lt;/span&gt; fine-tuned on intent classification. &lt;em&gt;Detection type:&lt;/em&gt; OOD detection for open-intent classification (NLP-specific). &lt;em&gt;Benchmarks:&lt;/em&gt; Four intent classification datasets (CLINC150, ROSTD, Banking77, SNIPS) with unseen intents as OOD​. &lt;em&gt;Key result:&lt;/em&gt; Introducing an auxiliary out-of-distribution data corpus (Wikipedia) and using likelihood ratio scoring yields a new SOTA OOD detector​, which can also &lt;strong&gt;enhance other detectors&lt;/strong&gt; by incorporating OOD density estimation​.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We argue that the state-of-the-art (SOTA) feature-based OOD detection methods, such as Mahalanobis and KNN &amp;nbsp;are suboptimal since they only estimate in-distribution density pin(x). To address this issue, we propose &lt;strong&gt;FLatS&lt;/strong&gt;, a principled solution for OOD detection based on likelihood ratio.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
FLatS can serve as a general framework capable of enhancing other OOD detection methods by incorporating out-distribution density pout(x) estimation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In FLatS, the IND density pin(x) is also estimated with KNN on the training corpus, while the OOD density pout(x) is estimated with KNN on OOD data. Though we are not access to the real OOD data, we leverage public corpus (e.g., Wiki, BookCorpus) as auxiliary OOD data. Apart from KNN,&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; we further demonstrate that the idea of FLatS to incorporate OOD distribution information is applicable to other OOD detection techniques.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The best-performing methods exploit the information of feature / hidden representation h(x; θ) of test case x encoded by the tested NLP model. (ex: Maha, KNN). 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
