        <dc:title>Holistic Sentence Embeddings for Better Out-of-Distribution Detection</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Detecting out-of-distribution (OOD) instances is significant for the safe deployment of NLP models. Among recent textual OOD detection works based on pretrained language models (PLMs), distance-based methods have shown superior performance. However, they estimate sample distance scores in the last-layer CLS embedding space and thus do not make full use of linguistic information underlying in PLMs. To address the issue, we propose to boost OOD detection by deriving more holistic sentence embeddings. On the basis of the observations that token averaging and layer combination contribute to improving OOD detection, we propose a simple embedding approach named Avg-Avg, which averages all token representations from each intermediate layer as the sentence embedding and significantly surpasses the state-of-the-art on a comprehensive suite of benchmarks by a 9.33% FAR95 margin. Furthermore, our analysis demonstrates that it indeed helps preserve general linguistic knowledge in fine-tuned PLMs and substantially benefits detecting background shifts. The simple yet effective embedding method can be applied to fine-tuned PLMs with negligible extra costs, providing a free gain in OOD detection. Our code is available at https://github.com/lancopku/Avg-Avg.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2022-10-14</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2210.07485</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 15:05:27</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2210.07485 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2210.07485</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2210.07485</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_172">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Findings of EMNLP 2022&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;CODE available&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;“Avg-Avg” Holistic Sentence Embeddings for Better OOD Detection&lt;/strong&gt; – &lt;em&gt;Sishuo Chen et al. (Findings of EMNLP 2022).&lt;/em&gt; This NLP-specific study improves OOD detection by changing how sentence embeddings are extracted from PLMs. It observes that using only the final-layer CLS embedding (as is standard) is suboptimal. They propose &lt;strong&gt;Avg-Avg embedding&lt;/strong&gt;, which &lt;em&gt;averages token representations from every Transformer layer&lt;/em&gt; to form a more holistic sentence vector​. This simple approach markedly &lt;strong&gt;boosted OOD detection performance&lt;/strong&gt;, preserving general linguistic features lost in the fine-tuned CLS representation​. On a suite of text classification benchmarks (spanning sentiment analysis, topic classification, etc.), Avg-Avg improved the FAR@95 by &lt;strong&gt;9.33%&lt;/strong&gt; (absolute) over the prior SOTA. &lt;em&gt;Models:&lt;/em&gt; RoBERTa-base and other PLMs (with embedding extraction tweaks). &lt;em&gt;Type:&lt;/em&gt; OOD detection (post-hoc embedding method). &lt;em&gt;NLP-specific:&lt;/em&gt; Yes. &lt;em&gt;Benchmarks:&lt;/em&gt; SST-2 vs. 20 Newsgroups (as OOD)​, TREC-QA, IMDB, etc., following settings from Zhou et al. 2021 and others. &lt;em&gt;Key result:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Layer-averaged embeddings&lt;/strong&gt; significantly improve distance-based OOD detectors, reducing false alarm rates by ~9–10% on multiple NLP tasks​.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Among recent textual OOD detection works based on pretrained language models (PLMs), &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;distance-based methods have shown superior performance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(Revisiting mahalanobis distance for transformer-based out-of-domain detection 2021) (Contrastive out-of-distribution detection for pretrained transformers 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;However, they estimate sample distance scores in the last-layer CLS embedding space (i.e., the inputs to the classification head) &amp;nbsp;and thus do not make full use of linguistic information underlying in PLMs. &amp;nbsp;As known,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;the CLS embedding space is optimized for the indistribution classification task during fine-tuning, thus not necessarily optimal for OOD detection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Works for &lt;strong&gt;Transformer&lt;/strong&gt;-based architectures 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;2.2 Methodology&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In the pretraining-finetuning paradigm, the CLS token is usually put at the beginning of S, and the corresponding vector produced by the last Transformer layer h1L is fed into the classification head for fine-tuning. In existing works &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Revisiting mahalanobis distance for transformer-based out-of-domain detection 2021) (Contrastive out-of-distribution detection for pretrained transformers 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, the CLS vector h1L is regarded as the sentence representation, and OOD detection is conducted in the corresponding embedding space.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To address the limitations of using just the &lt;strong&gt;CLS token&lt;/strong&gt; vector, the authors introduce two key strategies for creating a more &lt;strong&gt;holistic sentence representation&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(1) &lt;strong&gt;Intra-Layer Token Averaging&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;nbsp;Instead of using just the vector of the &lt;strong&gt;CLS token&lt;/strong&gt;, they &lt;strong&gt;average the hidden vectors&lt;/strong&gt; for &lt;strong&gt;all tokens&lt;/strong&gt; at each Transformer layer. We obtain Pi for each &lt;strong&gt;i-th layer&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(2) &lt;strong&gt;Inter-Layer Combination&lt;/strong&gt;: After obtaining the pooled representations from each layer (&lt;strong&gt;P1, P2, ..., PL&lt;/strong&gt;), they &lt;strong&gt;combine&lt;/strong&gt; these representations to get the final sentence representation (&lt;strong&gt;P&lt;/strong&gt;). Where &lt;strong&gt;M&lt;/strong&gt; is the subset of layers being combined. &lt;strong&gt;M&lt;/strong&gt; can include all layers (from 1 to L). v
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
M = {1, 2, . . . , L} (all layers are chosen) in Avg-avg. 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Datasets&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;we choose four datasets corresponding to three tasks as the in-distribution (ID) datasets: (same datasets as in &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Contrastive out-of-distribution detection for pretrained transformers 20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;20)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SST-2 and IMDB for sentiment analysis, 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
TREC-10) for question classification,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;20 Newsgroups &amp;nbsp;for topic classification. &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Among the four, any pair of datasets coming from different tasks is regarded as an ID-OOD.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For &lt;strong&gt;semantic shift&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;nbsp;we use the News Category and CLINC &amp;nbsp;datasets (ID andOOD parts share the same background distribution, but belong to different classes)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For &lt;strong&gt;background shift&lt;/strong&gt; : SST-2 as ID and IMDB as ID, Customer Reviews (CR for short) as OOD (they all belong to the sentiment analysis task but differ in background features, e.g, the length and style)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Model&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
fine-tuning the RoBERTa-base model on ID data
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;OOD detection Methods:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MSP
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
 Energy Score
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
LOF 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mahalanobis distance (MD for short) 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MD combined with contrastive targets
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;QLN33Z7H&quot; width=&quot;299&quot; height=&quot;534&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;8VZH2VPF&quot; width=&quot;359&quot; height=&quot;281&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;KK6ZP6ZS&quot; width=&quot;762&quot; height=&quot;382&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #05a2ef&quot;&gt;They used &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #05a2ef&quot;&gt;Mahalanobis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #05a2ef&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #05a2ef&quot;&gt;distance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt; (Revisiting mahalanobis distance for transformer-based out-of-domain detection 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #05a2ef&quot;&gt; for their experiment (the only difference lies in the embedding space).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
1) averaging all token representations outperforms the standard practice only using the CLS vector;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
2) combining token representations from all intermediate layers brings further improvements.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;These observations lead to an extremely simple yet effective pooling technique: averaging all token representations in each intermediate layer as the sentence embedding for OOD detection. We name the all-layer-all-token pooling technique Avg-Avg and demonstrate that it consistently uplifts the OOD detection performance of BERT and RoBERTa models on a comprehensive suite of textual OOD detection benchmarks.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Avg-Avg helps preserve general linguistic knowledge in fine-tuned PLMs and substantially benefits detecting background shifts.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
negligible extra costs,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Layer choice:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;The study tested how&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;layer selection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;affects OOD detection performance (measured by AUROC) when combining intermediate layers for sentence embeddings. Results showed AUROC&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;initially rises&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;as more layers are added,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;plateaus after 4 layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;, and peaks at&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;7 layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(only&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;0.3% higher&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;than using all layers via &quot;Avg-Avg&quot;). Since&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;OOD data is unavailable during training&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;, exhaustively searching for optimal layer combinations isn’t feasible. Thus,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;using all layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(Avg-Avg) is a practical and near-optimal choice.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
OOD texts can be categorized by whether they exhibit a background shift or a semantic shift &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(Types of out-of-distribution texts and how to detect them 2021).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;our method drastically strengthens the capability of detecting &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;background shifts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;; in contrast, it only slightly improves detecting &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;semantic shifts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
