        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Out-of-Scope Intent Detection with Self-Supervision and Discriminative Training</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Out-of-scope intent detection is of practical importance in task-oriented dialogue systems. Since the distribution of outlier utterances is arbitrary and unknown in the training stage, existing methods commonly rely on strong assumptions on data distribution such as mixture of Gaussians to make inference, resulting in either complex multi-step training procedures or hand-crafted rules such as confidence threshold selection for outlier detection. In this paper, we propose a simple yet effective method to train an out-of-scope intent classifier in a fully end-to-end manner by simulating the test scenario in training, which requires no assumption on data distribution and no additional post-processing or threshold setting. Specifically, we construct a set of pseudo outliers in the training stage, by generating synthetic outliers using inliner features via self-supervision and sampling out-of-scope sentences from easily available open-domain datasets. The pseudo outliers are used to train a discriminative classifier that can be directly applied to and generalize well on the test task. We evaluate our method extensively on four benchmark dialogue datasets and observe significant improvements over state-of-the-art approaches. Our code has been released at https://github.com/liam0949/DCLOOS.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2021-06-17</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2106.08616</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 15:08:07</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2106.08616 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2106.08616</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2106.08616</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_176">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Published as long oral paper in ACL 2021&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt; Specific to NLP. For (out of scope) OOS detection which is a special case of OOD detection. Needs full training. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Out-of-Scope Intent Detection with Self-Supervision and Discriminative Training&lt;/strong&gt; – &lt;em&gt;Li-Ming Zhan et al. (ACL 2021).&lt;/em&gt; This work addresses unknown intent (open-intent) detection in dialogue systems without relying on distributional assumptions. They introduce a simple end-to-end approach to &lt;em&gt;simulate the test scenario during training&lt;/em&gt;: generating &lt;strong&gt;pseudo-OOD examples&lt;/strong&gt; and training a classifier to distinguish them​. They obtain pseudo-OOD data via two strategies: (a) &lt;strong&gt;Self-supervised generation&lt;/strong&gt; – perturbing in-domain utterances using an adversarial attack (a variant of HotFlip) to create “plausible but out-of-domain” utterances, and (b) &lt;strong&gt;Open-domain sampling&lt;/strong&gt; – using readily available corpora (e.g. Wiki, Reddit) as unlabeled outlier examples​. By adding these synthetic outliers as an “extra class” during training, the model learns to reject unknown intents. This approach, called &lt;strong&gt;DCLOOS&lt;/strong&gt;, showed &lt;strong&gt;significant improvements&lt;/strong&gt; over prior state-of-the-art on &lt;strong&gt;four benchmark dialogue datasets&lt;/strong&gt;​. &lt;em&gt;Models:&lt;/em&gt; BERT-based intent classifiers. &lt;em&gt;Type:&lt;/em&gt; OOD intent detection (rejecting unknown intents). &lt;em&gt;NLP-specific:&lt;/em&gt; Yes (dialogue NLU). &lt;em&gt;Benchmarks:&lt;/em&gt; Four intent classification datasets, e.g. &lt;strong&gt;CLINC150, ATIS, Banking77, SNIPS&lt;/strong&gt; (each augmented with out-of-scope examples). &lt;em&gt;Key result:&lt;/em&gt; Training with &lt;strong&gt;synthetic and sampled OOD examples&lt;/strong&gt; in a discriminative fashion yields a robust unknown intent detector, outperforming methods that rely on confidence thresholds or assumed distributions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;OOS (out of Scope) detection&lt;/strong&gt; is a specific type of &lt;strong&gt;OOD detection&lt;/strong&gt; used in &lt;strong&gt;intent classification&lt;/strong&gt; tasks, like chatbots, to identify when a query doesn't match any predefined intent. It focuses on filtering out irrelevant or off-topic inputs. In contrast, &lt;strong&gt;OOD detection&lt;/strong&gt; is a broader concept that detects when an input comes from a &lt;strong&gt;different distribution&lt;/strong&gt; than the training data, which could involve unseen classes, domains, or other data shifts. While OOS is a &lt;strong&gt;special case&lt;/strong&gt; of OOD, it is specifically concerned with &lt;strong&gt;intent relevance&lt;/strong&gt; rather than general distribution shifts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
