        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_181"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_180"/>
        <dc:title>OutFlip: Generating Examples for Unknown Intent Detection with Natural Language Attack</dc:title>
        <dc:date>2021-08</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>OutFlip</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2021.findings-acl.45/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 15:09:10</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>504–512</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
           <bib:Conference><dc:title>Findings 2021</dc:title></bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_181">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;OutFlip: Generating Examples for Unknown Intent Detection with Natural Language Attack&lt;/strong&gt; – &lt;em&gt;DongHyun Choi et al. (Findings of ACL 2021).&lt;/em&gt; This paper specifically focuses on &lt;em&gt;data generation&lt;/em&gt; for open-set intent detection. &lt;strong&gt;OutFlip&lt;/strong&gt; adapts a white-box adversarial attack (HotFlip) to create OOD utterances by flipping tokens in in-domain sentences​. The idea is to produce linguistically valid sentences that fall outside the known intent classes. These synthetic “unknown” examples are then used to train a classifier with an explicit &lt;strong&gt;OOS (out-of-scope) class&lt;/strong&gt;. OutFlip’s novelty lies in using &lt;em&gt;gradient-based token swaps&lt;/em&gt; to generate challenging OOD samples without any external data​. In experiments on intent detection benchmarks (e.g. CLINC150), models augmented with OutFlip’s synthetic OOD data achieved higher OOD recall and balanced accuracy than previous methods that either use random out-of-domain data or no OOD data at all. &lt;em&gt;Models:&lt;/em&gt; BERT with an added “unknown” class. &lt;em&gt;Type:&lt;/em&gt; Novelty detection (unknown intent). &lt;em&gt;NLP-specific:&lt;/em&gt; Yes. &lt;em&gt;Benchmarks:&lt;/em&gt; Open-intent datasets like CLINC150 (with its out-of-scope set), and possibly SNIPS and others, evaluated on intent classification with unknown intents. &lt;em&gt;Key result:&lt;/em&gt; &lt;strong&gt;Adversarial generation of OOD&lt;/strong&gt; examples yields substantial detection gains – OutFlip-trained models showed improved performance over baselines by effectively simulating hard unknown inputs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
    <z:Attachment rdf:about="#item_180">
        <z:itemType>attachment</z:itemType>
        <dc:title>Full Text PDF</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2021.findings-acl.45.pdf</rdf:value>
