        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_185"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_184"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>Selective generation</dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>INTERESTING</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Out-of-Distribution Detection and Selective Generation for Conditional Language Models</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Machine learning algorithms typically assume independent and identically distributed samples in training and at test time. Much work has shown that high-performing ML classifiers can degrade significantly and provide overly-confident, wrong classification predictions, particularly for out-of-distribution (OOD) inputs. Conditional language models (CLMs) are predominantly trained to classify the next token in an output sequence, and may suffer even worse degradation on OOD inputs as the prediction is done auto-regressively over many steps. Furthermore, the space of potential low-quality outputs is larger as arbitrary text can be generated and it is important to know when to trust the generated output. We present a highly accurate and lightweight OOD detection method for CLMs, and demonstrate its effectiveness on abstractive summarization and translation. We also show how our method can be used under the common and realistic setting of distribution shift for selective generation (analogous to selective prediction for classification) of high-quality outputs, while automatically abstaining from low-quality ones, enabling safer deployment of generative language models.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-03-07</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2209.15558</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-22 15:10:14</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2209.15558 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2209.15558</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2209.15558</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_183">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Published in ICLR 2023&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: Papier interessant car c’est pour le moment le seul qui prend en compte l’output du moddèle et la génération. Donc on pourrait s’inspirer de ces idées pour établir une méthode OOD. Par exemple tester KNN au lieu de Maha ou une autre distance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h1&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;the paper detects&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;both input and output OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Out-of-Distribution Detection and Selective Generation for Conditional Language Models&lt;/strong&gt; – &lt;em&gt;Jie Ren et al. (ICLR 2023).&lt;/em&gt; This work extends OOD detection into the realm of &lt;strong&gt;generative NLP tasks&lt;/strong&gt;. It tackles scenarios like abstractive summarization and machine translation, where a conditional language model (CLM) may receive inputs far outside its training distribution. &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;The authors propose a lightweight OOD detector for sequence-to-sequence models&lt;/span&gt;, using metrics like sequence likelihood or model uncertainty to decide when the model is faced with an OOD input. They combine this with a &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;selective generation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; mechanism: if input is detected as OOD, the model abstains or backs off instead of producing possibly low-quality output​&lt;/span&gt;. This approach – analogous to selective prediction in classification – allows safer deployment of generative LLMs by avoiding confident but incorrect generations. &lt;em&gt;Models:&lt;/em&gt; T5 and other large seq2seq models (evaluated on summarization and translation). &lt;em&gt;Type:&lt;/em&gt; OOD detection for &lt;strong&gt;conditional generation&lt;/strong&gt; (with selective response). &lt;em&gt;NLP-specific:&lt;/em&gt; Yes (generative NLP). &lt;em&gt;Benchmarks:&lt;/em&gt; Evaluated on &lt;strong&gt;abstractive summarization&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g. CNN/DailyMail as ID, other news domains as OOD) and &lt;strong&gt;machine translation&lt;/strong&gt; tasks, including scenarios of domain shift. &lt;em&gt;Key result:&lt;/em&gt; The proposed method is &lt;strong&gt;highly accurate in flagging OOD inputs&lt;/strong&gt; for generation tasks. By rejecting low-confidence outputs, it improves the overall quality of system outputs under distribution shift, reducing nonsense/hallucinations on OOD inputs (demonstrating a practical safety improvement for LLM deployments).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;1 INTRODUCTION&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Def:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Conditional language models are typically trained given input sequence x = x1 . . . xL to &lt;strong&gt;autoregressively&lt;/strong&gt; generate the next token in a sequence y = y1 . . . yT as a classification over the token-vocabulary V
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Conditional language models (&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;CLMs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;) are predominantly trained to classify the next token in an output sequence, and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;may suffer even worse degradation on OOD inputs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; as&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;the prediction is done auto-regressively over many steps -&amp;gt; errors propagate and magnify through auto-regression&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;the space of low-quality outputs is greatly increased as arbitrary text sequences can be generated&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A lot of studies focused primarily on classification
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Case studies:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
abstractive summarization
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
translation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;2 OOD DETECTION IN CONDITIONAL LANGUAGE MODELS&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;The maximum softmax probability (&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;MSP&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;) commonly used OOD score for K-class classification problem.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Perplexity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; is used for CLMs. It is analogous to the negative MSP in classification, because both are based&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;on softmax probabilities. So perplexity is a natural OOD score for CLMs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2.1 PERPLEXITY IS ILL-SUITED FOR OOD DETECTION&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;NENQSCWE&quot; width=&quot;734&quot; height=&quot;246&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2.2 DETECTING OOD USING CLM’S EMBEDDINGS&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;This method detects OOD inputs or outputs for CLMs (e.g., translators) by analyzing embeddings of the input text and the model’s generated output.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;3DRR7LJF&quot; width=&quot;602&quot; height=&quot;246&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;2. How It Works&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Which layer? &lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;They use the &lt;strong&gt;final hidden layer&lt;/strong&gt; (i.e., last encoder layer and last decoder layer). No intermediate layers are used or explored.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Which Tokens Do They Use? &lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;They &lt;strong&gt;average all non-padding token representations&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Encoder-side (input)&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;math&quot;&gt;$\mathbf{z} = \frac{1}{L} \sum_{i=1}^{L} \mathbf{h}_i$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where &lt;span class=&quot;math&quot;&gt;$\mathbf{h}_i$&lt;/span&gt; is the hidden state from the final encoder layer for token xi&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Decoder-side (output)&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;math&quot;&gt;$\mathbf{w} = \frac{1}{T} \sum_{i=1}^{T} \mathbf{g}_i$&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where &lt;span class=&quot;math&quot;&gt;$\mathbf{g}_i$&lt;/span&gt; is the hidden state from the final decoder layer for token yi&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
So the model creates &lt;strong&gt;a single fixed-size vector&lt;/strong&gt; per input and per output, by averaging hidden vectors over the sequence (excluding padding).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;METHOD 1: RMD (Relative Mahalanobis Distance)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Step 1: Create &quot;Fingerprints&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Input Fingerprint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Convert the user’s input (e.g.,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;Translate this sentence&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;) into a numerical vector by averaging the model’s encoder states.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Output Fingerprint&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Convert the model’s generated text (e.g., the translation) into a vector using the decoder’s states.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Step 2: Learn &quot;Normal&quot; Patterns&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;During &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;training&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;, the method analyzes&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;correct input-output pairs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., good translations) to define what’s &quot;normal.&quot;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;It builds two&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Gaussian distributions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(bell curves) for:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Inputs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Based on training data (e.g., English sentences).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Outputs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Based on human-provided correct answers (e.g., French translations).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Step 3: Compare to a &quot;Background&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;To avoid false alarms, the method also learns a&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;background distribution&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;from:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Generic data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., random web text) for inputs.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Model-generated text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(not human-provided) for outputs.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Collects a&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;large set of background examples&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(not a single sentence), computes their embeddings, and then estimates the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;mean and covariance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;of these embeddings. This forms a&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;multivariate Gaussian (normal) distribution&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;representing the background.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;By comparing to both the training (ID) and background distributions, the method can distinguish between:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Inputs/outputs that are truly OOD (very different from both training and background).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Inputs/outputs that are just generic or irrelevant (similar to background, not a cause for alarm).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Limit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;: The background choice is crucial: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The background distribution acts as a reference for what is considered &quot;generic&quot; or &quot;irrelevant&quot; input/output.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;If the background is too similar to the training data, the method might miss truly OOD cases (false negatives).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;If the background is too broad or unrelated, the method might flag too many normal cases as OOD (false positives).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Step 4: Calculate OOD Scores&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;br&gt;For a new input/output:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Relative Mahalanobis Distance (RMD)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;= How far the input/output is from &quot;normal&quot; vs. how far it is from the &quot;background.&quot;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;High RMD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Likely OOD (e.g.,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;Explain quantum physics&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;for a cooking chatbot).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Low RMD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Likely safe.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Step 5: Reject or Generate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;If the input&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;or&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;output has a high RMD, the model&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;rejects the request&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., says&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;I can’t help with this&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Otherwise, it provides the generated output.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;METHOD 2: Binary Logits OOD score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Since we have explicitly defined two classes, &lt;strong&gt;in-domain&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;background/general domain&lt;/strong&gt;, another option is to &lt;strong&gt;train a binary classifier&lt;/strong&gt; to &lt;strong&gt;discriminate&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;embeddings&lt;/strong&gt; from the &lt;strong&gt;two classes. &lt;/strong&gt;We train a logistic regression model and use the un-normalized logit for the background as an OOD score. The &lt;strong&gt;Input Binary logits OOD score&lt;/strong&gt; uses the input embeddings as features, whereas the &lt;strong&gt;Output Binary logits OOD score&lt;/strong&gt; uses the decoded output embeddings as features. A higher score suggests higher likelihood of OOD. Though RMD is a generative-model based approach and the binary classifier is a discriminative model, we show that RMD is a generalized version of binary logistic regression&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;Experiment Setup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Summarization model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: PEGASUS-LARGE fine-tuned on&amp;nbsp;ID data &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;XSum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(BBC news summaries).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Datasets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;ID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: XSum (10k examples) and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;C4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(background).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Near-shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: CNN/DailyMail, Newsroom (news articles).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Far-shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Reddit TIFU, ForumSum, SamSum (casual dialogues).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Translation model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Transformer-base trained on ID data&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;WMT15 English-French&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Datasets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;ID&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: WMT15 (100k examples) and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;ParaCrawl&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(background).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: WMT test sets (2014–2015),&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OPUS&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;subsets (law, medical, IT), and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;MTNT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(noisy Reddit data).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Basline:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The proposed OOD detection scores are compared against&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;five baseline methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Perplexity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Original Use&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; Measures the model’s confidence in its output via sequence likelihood. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD Adaptation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Used as a proxy for uncertainty (lower confidence → higher chance of OOD&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Mahalanobis Distance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Computes embedding-based distance between test samples and in-domain data distribution.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;NLI Score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(for summarization): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Original Use&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Evaluates logical consistency between two texts (e.g., premise-hypothesis). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD Adaptation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Tests if summaries match input documents (in-domain vs. OOD factual errors)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;COMET&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(for translation): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Original Use&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Translation quality estimation (no reference needed) using&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;XLM-R&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;to predict translation quality without references. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD Adaptation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Flags poor translations as potential OOD outputs&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Prism&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(for translation): Computes perplexity using a&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;multilingual NMT model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;trained on&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;99.8M sentence pairs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;across 39 languages. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Original Use&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Multilingual translation evaluation via perplexity. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD Adaptation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Detects low-quality (OOD-like) translations&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;RMD&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Binary classifier&lt;/strong&gt; are &lt;strong&gt;better&lt;/strong&gt; at OOD detection than &lt;strong&gt;baselines. &lt;/strong&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;output OOD&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;scores&lt;/strong&gt; perform better for &lt;strong&gt;summarization&lt;/strong&gt;, while the &lt;strong&gt;input OOD scores&lt;/strong&gt; perform better for &lt;strong&gt;translation&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;em&gt;One possible reason is that when summarization outputs are low-quality (e.g. producing repeated text or irrelevant summaries) they look very different than reference summaries, making OOD output score more sensitive to the contrast.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Though &lt;strong&gt;RMD&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Binary logits OOD&lt;/strong&gt; scores both perform &lt;strong&gt;well&lt;/strong&gt; at OOD detection, &lt;strong&gt;RMD OOD&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;score is better at distinguishing &lt;strong&gt;near-OOD&lt;/strong&gt; from far-OOD.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;4 USING OOD SCORES FOR SELECTIVE GENERATION&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Goal: deployment of a CLM to &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;completely abstain from generating on inputs &lt;/span&gt;that are detected as OOD, for which we have shown in Section 3 our OOD scores are fairly accurate. In classification, this has been framed as determining when to trust a classifier, or &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;selective prediction:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Selective classification for deep neural networks (2017)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Simple and scalable predictive uncertainty estimation using deep ensembles (2017)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Plex: Towards reliability using pretrained large model extensions (2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this section, we seek to &lt;strong&gt;predict the quality of generation&lt;/strong&gt; given an example, which may be OOD and abstain if the predicted quality is low. We call this &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;selective generation&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Experiment Setup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We use the same models and datasets described in previous section but instead of simply detecting OOD examples, our focus now is to &lt;strong&gt;predict the quality of generation&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;for examples possibly outside the training distribution.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Measuring Translation quality:&lt;/em&gt; BLEURT
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Measuring Summarization quality&lt;/em&gt;: &amp;nbsp;Difficult to asses the quality of a summary. We report &lt;strong&gt;ROUGE-1&lt;/strong&gt; score as an automatic measure but &lt;strong&gt;primarily use human evaluation&lt;/strong&gt; to assess the quality.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4.2 PERPLEXITY HAS DIMINISHING CAPABILITY IN PREDICTING QUALITY ON OOD DATA&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Since the models are trained using negative log-likelihood as the loss, &lt;strong&gt;perplexity&lt;/strong&gt; (which is monotonically related) is a good predictor of output quality for ID data. &lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Perplexity (tied to the model’s training loss)&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;predicts output quality well for in-domain data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., Kendall’s τ = 0.256 for XSum summaries), but&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;fails on OOD data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4.3 COMBINING OOD SCORES AND PERPLEXITY&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;We observed that our &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;OOD scores&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;perplexity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; are &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;complementary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; in quality prediction. Combining the two provides a comprehensive estimation of uncertainty.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Proposed Methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; &amp;nbsp;to combine perplexity and OOD scores:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Linear Regression&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Trained on 10% data to predict quality (ROUGE-1/BLEURT) using perplexity and OOD scores.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Percentile Rank Sum (PRsum)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Sums percentile ranks of perplexity and OOD scores to normalize scale differences.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Takeaway&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;: Combining OOD scores and perplexity&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;outperforms single metrics&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;expensive baselines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;, offering a lightweight yet effective solution for quality-aware selective generation.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;combined OOD-perplexity scores&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;enable&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;selective generation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;by identifying when to generate high-quality outputs or abstain. Key findings:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Evaluation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Quality vs Abstention (QA) curves&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;show the combined scores achieve&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;highest quality&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;at nearly all abstention rates, outperforming single metrics (e.g., perplexity, OOD) and external tools (COMET/Prism).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Dataset Behavior&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Summarization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Far-OOD (e.g., Reddit) is filtered first; in-domain (XSum) survives longest.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Translation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Low-quality datasets (Koran) are eliminated early; high-quality (law) are retained.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Performance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD scores&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;excel at filtering&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;far-OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., α &amp;gt; 0.65 in translation).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Perplexity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;handles&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;near-OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;better (α &amp;lt; 0.65).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Real-World Impact&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Combined scores&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;prioritize high-quality outputs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;while minimizing unreliable generation.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;J7ZR38FH&quot; width=&quot;700&quot; height=&quot;341&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Related work:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;OOD detection problem is less studied in CLMs. A few studies explored OOD detection in semantic parsing &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Detecting compositionally out-of-distribution examples in semantic parsing 2021; Towards collaborative neural-symbolic graph semantic parsing via uncertainty 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, speech recognition&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Uncertainty estimation in autoregressive structured prediction 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, and machine translation &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Shifts: A dataset of real distributional shift across multiple large-scale tasks 2021; Wat zei je? detecting out-of-distribution translations with variational transformers 2020&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;), &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;but many of them focus on ensemble based methods like Monte Carlo dropout or deep ensemble which use the averaged perplexity after sampling multiple output sequences. The ensembling method costs N times of the inference time, which is not feasible in practice.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this work, we focus on developing scores that can be readily derived from the generative model itself, without much increase in computation. We include an &lt;strong&gt;ensemble-based baseline&lt;/strong&gt; in Section A.6 and show that its &lt;strong&gt;performance is worse than our methods.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Conclusion:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We have proposed lightweight and accurate scores to detect OOD examples for conditional language generation tasks. For real-world deployment, &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;we have also shown how our OOD scores can be combined with language model perplexity to selectively generate high-quality outputs while abstaining from low-quality ones in the setting of input distribution shift.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;The authors propose that their&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;OOD detection and selective generation methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(tested on summarization/translation) are&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;task-agnostic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;and&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;model-agnostic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;, applicable to any&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;sequence generation task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., image captioning) and compatible with&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;decoder-only architectures&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., GPT-3, PaLM).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
