        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_199"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Unsupervised Out-of-Domain Detection via Pre-trained Transformers</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Deployed real-world machine learning applications are often subject to uncontrolled and even potentially malicious inputs. Those out-of-domain inputs can lead to unpredictable outputs and sometimes catastrophic safety issues. Prior studies on out-of-domain detection require in-domain task labels and are limited to supervised classification scenarios. Our work tackles the problem of detecting out-of-domain samples with only unsupervised in-domain data. We utilize the latent representations of pre-trained transformers and propose a simple yet effective method to transform features across all layers to construct out-of-domain detectors efficiently. Two domain-specific fine-tuning approaches are further proposed to boost detection accuracy. Our empirical evaluations of related methods on two datasets validate that our method greatly improves out-of-domain detection ability in a more general scenario.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2022-05-20</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2106.00948</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-23 19:53:56</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2106.00948 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2106.00948</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2106.00948</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_198">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Accepted by ACL 2021. Code is available at https://github.com/rivercold/BERT-unsupervised-OOD&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: interesting paper,talks about which layer to choose for OOD detection and implements a new optimized way to find which layers to use. Method can be used for general purposes, outside supervised classification tasks. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;out-of-domain inputs can lead to unpredictable outputs and sometimes catastrophic safety issues.&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt; Prior studies on &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;out-of-domain (OOD) detection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt; require in-domain task labels and are limited to supervised classification scenarios. Our work tackles the problem of detecting out-of-domain samples with only &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;unsupervised in-domain data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt; We utilize the &lt;strong&gt;latent representations&lt;/strong&gt; of &lt;strong&gt;pre-trained transformers. &lt;/strong&gt;Two domain specific&lt;strong&gt; fine-tuning approaches &lt;/strong&gt;are further proposed to&lt;strong&gt; boost detection accuracy. &lt;/strong&gt;We regard the out-of-domain detection problem as checking whether the given test samples are drawn from the same distribution that generates the in-domain samples.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This method can be adapted to tasks where &lt;strong&gt;no classification labels are present&lt;/strong&gt;, such as active learning or transfer learning. 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We utilize the &lt;strong&gt;latent embeddings of pre-trained transformers&lt;/strong&gt; to represent the input data
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For supervised classification with &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;BERT&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, we can represent the text sequence using the hidden state of &lt;strong&gt;[CLS]&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We study how to&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;leverage all-layer features&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; from a pretrained transformer for OOD detection in an unsupervised manner. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;It is hard to extract features from a certain layer that work well for any OOD datasets. We propose a computationally &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;efficient&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; way to transform &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;all-layer features&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; of a pre-trained transformer into a low-dimension one.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We propose 2 different techniques for &lt;strong&gt;finetuning&lt;/strong&gt; a pre-trained transformer to further improve its capability of detecting OOD data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;With in-domain labels:&lt;/strong&gt; Suppose that we have multi-class label then we can use MSP with temperature T. =&amp;gt; requiring ID labels is a less practical scenario for OOD detection and will not be further discussed. 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Without in-domain labels&lt;/strong&gt; (the models we can obtain are usually not classifiers, but feature extractors instead.). Then we use classical classifiers for OOD like:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
One class &lt;strong&gt;SVM (OC-SVM)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
support vector data description
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;KDE&lt;/strong&gt; (kernel density estimation) &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;and all the work is in &lt;strong&gt;obtaining good representations&lt;/strong&gt; from pretrained transformers,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Description of the Method&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
f_l (x) ∈ Rd : d-dimensional feature embeddings corresponding to the l-th layer for input x
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
f (x) is the overall representation using all layers of f
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;There are &lt;strong&gt;several ways to combine the layers:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Features from the l-th layer f_l:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
option 1: using the hidden states of [CLS] of f_l(x)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
option 2: &lt;u&gt;or&lt;/u&gt; averaging all contextualized token embeddings at the l-layer.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Features from all layers:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using BERT features from only one layer might not be sufficient, as prior work showed that&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;different layers of BERT capture distinct linguistic properties.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
option 1: &amp;nbsp;concatenate all layer wise features f_l(x) =&amp;gt; no information loss but computationally expensive with OC-SVM
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
option 2: aggregation like max- or mean-pooling along the feature dimension across all layers, =&amp;gt; information loss.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Proposed method:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;automatically decide which layers’ features are important (efficient computationally)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Mahalanobis distance as features (MDF) for all layers &lt;/span&gt;:&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;By solving an optimization problem the aim is to find a hyper-ellipsoid such that most of the data points (latent representations of each example) are inside this hyper-ellipsoid. The center c and shape Σ of the ellipsoid are adjusted so that the data are optimally separated (see formula in the paper).&lt;/em&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;This amounts to compute Mahalanobis distance with OC-SVM problem. &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Feature fine-tuning&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;: &amp;nbsp;We can also fine-tune the pre-trained transformer f on the unsupervised ID dataset Dn so that f (x) can better represent the distribution of Dn&lt;/span&gt;. 2 approaches:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In-domain masked language modeling (&lt;strong&gt;IMLM&lt;/strong&gt;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Binary classification with auxiliary dataset (&lt;strong&gt;BCAD&lt;/strong&gt;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;URZWFG4E&quot; width=&quot;853&quot; height=&quot;391&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Datasets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Cross-Corpus (SST)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;In-Domain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: SST (sentiment analysis training set,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;no labels used&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: 500 samples each from&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;20 Newsgroups, Multi30K, RTE, SNLI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(unseen domains).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Cross-Intent (CLINIC150)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;In-Domain&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: 15,000 training queries (150 intents,&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;no intent labels used&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: 1,000 queries outside the 150 intents, mixed with 4,500 in-domain test samples.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Models&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Base Models&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: BERT and RoBERTa (base versions, 768D embeddings, 12 layers).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Training Data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Only&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;in-domain samples&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(no OOD data/labels during training)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Baselines&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;BERT/RoBERTa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;have&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;12 layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(for base models), each producing a&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;768-dimensional vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;for every token in the input text.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They test different settings to compare their method (&lt;strong&gt;MDF&lt;/strong&gt;) to: &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;scenario 1) RoBERTa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Single-Layer Features: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Uses&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;features from one specific layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., layer 5 or 12) of BERT/RoBERTa. They test all layers. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Option A&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Use the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;[CLS] token embedding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(a special token embedding for classification tasks).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Option B&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Average token embeddings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(AVG) at that layer to create a sentence representation.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;scenario &amp;nbsp;2) RoBERTa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Mean Pooling: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Averages&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;all 12 layers' embeddings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;into a single 768D vector.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;scenario 3) RoBERTa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Max Pooling: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Takes the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;maximum value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;for each feature dimension across all layers (768D vector).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;scenario 4) RoBERTa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Euclidean Distance Features (EDF): &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Computes&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Euclidean distance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(instead of Mahalanobis) between input embeddings and in-distribution centroids for each layer → 12D vector.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;scenario 5) TF-IDF + SVD (no deep learning):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;1) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Extract&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;TF-IDF&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp; features.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;2) Reduce dimensions to 100D using&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;SVD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt; &lt;br&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;YSXBILDW&quot; width=&quot;679&quot; height=&quot;539&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Using single-layer feature f_l(x)&lt;/strong&gt;: results obtained from using the &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;[CLS] embedding&lt;/span&gt; or &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;averaging token embeddings (AVG) at each layer of (Ro)BERT(a) models (&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;scenario 1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;=&amp;gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;The best f_l(x) for OOD is dataset-specific and there is no best combination that works with every OOD dataset. &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Pre-trained transformers produce good feature representations:&lt;/strong&gt; Methods using single-layer feature f_l outperforms frequency-based features (TFIDF) and zero-shot classification (MSP), 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Simple aggregations of all layers are not so effective&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: &amp;nbsp;The results of&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; max-pooling&lt;/span&gt; and&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; mean pooling&lt;/span&gt; are not very promising (&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;it helps for ROBERTA for some datasets but degrades perf compared to single layer feature for BER&lt;/span&gt;T) 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MDF is more effective&lt;/strong&gt;: MDF consistently &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;outperforms methods &lt;/span&gt;that directly use&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; features f_l(x), simple aggregations of f_l(x)&lt;/span&gt;, or TF-IDF features on all four metrics
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MDF also performs better than EDF.&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MDF is more efficient in training OC-SVM&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Fine-tuning techniques improve performance: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;we can see both MILM and BCAD improve OOD detection performance when incorporated with MDF separately.The overall best detecting performance is achieved by MILM + BCAD+ MDF, combining both proposed fine-tuning methods with MDF.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The proposed method &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;outperforms baselines that use one-layer features or by simple aggregations of all layers.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Related Work&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Those methods can only be useful when &lt;strong&gt;multi-class labels are available&lt;/strong&gt;, which limits their application in more general domains:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;A baseline for detecting misclassified and out-of-distribution examples in neural networks 2016&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Enhancing the reliability of out-of-distribution image detection in neural networks 2017&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;A simple unified framework for detecting out-of-distribution samples and adversarial attacks 2018&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Deep weighted averaging classifiers 2019&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Pretrained transformers improve out-of-distribution robustness 2020&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;A deep generative distance-based classifier for out-of-domain detection with mahalanobis space 2020&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
