        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_205"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_204"/>
        <dc:subject>Uncertainty Esrtimation</dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Efficient Out-of-Domain Detection for Sequence to Sequence Models</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Sequence-to-sequence (seq2seq) models based on the Transformer architecture have become a ubiquitous tool applicable not only to classical text generation tasks such as machine translation and summarization but also to any other task where an answer can be represented in a form of a finite text fragment (e.g., question answering). However, when deploying a model in practice, we need not only high performance but also an ability to determine cases where the model is not applicable. Uncertainty estimation (UE) techniques provide a tool for identifying out-of-domain (OOD) input where the model is susceptible to errors. State-of-the-art UE methods for seq2seq models rely on computationally heavyweight and impractical deep ensembles. In this work, we perform an empirical investigation of various novel UE methods for large pre-trained seq2seq models T5 and BART on three tasks: machine translation, text summarization, and question answering. We apply computationally lightweight density-based UE methods to seq2seq models and show that they often outperform heavyweight deep ensembles on the task of OOD detection.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-07</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2023.findings-acl.93/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-23 19:55:33</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>1430–1454</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
           <bib:Conference><dc:title>Findings 2023</dc:title></bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_205">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: Talk about generation so this is good. In short this paper tests existing UE (Uncertainty Estimation) usually tested for classification tasks to generation. Show that density based methods and especially Mahalanobis distance is best.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Uncertainty estimation (UE) vs. OOD deteciton:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Differences&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;: Uncertainty estimation measures a model's confidence in predictions (e.g., softmax scores), while OOD detection identifies inputs outside the training data distribution.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Similarity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;: Both use metrics like low confidence scores or feature-space distances to flag unreliable predictions, with OOD detection often relying on uncertainty estimates as part of its framework.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;we perform an &lt;strong&gt;empirical investigation of various novel&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; UE &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;(Uncertainty Estimation) &lt;/span&gt;methods for large pre-trained seq2seq models T5 and BART on three tasks: &lt;strong&gt;machine translation&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;text summarization&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;question answering&lt;/strong&gt;. We apply computationally lightweight density-based UE methods to seq2seq models and show that they often outperform heavyweight deep ensembles on the task of OOD detection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The task of &lt;strong&gt;text generation&lt;/strong&gt; involves &lt;strong&gt;complex autoregressive probabilistic&lt;/strong&gt; models and usually requires making not one but multiple predictions (one per output token). These two factors make UE of predictions in text generation tasks much &lt;strong&gt;more complicated than in standard text classification tasks.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Related work&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;UE for text generation models is still an area of ongoing research with only a limited number of works.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Uncertainty estimation in autoregressive structured prediction 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;=&amp;gt; propose various ensemble-based UE methods for seq2seq models and evaluate them on two tasks: NMT and automatic speech recognition.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Detecting compositionally out-of-distribution examples in semantic parsing 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; =&amp;gt; explores ensemble-based methods in conjunction with Monte Carlo (MC) dropout. Find that the ensemble-based UE methods&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;lead to the best results for OOD detection in the neural semantic parsing task&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Wat zei je? detecting out-of-distribution translations with variational transformers 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; =&amp;gt; introduce a novel UE method BLEUVar, which is also based on MC dropout. The method shows improvements over the baselines in NMT.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(You need only uncertain answers: Data efficient multilingual question answering 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; further explore this method (BLEUVar) for OOD detection in question answering
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Should we trust this summary? Bayesian abstractive summarization to the rescue 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; =&amp;gt; show that BLEUVar can also be applied for UE in summarization.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;=&amp;gt; The above methods entail performing multiple model inferences for each individual input, resulting in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;high computational overhead&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;. (This is also what the paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Out-of-Distribution Detection and Selective Generation for Conditional Language Models&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; said)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Semantic uncertainty: Linguistic invariances for uncertainty estimation in natural language generation 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;=&amp;gt; method that does not leverage MC dropout, but samples multiple predictions without additional inferences. It is called &lt;strong&gt;semantic entropy&lt;/strong&gt; and is based on the idea that different samples can have the same meaning. Outperforms the standard predictive entropy based methods proposed in&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Uncertainty estimation in autoregressive structured prediction 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; on the free-form question answering task.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Framework of the paper:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In this work, we show that &lt;strong&gt;there is significant room for improvement&lt;/strong&gt; for existing OOD detection methods in seq2seq tasks. Moreover, &lt;strong&gt;most of them are computationally intensive.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We adopt methods based on &lt;strong&gt;fitting the probability density of latent instance representations&lt;/strong&gt; obtained from a trained neural network&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(A simple unified framework for detecting out-of-distribution samples and adversarial attacks 2018), (Detection of word adversarial examples in text classification: Benchmark and baseline via robust density estimation 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;. While these methods are shown to be effective for &lt;strong&gt;text classification&lt;/strong&gt; tasks, their application in &lt;strong&gt;text generation&lt;/strong&gt; tasks has received limited research attention.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;UE methods on 3 different sequence generation tasks:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
NMT (Neural Machine Translation)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ATS (&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Automatic Text&amp;nbsp;Summarization)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
QA (Question Answering)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Various types of OOD inputs:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
permutations of tokens from original input
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
texts from a new domain
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
texts from another language.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;OOD detection using uncertainty estimation&lt;/strong&gt; is a &lt;strong&gt;binary&lt;/strong&gt; classification task, where an uncertainty score U (x) of a given input x is a predictor of x coming from an unknown domain.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Short overview of the approaches for UE of autoregressive model predictions investigated here:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;Tested methods 1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;2.1 Information-based Uncertainty Estimation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; in &amp;nbsp;(seq2seq) models (e.g., translators/summarizers)&lt;/span&gt;. &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;We can think of these as &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;generation-aware OOD detectors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;: they require model outputs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Maximum Sequence Probability (MSP)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Use the probability of the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;most confident output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(highest probability sequence in the beam) as the uncertainty score.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Normalized Sequence Probability (NSP)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Compute the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;average probability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;of all sequences in the beam, treating them as samples from the model’s output distribution.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Beam Entropy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Calculate the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;average entropy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;of the model’s token-level predictions across all sequences in the beam (higher entropy = more uncertainty).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;Tested methods 2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;2.2 Ensembling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; to estimate the Uncertainty (following &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Uncertainty estimation in autoregressive structured prediction 2020). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;These methods are &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;expensive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; (multiple forward passes) but can capture model disagreement very effectively — especially useful for ambiguous or noisy inputs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;1. Two Ensemble Approaches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Product of Expectations (PE)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Average token probabilities across models&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;, then generate sequences.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Expectation of Products (EP)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: &amp;nbsp;Generate sequences&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;independently&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;from each model, then average sequence probabilities.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;2. Uncertainty Metrics&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Sequence-Level&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Total Uncertainty (TU)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Measures&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;overall unpredictability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;via entropy of the ensemble’s averaged sequence probabilities&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Reverse Mutual Information (RMI)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Quantifies&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;disagreement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;between models by comparing each model’s output to the ensemble average&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Token-Level&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Mutual Information (MI)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Measures&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;disagreement&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;between models at each token&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Expected Pairwise KL (EPKL)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Averages how much each model’s token predictions differ from others in the ensembles&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;RMI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Token-level version of sequence RMI, detecting local uncertainty spikes (e.g., ambiguous words).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;3. Practical Implementation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;PE-S-TU&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Sequence-level total uncertainty using PE.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;EP-S-RMI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Sequence-level RMI using EP.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;PE-T-EPKL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Token-level EPKL uncertainty averaged across the sequence using PE.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;EP-T-RMI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Token-level RMI using EP.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(229, 110, 238, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(maybe need to complete)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;Tested methods 3)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;2.3 Density-based Methods.&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;These are &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;lightweight&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;, fast, and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;non-generative&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; — making them appealing for inference-time OOD filtering. =&amp;gt; those are the methods that worked best in this paper.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Density-based methods exhibited outstanding performance in UE of deep neural network predictions&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;A simple unified framework for detecting out-of-distribution samples and adversarial attacks 2018&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Uncertainty estimation using a single deep deterministic neural network 2020&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Nonparametric uncertainty quantification for single deterministic neural network 2022&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Detection of word adversarial examples in text classification: Benchmark and baseline via robust density estimation 2022&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The basic idea behind density-based UE methods is to leverage the latent space of the model and fit the probability density of the training input representations within it.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We adopt two state-of-the-art methods of this type for seq2seq models:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mahalanobis Distance (MD)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Robust Density Estimation (RDE) &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Detection of word adversarial examples in text classification: Benchmark and baseline via robust density estimation 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;How exactly are the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;hidden representations averaged&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt; to compute Mahalanobis distance and detect OOD inputs?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Where Are the Representations Taken From?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;The authors explore two main sources for hidden representations:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Encoder representations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;: From the final encoder layer&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Decoder representations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;: From the final decoder layer (less effective in practice)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Let’s focus on encoder representations, since &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;MD-Enc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; (Mahalanobis Distance using Encoder) gives the best performance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Step-by-Step: How Encoder Representations Are Averaged&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Step 1: Token Embeddings from Final Encoder Layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;The input text (e.g., a source sentence or question) is tokenized.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;The input is passed through the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;encoder&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;The final encoder layer outputs a hidden state for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;each token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;, giving a tensor of shape:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;[batch_size, sequence_length, hidden_size]&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;For example, if the input is:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;&quot;The cat sat on the mat.&quot;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;the encoder outputs a hidden vector for each token:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;h₁, h₂, h₃, ..., hₙ ∈ ℝ^d&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Step 2: Exclude Padding Tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Some input sequences are shorter than others, so they are padded.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;The hidden vectors corresponding to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;padding tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; are excluded from the averaging.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;This is done using an &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;attention mask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; or padding mask during the averaging.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Step 3: Average Over Tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;The non-padding token representations are &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;averaged&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; to produce a single fixed-size vector per input:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;57SQXXSC&quot; width=&quot;394&quot; height=&quot;41&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Where:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;N&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; = number of real tokens (excluding padding),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;hᵢ&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; = hidden state of token &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;i&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;h̄&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; ∈ ℝ^d is the averaged vector for the input sequence.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;This &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;h̄&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; becomes the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;summary embedding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; for the entire input — used as the representation for Mahalanobis distance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Step 4: Compute Class-Conditional Mean and Covariance (for ID data)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Over the training (ID) dataset, the authors compute:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;The &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;mean vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;μ&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; of encoder representations for each class (in supervised tasks), or globally for unsupervised detection.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;The &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;covariance matrix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;Σ&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; over those vectors.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;These statistics are computed using the same averaging method above.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Step 5: Mahalanobis Distance Scoring&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;For a new input, get its encoder representation &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;h̄&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; (averaged as above).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Compute the Mahalanobis distance to the ID distribution:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;D_M(h̄) = √[(h̄ - μ)ᵀ Σ⁻¹ (h̄ - μ)]&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;A large distance → more likely OOD&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Decoder Representations (Why They &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Underperform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;The authors also try &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;averaging decoder token representations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; (e.g., from the generated output or ground-truth response).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;In a sequence-to-sequence (seq2seq) model like &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;T5&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; or &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;BART&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;, the decoder generates the output tokens step-by-step.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;During &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;training or teacher-forced evaluation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;, the model is fed the entire gold target sequence (summary, translation, or answer), and it computes:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;One decoder hidden state per output token&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;These are the hidden vectors at the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;final decoder layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;How are decoder token representations averaged?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Tokenize the target sequence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Example: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;&quot;The Eiffel Tower is in Paris.&quot;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; → &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;y₁, y₂, ..., y_T&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Pass target tokens through the decoder (&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;with teacher forcing)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;The decoder gets &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;[y₁, ..., y_T]&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; as input (not generated),&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;It produces decoder hidden states:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;h₁, h₂, ..., h_T where hᵢ ∈ ℝ^d&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;These are taken from the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;final decoder layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Mask out padding tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; (if used):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Some sequences may be shorter than others — padding is applied&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;A mask ensures only &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;real tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; are included in the average&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Compute the mean vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; across all decoder tokens:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;IH57ELTF&quot; width=&quot;186&quot; height=&quot;73&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;This &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;h̄_dec&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; is the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;decoder-side latent representation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt; of the output sequence&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;It’s analogous to the encoder’s averaged representation &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;h̄_enc&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Once you have &lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;h̄_dec&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;, you can use it to compute: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Mahalanobis Distance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;This approach works less well, partly because:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Generation quality may vary,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Decoder states are more variable,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Averaging decoder outputs is noisier (token dependencies, logits).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;Hence, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;MD-Enc&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;, which averages the encoder-side hidden states, performs best.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;3.2 Machine Translation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Experimental setup&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The study evaluates&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;OOD detection methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;for sequence-to-sequence models using:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;In-Domain (ID) Datasets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;WMT’17 English-German&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(En-De)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;WMT’20 English-Russian&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(En-Ru)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Out-of-Domain (OOD) Datasets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;WMT’14 French texts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(unrelated language/domain)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;LibriSpeech test-clean&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(speech transcriptions)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Reddit comments&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(Shifts dataset; informal text)&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Rationale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: These represent diverse OOD scenarios (language, domain, style shifts).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Multilingual mBART&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(Liu et al., 2020) generates predictions for analysis.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Goal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Test OOD detection robustness across&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;mixed domains&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;using Malinin &amp;amp; Gales (2020)’s benchmark framework.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Mahalanobis Distance (MD)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;method&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;outperforms all others&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;in detecting OOD data across most settings. And RDE yield adequate results but MD &amp;gt; RDE.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;MD substantially outperforms all other methods, and it is steadily better than the random choice baseline, while other methods are sometimes worse than the random choice.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Ensemble methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;fail on&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;WMT’14&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(below random-guess performance due to model overconfidence).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Density-based methods (e.g., MD) add&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;minimal computational overhead&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;vs. ensembles (Table 13).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;3.3 Abstractive Text Summarization (ATS)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Datasets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;In-Domain (ID) &amp;amp; OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: XSum (news), AESLC (emails), Movie Reviews (MR), Debate (argumentative texts).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Cross-Testing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Each dataset serves as both ID and OOD in separate experiments (e.g., XSum as ID vs. MR as OOD).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Standard BART&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(Lewis et al., 2020) generates summaries for analysis.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;the density-based methods&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;relying on both encoder and decoder features provide a large improvement over both information-based and ensemble-based methods.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
some OOD configurations where density-based methods achieve the optimal quality turn out to be challenging for both information-based and ensemble-based methods
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
when XSum is the ID dataset, RDE based on encoder features fails to perform well.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The ensemble-based methods struggle to work stable across different settings.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;3.4 Question Answering (QA)&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Datasets&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;SimpleQuestions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(single-relation QA).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Mintaka&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(complex temporal/conditional questions).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;RuBQ 2.0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(Russian-language QA over Wikidata).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;T5 pre-trained for QA&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(Roberts et al., 2020) generates answers in zero-shot mode.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Key Adjustment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Bootstrap sampling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(resampling with replacement) is applied to estimate prediction confidence and standard deviation, addressing the lack of diversity in single-model/density-based methods.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;density-based&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; estimates obtained from encoder derived embeddings &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;outperform&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; all the other uncertainty methods by a large margin.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;

&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Final Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;the &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;density-based approaches are both more effective and computationally efficient than previously explored state-ofthe-art ensemble-based or MC dropout-based methods&lt;/span&gt;. The improvement is consistently observed in all considered tasks.&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Especially Mahalanobis distance &amp;gt; RD &amp;gt;&amp;gt; MC dropout or ensemble base methods&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
