        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>A Survey on Hallucination in Large Language Models: Principles, Taxonomy, Challenges, and Open Questions</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>The emergence of large language models (LLMs) has marked a significant breakthrough in natural language processing (NLP), fueling a paradigm shift in information acquisition. Nevertheless, LLMs are prone to hallucination, generating plausible yet nonfactual content. This phenomenon raises significant concerns over the reliability of LLMs in real-world information retrieval (IR) systems and has attracted intensive research to detect and mitigate such hallucinations. Given the open-ended general-purpose attributes inherent to LLMs, LLM hallucinations present distinct challenges that diverge from prior task-specific models. This divergence highlights the urgency for a nuanced understanding and comprehensive overview of recent advances in LLM hallucinations. In this survey, we begin with an innovative taxonomy of hallucination in the era of LLM and then delve into the factors contributing to hallucinations. Subsequently, we present a thorough overview of hallucination detection methods and benchmarks. Our discussion then transfers to representative methodologies for mitigating LLM hallucinations. Additionally, we delve into the current limitations faced by retrieval-augmented LLMs in combating hallucinations, offering insights for developing more robust IR systems. Finally, we highlight the promising research directions on LLM hallucinations, including hallucination in large vision-language models and understanding of knowledge boundaries in LLM hallucinations.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2025-03-31</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>A Survey on Hallucination in Large Language Models</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2311.05232</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-28 07:43:40</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2311.05232 [cs]</dc:description>
        <bib:pages>1-55</bib:pages>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:1046-8188,%201558-2868">
        <prism:volume>43</prism:volume>
        <dc:title>ACM Transactions on Information Systems</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1145/3703155</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>2</prism:number>
        <dcterms:alternative>ACM Trans. Inf. Syst.</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 1046-8188, 1558-2868</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_239">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Accepted by ACM Transactions on Information Systems (TOIS)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;INTRODUCTION:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
LLMs are prone to hallucination, generating plausible yet nonfactual content.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hallucinations in &lt;strong&gt;conventional&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;natural language generation&lt;/strong&gt; (NLG) tasks have been extensively studied &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Survey of Hallucination in Natural Language Generation 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;. They define hallucinations = generated content that is either nonsensical or unfaithful to the provided source content. Hallucinations categorized into 2 types: &lt;em&gt;intrinsic hallucination&lt;/em&gt;, where the generated output contradicts the source content, and&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;extrinsic hallucination&lt;/em&gt;, where the generated output cannot be verified from the source. However,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;given their remarkable versatility across tasks, &lt;strong&gt;understanding hallucinations in LLMs presents a unique challenge compared to models tailored for specific tasks&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2 types of hallucinations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Factuality hallucination&lt;/strong&gt; emphasizes the discrepancy between generated content and verifiable real world facts, typically manifesting as factual inconsistencies.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Faithfulness hallucination&lt;/strong&gt; captures the divergence of generated content from user input or the lack of self-consistency within the generated content. Sub-divided in:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;instruction inconsistency&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: the content deviates from the user’s original instruction
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;context inconsistency&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: discrepancies from the provided context
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;logical inconsistency&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: pointing out internal contradictions within the content.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;3 approaches to mitigate hallucinations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
data-related
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
training-related
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
inference-related
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;This study &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;encompass these other studies:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Survey of Hallucination in Natural Language Generation (2023): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;shed light on hallucinations in pre-trained models for NLG tasks, leaving LLMs outside their discussion purview&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Trustworthy llms: a survey and guideline for evaluating large language models' alignment (2023):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt; took a broader view of LLM trustworthiness without delving into specific hallucination phenomena,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;A survey of hallucination in large foundation models (2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;A comprehensive survey of hallucination mitigation techniques in large language models (2024) : &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;mainly focused on discussing the mitigation strategies combating LLM hallucinations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Survey on factuality in large language models: Knowledge, retrieval and domain-specificity (2023): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;provided an in-depth look at factuality in LLMs. However, our work narrows down to a critical subset of trustworthiness challenges, specifically addressing factuality and extending the discussion to include faithfulness hallucinations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Siren's song in the AI ocean: a survey on hallucination in large language models (2023): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;presented research closely aligned with ours, detailing LLM hallucination taxonomies, evaluation benchmarks, and mitigation strategies. However, our survey sets itself apart through a unique taxonomy and organizational structure. We present a detailed, layered classification of hallucinations and conduct a more comprehensive analysis of the causes of hallucinations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;LW76BZXE&quot; width=&quot;806&quot; height=&quot;492&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;PCZ29NE3&quot; width=&quot;806&quot; height=&quot;303&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;DEFINITIONS:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;3 stages of LLM trainig:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Pre-training
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Supervised Fine-Tuning
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
RLHF
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2 types of Hallucinations:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;intrinsic hallucination:&lt;/strong&gt; the model outputs directly conflict with the provided source context.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;extrinsic hallucination:&lt;/strong&gt; outputs that cannot be verified using the provided source context or external knowledge bases.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2 types of Hallucinations in LLMs:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Factuality Hallucination:&lt;/strong&gt; discrepancy between generated content and verifiable real world facts, typically manifesting as factual inconsistencies.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Factual Contradiction: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;occurs when an LLM generates text with&amp;nbsp;real-world facts that contradict each other. It is the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;most common type of hallucination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Subtypes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Entity-Error Hallucination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: &amp;nbsp;Incorrect entities in generated text. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Example&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Claiming&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;Thomas Edison invented the telephone&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(correct entity:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Alexander Graham Bell&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Relation-Error Hallucination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Incorrect relationships between entities. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Example&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Stating&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;Thomas Edison invented the light bulb&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(he improved it; others like Humphry Davy contributed earlier).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Fa&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;ctual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Fabrication&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;occurs when an LLM generates&amp;nbsp;unverifiable or&amp;nbsp;exaggerated claims&amp;nbsp;not supported by real-world evidence.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Subtypes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Unverifiability Hallucination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: &amp;nbsp;Statements that are&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;entirely fabricated or lack verifiable sources&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Example&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Claiming&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;the Eiffel Tower’s construction caused the extinction of the Parisian tiger&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(a nonexistent species).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Overclaim Hallucination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Assertions that&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;exaggerate validity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;without universal consensus or evidence. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Example&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Stating&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;the Eiffel Tower sparked the global green architecture movement&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(no broad agreement supports this).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Faithfulness Hallucination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;occurs when LLM outputs&amp;nbsp;deviate from user instructions, context, or logical consistency.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3 Subtypes:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Instruction Inconsistency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Outputs&amp;nbsp;misalign with user directives&amp;nbsp;(non-malicious deviations). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Example&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: User requests translation, but LLM answers questions instead&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Context Inconsistency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Outputs&amp;nbsp;contradict user-provided context. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Example&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: User states&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;Nile’s source is in the Great Lakes region&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;, but LLM denies it&lt;/span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;sup&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.perplexity.ai/search/can-you-help-me-sumarize-the-d-Ll8k9vUQSIaF9h22ecwIgw#user-content-fn-survey&quot; rel=&quot;noopener noreferrer nofollow&quot;&gt;1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Logical Inconsistency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Self-contradictory reasoning&amp;nbsp;(e.g., correct steps but wrong final answer). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Example&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Solving&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;2x = 8&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;correctly but concluding&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;x = 4&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(contradicts intermediate steps).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Hallucinations of LLMs predominantly surface at &lt;/span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;factual&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; levels&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;HALLUCINATION CAUSES:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hallucination from Data &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;=&amp;gt; the principal contributors to LLM hallucinations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Misinformation and biases&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; (Imitative Falsehood, societal biases)&lt;/em&gt; : the presence of misinformation and biases in the flawed pre-training data sources
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Knowledge Boundary&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; (Long-tail Knowledge, Up-to-date Knowledge, Copyright-sensitive Knowledge):&lt;/em&gt; the knowledge boundary inherently bounded by the scope of the pre-training data. Comes from the inability of LLMs to memorize all factual knowledge encountered during pre-training, especially the less frequent long-tail knowledge; and the intrinsic boundary of the pre-training data itself.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Inferior Alignment Data&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; : occurs during &lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;supervised fine-tuning (SFT) when annotators introduces new factual knowledge beyond pre-trained LLM boundaries. The intriduction of new knowledge during SFT &amp;nbsp;increase hallucinations, particularly when instructions focus excessively on task formats or complexity.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hallucination from Training&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Hallucination from Pre-training&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. predicting only the next token limits the ability to capture intricate contextual dependencies, potentially increasing risks for the emergence of hallucination. LLMs can occasionally exhibit unpredictable reasoning hallucinations spanning both long-range and short-range dependencies, which potentially arise from the limitations of soft attention, where attention becomes diluted across positions as sequence length increases. An erroneous token generated by the model cascades errors throughout the subsequent sequence, akin to a snowball effect
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Hallucination from Supervised Fine-tuning&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. when the demands of annotated instructions exceed the model’s pre-defined capability boundaries OR over-fitting on new factual knowledge encourages LLMs prone to fabricating content OR models’ inability to reject since traditional SFT methods typically force models to complete each response, without allowing them to accurately express uncertainty
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Hallucination from RLHF&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;exhibit&amp;nbsp;sycophantic behavior&amp;nbsp;(generating answers that prioritize pleasing humans over truthfulness)&amp;nbsp;there is therefore a misalignment between LLM internal beliefs and the generated output (the model chooses a clearly incorrect answer, despite being aware of its inaccuracy).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hallucination from Inference&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Imperfect Decoding Strategies&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. the randomness in their decoding strategies of LLMs allows for more diverse and high quality text. But the randomness in decoding strategies comes at a cost, as it is positively correlated with an increased risk of hallucinations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Over-confidence&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. Language models often exhibit a localized focus within their attention mechanisms, giving priority to nearby words and resulting in a notable deficit in context attention. This concern is further amplified in LLMs that tend to generate lengthy and comprehensive responses where there is a higher risk of instruction forgetting.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Softmax Bottleneck&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. The majority of language models utilize a softmax layer at the final layer. The softmax can constrains the expressivity of the output probability distributions given the context which prevents LMs from outputting the desired distribution.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Reasoning Failure&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. Even if the LLM possesses the necessary knowledge, it may struggle to produce accurate results if multiple associations exist between questions, due to its limitations in reasoning.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;When LLMs encounter information that falls outside their limited knowledge boundaries, they are more susceptible to generating hallucinations. Hallucinations when LLMs attempt to generate information in domains where their training data is inaccessible&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; (Lila: so detecting OOD is useful for hallucinations)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;HALLUCINATION DETECTION AND BENCHMARKS&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;4.1 Hallucination Detection approaches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(1)&lt;/span&gt; Factuality hallucination detection:&lt;/strong&gt; assessing whether the output of LLMs aligns with real-world facts.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2 categories:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Fact-checking&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: verifying the factuality of the generated response against trusted knowledge sources.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
two primary steps: (1) fact extraction, (2) fact verification.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;methodologies divided into 2 parts:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;External retrieval&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Factscore: Fine-grained atomic evaluation of factual precision in long form text generation 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;a fine-grained factual metric tailored for evaluating long-form text generation. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Fine-grained evaluation of factual precision in long-form text. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Limited scope, resource-intensive for large-scale use.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(FacTool: Factuality Detection in Generative AI--A Tool Augmented Framework for Multi-Task and Multi-Domain Scenarios 2023):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; framework that equips LLMs with the capability to identify factual inaccuracies by utilizing a collection of external tools dedicated to evidence gathering. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Versatile across tasks/domains; integrates external tools for fact-checking. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Relies on the quality of external tools; integration complexity.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Retrieving supporting evidence for llms generated answers 2023&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;) improved the retrieval process through query expansion. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Improved retrieval process for better fact-checking. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Depends on the quality of retrieved evidence; can be time-consuming.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(RAGTruth: A Hallucination Corpus for Developing Trustworthy Retrieval-Augmented Language Models 2024). &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;(&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;not in this paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;) Retrieve facts from a trusted knowledge base and check if model output matches. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Very accurate for knowledge-intensive tasks (e.g., QA). &lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Requires fast retrieval system; not real-time; needs clean reference data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255)&quot;&gt;KnowHalu: Hallucination Detection via Multi-Form Knowledge Based Factual Checking &lt;/span&gt;2024). &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;(&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;not in this paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;)&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;Enrich answers with knowledge graphs or retrieval documents, then check consistency. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Lowers hallucination via retrieval; can be used to verify. &lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;: Heavy infrastructure requirement (retrievers, rankers).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Internal checking&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(CoVe Chain-of-verification reduces hallucination in large language models 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;LLM first generates verification questions for a draft response and subsequently leverages its parametric knowledge to assess the consistency of the answer against the original response. Operates under the assumption that, when appropriately prompted, LLMs can self-correct their mistakes and provide more accurate fact. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Enables self-correction, enhances consistency in responses. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Struggles with highly erroneous initial outputs; limited error detection.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Towards mitigating hallucination in large language models via self-reflection 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; similarly focused on the medical domain and introduced an iterative self-reflection process. This process leverages the inherent ability of LLMs to first generate factual knowledge and then refine the response until it aligns consistently with the provided background knowledge. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Iterative refinement helps improve factual accuracy, especially in specific domains. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Limited to specific domains; may not fully correct initial errors.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Language models (mostly) know what they know 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;, (&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Self-alignment for factuality: Mitigating hallucinations in llms via self-evaluation 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ (not relevant)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(235, 82, 247)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;calculates the probability 𝑝 (𝑇 𝑟𝑢𝑒) to assess the factuality of the response to a boolean question, relying exclusively on the model’s internal knowledge. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Uses the model’s internal knowledge for factual assessment, no external data needed. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Limited awareness of unknown facts; may misjudge confidence in inaccuracies. &lt;strong&gt;Also&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Do large language models know what they don't know? 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(The dawn after the dark: An empirical study on factuality hallucination in large language models 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ (not relevant) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;instruct the LLM to directly predict hallucination judgments considering all factual statements. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Directly predicts hallucinations, based on empirical evidence. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Potential for false positives; findings may not generalize across all LLMs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(LLM-Check: Investigating Detection of Hallucinations in Large Language Models 2024) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (very relevant)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;(&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;not in this paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;) Analyze attention patterns, hidden activations, and token distribution in a single generation. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Super-fast detection (~45× faster than SelfCheckGPT), no retrieval needed. &lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Also needs model internals, but can simulate using small open models.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;However, as LLMs are not inherently reliable factual databases, solely relying on LLMs’ parametric knowledge for fact-checking may result in inaccurate assessments&lt;/span&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Uncertainty Estimation&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: detecting factual inconsistency via internal uncertainty signals. Aim: not rely on external knowledge sources for fact-checking. The origin of LLM hallucinations is inherently tied to the model’s uncertainty. Therefore, by estimating the uncertainty we could detect hallucinations.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Methodologies divided into 2 parts:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;LLM internal states&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;: The internal states of LLMs can serve as informative indicators of their uncertainty, often manifested through metrics like token probability or entropy.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(A stitch in time saves nine: Detecting and mitigating hallucinations of llms by validating low-confidence generation 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (not really relevant)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;determined the model’s uncertainty towards key concepts quantified by considering the minimal token probability within those concepts. =&amp;gt; heavily rely on the self-correction capabilities of LLMs, which may be unreliable + time consuming. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Focuses on identifying uncertainty in key concepts, reducing hallucinations by validating low-confidence generations. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Relies heavily on LLMs’ self-correction abilities, which can be unreliable and time-consuming.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Zero-resource hallucination prevention for large language models 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; a language model’s&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;ability to adeptly reconstruct an original concept from its generated explanation is indicative of its proficiency with that concept. =&amp;gt; heavily rely on the self-correction capabilities of LLMs, which may be unreliable + time consuming. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Focuses on the model's ability to reconstruct concepts, helping detect issues in understanding. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Relies on self-correction of LLMs, which may be inaccurate or slow.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Llm lies: Hallucinations are not bugs, but features as adversarial examples 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (not really relevant)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;interpreted hallucination through the lens of adversarial attacks. They observed that the first token generated from a raw prompt typically exhibits low entropy, compared to those from adversarial attacks. They proposed setting an entropy threshold to define such hallucination attacks. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Uses entropy thresholds to detect hallucinations, providing a method for adversarial attack detection. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; The focus on entropy thresholds can miss some subtle hallucinations, and it may not be universally applicable across models.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(MIND: Unsupervised Real-Time Hallucination Detection based on the Internal States of Large Language Models 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;. &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (not really relevant) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;(&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;not in this paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;) &amp;nbsp;Train an MLP on LLM hidden states during generation to detect hallucination. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Real-time, no reference documents needed, best reported results (HELM benchmark). &lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Needs access to model internals (only feasible for open models).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(EGH Embedding and Gradient Say Wrong: A White-Box Method for Hallucination Detection 2024)&lt;/span&gt;. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (not really relevant) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;(&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;not in this paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;) &amp;nbsp;Measure how much the output changes between &lt;em&gt;unconditional&lt;/em&gt; and &lt;em&gt;conditional&lt;/em&gt; input, using embeddings and gradient features. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Very high accuracy on HaluEval, SelfCheckGPT benchmark. &lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Needs a modified input forward pass (not 100% black-box).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(INSIDE: LLMs' Internal States Retain the Power of Hallucination Detection 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; (&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;not in this paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;) &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (not best best ok)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;LLM behavior&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;: When systems are only accessible via API calls, access to the output’s token-level probability distribution might be unavailable.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Methods involve probing a model’s uncertainty, through natural language prompts or by examining its behavioral manifestations. &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Language models (mostly) know what they know 2022&lt;/span&gt;) (Can llms express their uncertainty? an empirical evaluation of confidence elicitation in llms 2023&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;). &lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;For example:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Selfcheckgpt: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; detected hallucinations via evaluating the consistency among the factual statements by &amp;nbsp;sampling multiple responses from an LLM for the same prompt.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, these methods predominantly rely on &lt;u&gt;direct queries&lt;/u&gt; that explicitly solicit information or verification from the model. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;: No external data needed; fully black-box. &lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Requires multiple generations → computationally expensive.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255)&quot;&gt;(InterrogateLLM: Zero-Resource Hallucination Detection in LLM-Generated Answers &lt;/span&gt;2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;(&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;not in this paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;) Reverse-generate the query from the model's answer multiple times. If inconsistencies appear → likely hallucination. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Zero-resource, no retrieval needed, very strong results in QA. &lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Works best for question answering, less tested on summarization.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Do Language Models Know When They're Hallucinating References? 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; advocated for the use of &lt;u&gt;indirect queries&lt;/u&gt; and by using these indirect queries consistency across multiple model generations can be better evaluated. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Uses indirect queries to evaluate consistency, improving hallucination detection across multiple generations. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Relies on multiple model generations, which can be resource-intensive.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Lm vs lm: Detecting factual errors via cross examination 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;LMvLM approach. This strategy leverages an examiner LM to question an examinee LM, aiming to unveil inconsistencies of claims during multi-turn interaction. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Uses an examiner model to probe the examinee model, detecting inconsistencies over multiple interactions. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; May be computationally expensive and complex due to multi-turn interaction requirements.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Other methods (&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(5, 162, 239)&quot;&gt;not in this paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Token Entropy&lt;/span&gt; (various methods) Hallucinated outputs often have higher uncertainty. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Simple to compute (available during generation). &lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Very noisy — not always correlated with factuality.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Predictive Probability (PP) / Predictive Entropy (PE)&lt;/span&gt; (baseline in MIND, LLM-Check papers) &lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Max, mean, or min pooling of per-token probabilities or entropies across output sequence. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Minimal overhead. &lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Poor detection performance compared to other methods. It has been shown that the length normalized entropy performs better than the non-normalized one&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;original source:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; prompt, context given by the user, references of documents given by the user, the reference material from which the model should derive its answer. &lt;strong&gt;This is the factual or authoritative information that is being used as the basis for generating the output.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;ex&lt;/u&gt;: Source content (Question): &quot;What is the capital of France?&quot; -&amp;gt; Generated content (Answer): &quot;The capital of France is Paris.&quot;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(2)&lt;/span&gt; Faithfulness hallucination detection&lt;/strong&gt;: focuses on evaluating the faithfulness of model’s outputs to the contextual information provided. We categorize existing hallucination detection metrics tailored to faithfulness into the following groups:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Fact-based Metrics: &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;assesses faithfulness by measuring the overlap of facts between the generated content and the source content.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;n-gram-based metrics&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;: BLEU, &amp;nbsp;ROUGE, PARENT-T =&amp;gt; fall short in differentiating the nuanced discrepancies between the generated content and the source content &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Simple and widely used for evaluating content overlap between generated and source content. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Fail to capture nuanced discrepancies; can’t distinguish between superficial and meaningful differences in text.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Entity-based metrics:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt; &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Entity-level factual consistency of abstractive text summarization 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;calculating the overlap of entities. However, even if entities match, the relations between them&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;might be erroneous. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Focuses on entity overlap, useful for checking basic factual consistency.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Even if entities match, the relationships between them might still be wrong, which limits the metric's effectiveness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Relation-based metric&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;s:&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Assessing the factual accuracy of generated text 2019)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; metric that computes the overlap of relation tuples extracted using trained end-to-end fact extraction models. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Uses relation tuples, which can give deeper insights into factual consistency beyond simple entity matching. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Requires accurate fact extraction models, and errors in relation extraction can lead to incorrect assessments.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Classifier-based Metrics: &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;utilizing trained classifiers to distinguish the level of entailment between the generated content and the source content. Idea that genuinely faithful content should inherently be entailed by its source content.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Ranking generated summaries by correctness: An interesting but challenging application for natural language inference 2019), (On faithfulness and factuality in abstractive summarization 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; trained classifiers on NLI (Natural Language Inference) datasets to identify factual inaccuracies, especially in the context of abstract summarization. However,&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Looking beyond sentence-level natural language inference for question answering and text summarization 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;highlighted that the mismatch in input granularity between conventional NLI datasets and inconsistency detection datasets limits their applicability. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; NLI to identify factual inaccuracies and assess faithfulness at a high level. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Mismatch in granularity between NLI datasets and inconsistency detection tasks limits applicability; often requires large, annotated datasets.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Adversarial nli for factual correctness in text summarisation models 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; fine-tuning on adversarial datasets. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Fine-tuning on adversarial datasets helps train classifiers to better handle edge cases and subtle errors in summarization. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Adversarial datasets are complex and may not generalize well to all types of text generation tasks.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Evaluating factuality in generation with dependency-level entailment 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; decomposing the entailment decisions at the dependency arc level. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Provides a more granular level of entailment detection by focusing on dependency arcs, allowing better distinction of factuality. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; More computationally complex and may not capture higher-level inconsistencies in larger contexts.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(SummaC: Re-visiting NLI-based models for inconsistency detection in summarization 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;segmenting documents into sentence units then aggregating scores between sentence pairs. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Segments documents into smaller units (sentences) to evaluate entailment and factuality more effectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Sentence-level evaluation might miss larger, document-level inconsistencies that affect overall faithfulness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;using data from related tasks to finetune the classifier has shown promise in evaluating faithfulness&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;u&gt;BUT&lt;/u&gt; gap between related tasks and the downstream task + scarcity of annotated data. Solution: data augmentation methods to construct synthetical data for fine-tuning the classifier, either by:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
rule-based perturbation &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Evaluating attribution in dialogue systems: The BEGIN benchmark 2022), (Evaluating the factual consistency of abstractive text summarization 2019), (Rome was built in 1776: A case study on factual correctness in knowledge-grounded response generation 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
or generation &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Detecting hallucinated content in conditional neural sequence generation 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;QA-based Metrics:&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; employing question-answering systems to validate the consistency of information between the source content and the generated content;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Method&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;(1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; Extract Target Answers: Identify key claims/info from the LLM’s output &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;(2)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; Question Generation: Create questions targeting those claims &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;(3)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; Source Answers: Generate answers to those questions using the original context/source &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;(4)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; Faithfulness Scoring: Compute similarity (e.g., semantic overlap, NLI, or token-based) between LLM’s answers and source answers. However, variability in aspects like answer selection, question generation, and answer overlap, leading to diverse performance outcome&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(QAFactEval: Improved QA-based factual consistency evaluation for summarization 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; conducted an in-depth evaluation of the components within QA-based metrics. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; In-depth evaluation of the components of QA-based metrics, improving factual consistency assessment in summarization. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Complexity and potential limitations in scalability for diverse types of content or tasks beyond summarization.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Uncertainty-based Metrics: &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;assesses faithfulness by measuring the model’s confidence in its generated outputs&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;entropy-based uncertainty&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(On hallucination and predictive uncertainty in conditional language generation 2021)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; revealed a positive correlation between hallucination likelihood in data-to-text generation and predictive uncertainty, which is estimated by deep ensembles. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Shows positive correlation between predictive uncertainty and hallucination likelihood, useful for detecting potential errors. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Requires deep ensembles, which are resource-intensive and may not scale efficiently for large models.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Looking for a needle in a haystack: A comprehensive study of hallucinations in neural machine translation 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; leveraged the variance in hypotheses yielded by Monte Carlo Dropout as an uncertainty measure within neural machine translation. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Uses variance from multiple hypotheses generated through Monte Carlo Dropout, providing a robust uncertainty measure. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Computationally expensive due to the need for generating multiple hypotheses for uncertainty estimation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Mutual information alleviates hallucinations in abstractive summarization 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; employed conditional entropy to assess model uncertainty in abstractive summarization. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Utilizes conditional entropy, which effectively measures uncertainty, helping reduce hallucinations in summarization. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Might not generalize well across different domains or tasks, limiting its utility outside summarization.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;log-probability base uncertainty&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;: can be applied at different levels of granularity, such as word or sentence level.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Gptscore: Evaluate as you desire 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ (not relevant) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;, (Looking for a needle in a haystack: A comprehensive study of hallucinations in neural machine translation 2022), (Bartscore: Evaluating generated text as text generation 2021&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; adopted length-normalized sequence log-probability to measure model confidence. However, the hallucinated token can be assigned high probability when the&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;preceding context contains the same hallucinated information. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Simple to apply at the word or sentence level, offering a quick measure of model confidence. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Hallucinated tokens can still receive high probabilities if the preceding context includes similar (but incorrect) information, leading to false confidence.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(EUBHD : Enhancing uncertainty-based hallucination detection with stronger focus 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #4eb31c&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(relevant)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; focused on the most informative and important keywords and introduced a penalty mechanism to counteract the propagation of hallucinated content. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Focuses on the most important keywords and introduces a penalty mechanism to curb the spread of hallucinated content. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; The penalty mechanism may not always effectively capture all types of hallucinations, and it could lead to overly cautious evaluations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;LLM-based Judgement: &lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;LLMs are induced to serve as evaluators, assessing the faithfulness of generated content through specific prompting strategies. By providing LLMs with concrete evaluation guidelines and feeding them both the model-generated and source content, they can effectively assess faithfulness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The final evaluation output can either be a binary judgment on faithfulness&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Chatgpt as a factual inconsistency evaluator for text summarization 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Uses LLMs themselves as evaluators, providing a flexible, interpretable way to assess faithfulness directly from the model. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Relies on the quality of the LLM’s self-evaluation capabilities, which may not always be consistent or accurate. OR a k-point Likert scale indicating the degree of faithfulness &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Human-like summarization evaluation with chatgpt 2023). &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Allows for more nuanced evaluations using Likert scales, providing deeper insights into the degree of faithfulness. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Requires careful prompt engineering and may be influenced by biases in the LLM's internal judgment.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For prompt selection, evaluation prompt can either be/&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
direct prompting, chain-of-thought prompting &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Evaluating correctness and faithfulness of instruction-following models for question answering 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Multiple prompt strategies (e.g., direct, chain-of-thought, in-context learning) enhance the flexibility of faithfulness evaluation. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; The effectiveness of prompting strategies depends on the specific task and the LLM's ability to follow complex instructions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
using in-context-learning &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Multi-dimensional evaluation of text summarization with in-context learning 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
or allowing the model to generate evaluation results accompanying with explanations &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Llms as factual reasoners: Insights from existing benchmarks and beyond 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;4.2 Hallucination Benchmarks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2 primary domains:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hallucination Evaluation Benchmarks:&lt;/strong&gt; assess the extent of hallucinations generated by existing cutting-edge LLMs. Emphasizing factual inaccuracies and inconsistency from the given contexts. Targets long-tailed knowledge and challenging questions. As for evaluating,&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; Performance is measured using accuracy metrics for multiple-choice QA, which checks how often the model selects the correct answer, or through generative QA, where answers are evaluated either by human judgment (assessing factual correctness and coherence) or by proxy models (automated models that score the quality of the generated responses)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hallucination Detection Benchmarks:&lt;/strong&gt; evaluate the performance of existing hallucination detection methods.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;LN7XADJI&quot; width=&quot;1066&quot; height=&quot;536&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;UR327F9B&quot; width=&quot;1055&quot; height=&quot;471&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;EUQ9QNYL&quot; width=&quot;1051&quot; height=&quot;113&quot;&gt;+other benchmarks in the paper: ex&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Expertqa: Expert-curated questions and attributed answers 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;HALLUCINATION MITIGATION&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I did not detailed this part too much as this is not the main focus of my project. Indeed, this part is about &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;techniques to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;avoid hallucinations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; which is different from techniques to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;detect hallucinations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;5.1 Mitigating Data-related Hallucinations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(1) &lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;data filtering&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;: aiming at selecting high-quality data to avoid introducing misinformation and biases,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(2) &lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;model editing&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;: focusing on injecting up-to-date knowledge by editing model’s parameters,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(3) &lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;retrieval-augmented generation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;: leveraging external non-parametric database for knowledge supplying.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Post-hoc Retrieval&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: with RAG, relevant knowledge is firstly retrieved by a retriever from external sources, and then the final response is generated by a generator conditioning on both user query and retrieved documents. By decoupling external knowledge from LLM, RAG can effectively alleviate the hallucination caused by the knowledge gap without affecting the performance of LLM. &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;=&amp;gt; interesting for me because I could use RAG to detect hallicinations/for fact-checking.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Rarr: Researching and revising what language models say, using language models 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; research-then-revise workflow, which initially research relevant evidence and subsequently revise the initial generation based on detected discrepancies with the evidence
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Verify-and-edit: A knowledge-enhanced chain-of-thought framework 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; verify-and-edit framework to enhance the factual accuracy of reasoning chains by incorporating external knowledge. For reasoning chains that show lower-than-average consistency, the framework generates verifying questions and then refines the rationales based on retrieved knowledge, ensuring a more factual response.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Improving language models via plug-and-play retrieval feedback 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;enhanced the post-hoc retrieval method through diverse answer generation. Instead of generating just a single answer, they sample various potential answers, allowing for a more comprehensive retrieval feedback (ensembling technique).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;5.2 Mitigating Training-related Hallucination&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Various optimization methods ranging from training stages and alignment stages (SFT &amp;amp; RLHF), aiming to mitigate hallucinations within the training process.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;5.3 Mitigating Inference-related Hallucination&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Strategies aimed at refining the decoding strategy to enhance both the factuality and faithfulness of the LLMs’ outputs.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Factuality Enhanced Decoding&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Chain of natural language inference for reducing large language model ungrounded hallucinations 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;first detected contextual hallucinations at both the sentence and entity levels and then incorporated the judgments to refine the generated response.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Faithfulness Enhanced Decoding&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(KCTS: knowledge-constrained tree search decoding with token-level hallucination detection 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;employed a token-level hallucination detection discriminator to identify contextual hallucinations and then guides the faithful generation process by reweighing the token distribution
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Towards faithful chain-of-thought: Large language models are bridging reasoners 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;conducted a deep analysis of the causal relevance among the context, CoT, and answer during unfaithful reasoning. Analysis revealed that the unfaithfulness issue lies in the inconsistencies in the context information obtained by the CoT and the answer. To address this, they proposed inferential bridging, which takes the attribution method to recall contextual information as hints to enhance CoT reasoning and filter out noisy CoTs that have low semantic consistency and attribution scores to the context.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;HALLUCINATIONS IN RETRIEVAL AUGMENTED GENERATION&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite being designed to mitigate LLM hallucinations, retrieval-augmented LLMs can still produce hallucinations. Hallucinations in RAG present considerable complexities, manifesting as outputs that are either factually inaccurate or misleading. &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;These hallucinations occur when the content generated by the LLM does not align with real-world facts, fails to accurately reflect the user’s query, or is not supported by the retrieved information&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;UNDERSTANDING KNOWLEDGE BOUNDARY IN LLMs&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Despite the impressive capacity to capture factual knowledge from extensive data, &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;LLMs still face challenges in recognizing their own knowledge boundaries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. This shortfall leads to the occurrence of &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;hallucinations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, where LLMs confidently produce falsehoods without an awareness of their own knowledge limits.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(How to catch an ai liar: Lie detection in black-box llms by asking unrelated questions 2023): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;A method to catch hallucinations in LLMs by querying them with unrelated questions. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;: Focuses on detecting when models &quot;lie&quot; by probing their confidence in unrelated queries. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;: Unrelated questions may not always be effective at revealing subtle hallucinations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Investigating the factual knowledge boundary of large language models with retrieval augmentation 2023) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;This paper explores using external retrieval augmentation to help LLMs detect their own factual limitations. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;: Combines LLMs with external retrieval systems to enhance factuality detection. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;: The reliance on external retrieval might not always be accurate or reliable.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Knowing what llms do not know: A simple yet effective self-detection method 2023): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (interesting)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;Proposes a method for LLMs to detect when they are producing answers outside their knowledge base. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;: Simple method for self-detection of hallucinations based on model uncertainty. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;: May not be effective for highly confident but false responses.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;a line of work has revealed that LLMs contain latent structures within their activation space that relate to beliefs about truthfulness.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(SAPLMA : The Internal State of an LLM Knows When It’s Lying 2023)&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (kind of relevant) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;Train a supervised model on LLM hidden layers to predict hallucination likelihood. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Supervised, good on small hallucination datasets. &lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt; Needs a lot of labeled hallucination data to generalize. &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;do not adequately generalize&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(CCS: Discovering latent knowledge in language models without supervision 2022): )&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (kind of relevant)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;Proposes using latent space exploration to detect knowledge limitations in LLMs, without supervision. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;: Explores unsupervised probing techniques to detect factual consistency. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;: Lack of supervision may result in unreliable or imprecise results, and the method doesn't generalize well. &amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;do not adequately generalize.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Inference-time intervention: Eliciting truthful answers from a language model 2023):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (not really relevant: says a little bit the same as the CSS paper) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;Focuses on techniques for intervening during inference to improve the truthfulness of LLMs’ answers. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;: Provides an approach to actively improve the factuality of model outputs. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;: Intervention techniques may not be universally applicable across all tasks or models.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Relative representations enable zero-shot latent space communication 2022):)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (relevant)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;Discusses the use of zero-shot methods to extract truthful knowledge from LLMs. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;: Leverages latent space to improve zero-shot reasoning for truthfulness detection. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;: Effectiveness depends on the quality of the latent representations, which can be inconsistent.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(The curious case of hallucinatory (un) answerability: Finding truths in the hidden states of over-confident large language models 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (very relevant)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;found substantial evidence for LLMs’ ability to encode the unanswerability of questions, despite the fact that these models exhibit overconfidence and produce hallucinations when presented with unanswerable questions. Description: Investigates how LLMs encode their beliefs about answerability and hallucinations in their hidden states. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;: Demonstrates how hidden states can provide insight into LLMs’ awareness of factual correctness. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;: The overconfidence of models may obscure true factual inconsistencies.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Still no lie detector for language models: Probing empirical and conceptual roadblocks 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;READ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; (says that SAPLMA and CSS do not work)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; employed empirical and conceptual tools to probe whether or not LLMs have beliefs.Their empirical results suggest that&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;current &lt;strong&gt;lie-detector methods for LLMs are not yet fully reliable&lt;/strong&gt;. Description: Analyzes the challenges in creating reliable lie detection methods for LLMs. &lt;u&gt;Strengths&lt;/u&gt;: Thorough examination of the current state of lie detection in LLMs. &lt;u&gt;Weaknesses&lt;/u&gt;: It highlights ongoing issues but does not offer new solutions for effective lie detection.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The probing methods proposed by &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Discovering latent knowledge in language models without supervision 2022), (The internal state of an LLM knows when it's lying 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;do not adequately generalize&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Consequently, whether we can effectively probe LLMs’ internal beliefs is ongoing, requiring further research.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h4&gt;&lt;/h4&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
