        <dc:title>Inference-Time Intervention: Eliciting Truthful Answers from a Language Model</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>We introduce Inference-Time Intervention (ITI), a technique designed to enhance the &quot;truthfulness&quot; of large language models (LLMs). ITI operates by shifting model activations during inference, following a set of directions across a limited number of attention heads. This intervention significantly improves the performance of LLaMA models on the TruthfulQA benchmark. On an instruction-finetuned LLaMA called Alpaca, ITI improves its truthfulness from 32.5% to 65.1%. We identify a tradeoff between truthfulness and helpfulness and demonstrate how to balance it by tuning the intervention strength. ITI is minimally invasive and computationally inexpensive. Moreover, the technique is data efficient: while approaches like RLHF require extensive annotations, ITI locates truthful directions using only few hundred examples. Our findings suggest that LLMs may have an internal representation of the likelihood of something being true, even as they produce falsehoods on the surface.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-06-26</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>Inference-Time Intervention</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2306.03341</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-29 11:32:45</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2306.03341 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2306.03341</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2306.03341</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_745">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: NeurIPS 2023 spotlight; code: https://github.com/likenneth/honest_llama&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: Not very relevant. Says basically the same as the paper:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;“CSS: Discovering Latent Knowledge in Language Models Without Supervision (2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;”&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This paper introduces a technique called &lt;strong&gt;Inference-Time Intervention (ITI)&lt;/strong&gt; to make LLMs (like LLaMA) more &lt;strong&gt;truthful&lt;/strong&gt; during generation, &lt;strong&gt;without retraining or fine-tuning&lt;/strong&gt; the model. It works by modifying internal &lt;strong&gt;activations&lt;/strong&gt; at specific attention heads during inference — steering the model’s behavior in a &quot;truthful&quot; direction.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
LLMs often &lt;strong&gt;generate fluent but incorrect (hallucinated)&lt;/strong&gt; statements. Interestingly, prior work suggests that even when LLMs say something false, they may &lt;strong&gt;internally &quot;know&quot; the truth&lt;/strong&gt; — but fail to express it.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Generation-Discrimination Gap (G-D Gap)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Generation Accuracy&lt;/strong&gt;: The model's actual output.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Probe Accuracy&lt;/strong&gt;: The accuracy of a classifier trained on the model's internal activations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Large G-D gaps indicate the model &lt;strong&gt;contains latent knowledge&lt;/strong&gt; it fails to express.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Goal: &lt;strong&gt;Close the G-D gap&lt;/strong&gt; by making the model express what it &quot;knows&quot; to be true, using &lt;strong&gt;no retraining&lt;/strong&gt; and only &lt;strong&gt;modifying inference behavior&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;A theme in the literature is that the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;activation space of many language models&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; appears to contain &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;interpretable directions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;, which play a causal role during inference&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method: Inference-Time Intervention (ITI)&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;1. Identify Truthful Directions&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use a linear probe to find vectors in the attention head activations that &lt;strong&gt;separate truthful from false&lt;/strong&gt; answers.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
distinguishing between &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;attention-head output values&lt;/span&gt; that lead to true or false answers.&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;probe: pθ (xh_l ) = sigmoid(⟨θ, xh_l ⟩). There is &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;one probe per attention head per layer&lt;/span&gt;: the vector xh_l&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;represents the value that the h-th attention head in layer l will contribute to the residual stream.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Dataset: &lt;strong&gt;TruthfulQA&lt;/strong&gt;, a benchmark with adversarial questions targeting common misconceptions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The authors use the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;TruthfulQA dataset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; by &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;concatenating&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; each &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;question-answer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; pair and&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; extracting the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; (&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;head activations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;) at the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;last token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;every attention head in each transformer layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;. They create probing datasets {(xh_l , y)i}_i=1..N for each head, split them into training and validation sets (4:1 ratio), and train binary linear classifiers to see how well each head’s activations predict benchmark performance.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For each attention head and layer:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Collect activations on QA pairs labeled true/false.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Train a simple &lt;strong&gt;linear classifier (probe)&lt;/strong&gt; on those activations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Most attention heads perform at chance level, but some show strong predictive power.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;The best result comes from the 18th head in the 14th layer, achieving 83.3% validation accuracy.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Information relevant to the task is mostly processed in early to middle layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;, with a few standout heads in each layer.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; direction orthogonal to the probe’s decision boundary is treated as the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&quot;truth direction&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For each trained probe, we can think of its parameter θh_l (after normalization) as the first truthful direction. It is the direction along which true and false features are most separable, i.e., the most informative direction&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Why&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;? &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;Y8XY9H9M&quot; width=&quot;769&quot; height=&quot;326&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;First direction (θ):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;They train a linear probe to find the direction in activation space that best separates true from false answers-this is the most informative, “truthful” direction.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Second direction (θ′):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;They train another probe, constrained to be orthogonal to the first, that best separates the classes along a new axis-similar to principal component analysis (PCA).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;They project data onto these two directions and visualize the results. The visualization shows that, while there is substantial overlap between true and false distributions, the second direction still allows for better-than-chance separation. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;This suggests that the concept of “truth” is not confined to a single direction but is distributed across a subspace within the activation space&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2. Shift Activations During Inference&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Once these &quot;truthful&quot; directions are found:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;During token generation:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Identify the &lt;strong&gt;top-K attention heads&lt;/strong&gt; most associated with truth.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Shift their activations&lt;/strong&gt; along the &quot;truthful&quot; direction using a strength parameter α.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The shift is repeated for &lt;strong&gt;each token&lt;/strong&gt; in the generation process.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This nudges the model to follow its &lt;strong&gt;internal truth representation&lt;/strong&gt;, influencing what it outputs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3. Variants of Direction Shifts&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They compare different ways of defining the intervention direction:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Probe direction&lt;/strong&gt; (from the linear classifier)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mass mean shift&lt;/strong&gt; (difference between means of true and false activations) ← &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;best&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Contrast-Consistent Search (CCS)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Baselines&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;1) Supervised Fine-Tuning (SFT): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The model is fine-tuned on question-answer pairs to encourage truthful answers and discourage false ones using cross-entropy loss. All model parameters are updated, aiming to serve as a strong upper bound for parameter-efficient fine-tuning.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;2) Few-Shot Prompting (FSP): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The model receives several example Q&amp;amp;A pairs (e.g., 50-shot) in the prompt to guide it toward truthful answers.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;3) Instruction Fine-Tuning (IFT): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The model is fine-tuned on instruction-following datasets to improve truthfulness and alignment.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;4) ITI Activation Shift Directions: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Three directions for intervening in model activations are compared:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Probe Weight Direction:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;Uses the direction found by a linear probe that best separates truthful from false answers.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Mass Mean Shift:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;The difference between the mean activation vectors (hidden states) for true and false answers at a given attention head.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Contrast-Consistent Search (CCS):&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;Finds a direction using only pairwise internal activations &lt;/span&gt;(hidden states)&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;(without labels); labels are used only afterward to identify the truthful direction for intervention.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Dataset&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: TruthfulQA (Main Benchmark)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Models&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: LLaMA-7B, Alpaca, Vicuna
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Key metric: &lt;strong&gt;True*Informative&lt;/strong&gt; — combines truthfulness and informativeness&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;L5RTIT27&quot; width=&quot;814&quot; height=&quot;221&quot;&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Large improvements with minimal changes to model behavior (low KL divergence and CE loss increase).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The truthful direction is easy to identify and &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;requires only a small subset of data for effective intervention&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;; truthfulness plateaus quickly as training set size increases.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Our findings suggest that LLMs may have an internal representation of the likelihood of something being true, even as they produce falsehoods on the surface.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Identify Truthful Directions&lt;/u&gt;: &lt;/em&gt;Best results came from a small subset of heads (e.g., Head 18, Layer 14 in LLaMA-7B), showing that&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;truth is encoded sparsely&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Mass mean shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; outperformed other direction-finding methods.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Head-wise selection of top attention heads performed best (vs. full or point-wise selection) =&amp;gt;During inference-time intervention (ITI), the model doesn’t modify all activations blindly. Instead, it chooses where to apply the shift toward the &quot;truth direction.&quot; Moreover, Intervening on too many heads harms performance due to oversteering. Although it might seem intuitive that more intervention = better, the authors find the opposite.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Trade-off observed: stronger interventions improve &lt;strong&gt;truth&lt;/strong&gt;, but reduce &lt;strong&gt;helpfulness&lt;/strong&gt; (i.e., more “I have no comment” answers).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Generalization Beyond TruthfulQA&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Tested zero-shot generalization on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Natural Questions&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;TriviaQA&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MMLU&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Slight improvements observed, especially on MMLU:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
e.g., LLaMA-7B on MMLU: 35.7% → 40.2% with ITI
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Shows &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;potential for transfer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; to other QA tasks.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
