    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://arxiv.org/abs/2309.03883">
        <z:itemType>preprint</z:itemType>
        <dc:publisher>
           <foaf:Organization><foaf:name>arXiv</foaf:name></foaf:Organization>
        </dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Chuang</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Yung-Sung</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Xie</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Yujia</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Luo</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Hongyin</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Kim</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Yoon</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Glass</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>James</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>He</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Pengcheng</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_274"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_275"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Artificial Intelligence</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>DoLa: Decoding by Contrasting Layers Improves Factuality in Large Language Models</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Despite their impressive capabilities, large language models (LLMs) are prone to hallucinations, i.e., generating content that deviates from facts seen during pretraining. We propose a simple decoding strategy for reducing hallucinations with pretrained LLMs that does not require conditioning on retrieved external knowledge nor additional fine-tuning. Our approach obtains the next-token distribution by contrasting the differences in logits obtained from projecting the later layers versus earlier layers to the vocabulary space, exploiting the fact that factual knowledge in an LLMs has generally been shown to be localized to particular transformer layers. We find that this Decoding by Contrasting Layers (DoLa) approach is able to better surface factual knowledge and reduce the generation of incorrect facts. DoLa consistently improves the truthfulness across multiple choices tasks and open-ended generation tasks, for example improving the performance of LLaMA family models on TruthfulQA by 12-17% absolute points, demonstrating its potential in making LLMs reliably generate truthful facts.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-03-11</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>DoLa</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2309.03883</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-29 11:42:17</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2309.03883 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2309.03883</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2309.03883</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_274">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: ICLR 2024 main conference paper. The source code is available at https://github.com/voidism/DoLa&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;My comments: Absolutely Not relevant it is for avoiding Hallucination, not detecting them&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;DoLa (Decoding by Contrasting Layers)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;is a decoding strategy designed to reduce hallucinations in large language models (LLMs) by leveraging&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;factual knowledge localization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;within transformer layers. Here's how it works:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Core Concept&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;DoLa exploits the observation that factual knowledge in LLMs is&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;localized to specific transformer layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;. By contrasting predictions from&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;later layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(where factual knowledge emerges) against&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;earlier layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(which encode more general features), it amplifies factually relevant signals while suppressing hallucinations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;Method Breakdown&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Layer Selection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Earlier layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., intermediate layers) provide&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;general linguistic features&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Later layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., final layers) encode&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;factual knowledge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;refined through deeper processing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;136&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Logit Contrasting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Compute&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;next-token probabilities&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;using logits from both:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Later layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;→ Full knowledge refinement.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Earlier layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;→ Baseline linguistic representation.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Subtract earlier-layer logits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;from later-layer logits to isolate&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;fact-specific signals&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;adjusted_logits = logits(later_layer) - logits(earlier_layer)&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;This emphasizes tokens where factual knowledge&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;emerges distinctly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;in later layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;13&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Decoding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Use the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;contrastive logits&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;to select tokens that reflect factual consistency.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Example&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: If &quot;Paris&quot; becomes highly probable in later layers but not earlier ones, it is prioritized as a factual answer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;3&lt;/code&gt;&lt;span class=&quot;citation&quot; data-citation=&quot;%7B%22citationItems%22%3A%5B%5D%2C%22properties%22%3A%7B%7D%7D&quot;&gt;()&lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;8&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
    <z:Attachment rdf:about="#item_275">
        <z:itemType>attachment</z:itemType>
