            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>MIND: Unsupervised Real-Time Hallucination Detection based on the Internal States of Large Language Models</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Hallucinations in large language models (LLMs) refer to the phenomenon of LLMs producing responses that are coherent yet factually inaccurate. This issue undermines the effectiveness of LLMs in practical applications, necessitating research into detecting and mitigating hallucinations of LLMs. Previous studies have mainly concentrated on post-processing techniques for hallucination detection, which tend to be computationally intensive and limited in effectiveness due to their separation from the LLM's inference process. To overcome these limitations, we introduce MIND, an unsupervised training framework that leverages the internal states of LLMs for real-time hallucination detection without requiring manual annotations. Additionally, we present HELM, a new benchmark for evaluating hallucination detection across multiple LLMs, featuring diverse LLM outputs and the internal states of LLMs during their inference process. Our experiments demonstrate that MIND outperforms existing state-of-the-art methods in hallucination detection.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-06-10</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2403.06448</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-04-30 14:15:23</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2403.06448 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2403.06448</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2403.06448</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_361">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; showed that the contextualized embedding vectors (= hidden states) of LLMs exhibit discernible differences during hallucination and non-hallucination states. Furthermore, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;utilizing the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;last token’s contextualized embedding of the final layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;is sufficient for hallucination detection. But requires training a Hallucination Detector with outside data. And evaluation dataset is the same as training dataset so this is a bit cheating. SelfCheckGPT-NLI second best method.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;CODE provided&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MIND: an &lt;strong&gt;unsupervised&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;real time&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;reference free, compatible &lt;/strong&gt;(to transformer LLMs)&lt;strong&gt;, lightweight&lt;/strong&gt; training framework that leverages the internal states of LLMs for real-time hallucination detection without requiring manual annotations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Additionally, we present HELM, a new benchmark for evaluating hallucination detection across multiple LLMs, featuring diverse LLM outputs and the internal states of LLMs during their inference process. &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;HELM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;advances LLM evaluation by providing&amp;nbsp;multi-model outputs&amp;nbsp;(texts from 6+ LLMs) with&amp;nbsp;human-annotated hallucination labels, along with&amp;nbsp;internal model snapshots&amp;nbsp;(contextualized embeddings, self-attentions, hidden-layer activations) recorded during inference.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The following methods detect hallucinations &lt;strong&gt;after LLMs finish the inference process,&lt;/strong&gt; we refer to these hallucination detection methods as &lt;strong&gt;post-processing methods&lt;/strong&gt;. (&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;Selfcheckgpt: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models 2023), (Enhancing uncertainty-based hallucination detection with stronger focus 2023), (The internal state of an LLM knows when it's lying 2023) =&amp;gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;extreme computation costs and high latency.&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;Unfortunately, the &lt;strong&gt;post-processing methods are suboptimal&lt;/strong&gt; for the applications of hallucination detection models for LLMs in practice
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The MIND framework consist of two steps:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 1) Automatic/unsupervised training data generation:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;36MAVA9W&quot; width=&quot;1205&quot; height=&quot;402&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
our method aims to create customized training data for training a Hallucination Detection Model for our LLM.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The authors generate hallucination-labeled training data&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;without human annotation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;by:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Input Creation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Select Wikipedia articles (e.g., WikiText-103).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Truncate each article at a&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;random entity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., &quot;Paris&quot;) after the first sentence, producing a partial article&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;w'_i&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;LLM Generation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Feed&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;w'_i&lt;/code&gt;&amp;nbsp;into an LLM (&lt;code&gt;L_i&lt;/code&gt;) to generate a continuation (&lt;code&gt;G_i&lt;/code&gt;), truncated at the first sentence.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Record the LLM’s&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;internal states&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;S_i&lt;/code&gt;: embeddings, attention maps) during generation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Automatic Labeling&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Non-Hallucination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;H_i=0&lt;/code&gt;): If&amp;nbsp;&lt;code&gt;G_i&lt;/code&gt;&amp;nbsp;starts with the correct entity&amp;nbsp;&lt;code&gt;e_i&lt;/code&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., &quot;Paris is...&quot;).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Hallucination&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;H_i=1&lt;/code&gt;): If&amp;nbsp;&lt;code&gt;G_i&lt;/code&gt;&amp;nbsp;omits&amp;nbsp;&lt;code&gt;e_i&lt;/code&gt;&amp;nbsp;or introduces incorrect entities (e.g., &quot;London is...&quot;).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Dataset Construction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Each training example is a tuple&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;code&gt;D_i = (L_i, w_i, G_i, S_i, H_i)&lt;/code&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Combines&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;text pairs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(original vs. generated) with&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;internal state data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;for training a hallucination detector.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 2) Hallucination classifier training.&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3.2.1 Feature Selection&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: Can we detect hallucinations in LLMs using contextualized embeddings? &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;YES, with experiments, the paper found that the contextualized embedding vectors (also named hidden states) of LLMs exhibit discernible differences during hallucination and non-hallucination states. Furthermore, it is noteworthy that an effective distinction can be achieved by merely utilizing the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;last token’s contextualized embedding of the final layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3.2.2 Training Process:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
n represents the dimension of the LLM’s contextualized embedding
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We choose the contextualized embeding of the &lt;strong&gt;last token&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;of last Transformer layer&lt;/strong&gt; as the input of the MIND classifier.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;output P of the hallucination classifier is a binary label (hallucination or not):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;HMHW4DG3&quot; width=&quot;266&quot; height=&quot;26&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We use the BCE loss.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3.3 Real-time Hallucination Detection&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;After training the&amp;nbsp;Hallucination Classifier, the MIND framework enables&amp;nbsp;real-time detection&amp;nbsp;by analyzing the LLM’s&amp;nbsp;contextualized token embeddings&amp;nbsp;during generation. The classifier outputs a&amp;nbsp;hallucination probability&amp;nbsp;for each token, flagging potential inaccuracies as the LLM generates text.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Dataset HELM:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
HELM dataset mostly created from &lt;strong&gt;Wikipedia&lt;/strong&gt; and the hallucination classifier is also trained on Wikipedia so if it achieves good perf on HELM data this is normal =&amp;gt; “&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;cheating&lt;/span&gt;”.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Baselines:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Predictive Probability (PP) &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Selfcheckgpt: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Predictive Entropy (PE) &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Language models (mostly) know what they know 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;SelfChekGPT (SCG) &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Selfcheckgpt: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models 2023) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;SCG employs four distinct techniques each of which evaluates the consistency of responses from the LLM:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SCG_BERTScore
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SCG_QA
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SCG_NLI
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SCG_n-gram.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SAPLMA&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(The internal state of an LLM knows when it's lying 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
EUBHD &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Enhancing uncertainty-based hallucination detection with stronger focus 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
GPT4-HDM &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Halueval: A large-scale hallucination evaluation benchmark for large language models 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(1) MIND demonstrates broad effectiveness in all six LLMs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(2) MIND outperforms existing reference-free hallucination detection methods at both sentence and passage levels.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;(3) SelfCheckGPT-NLI (SCG_NLI) emerged as the second-best hallucination detection method after MIND.&lt;/span&gt; Remarkably, it even surpasses MIND in performance on certain models. However, a critical drawback of SelfCheckGPT is its latency, specifically, the time taken to detect hallucination.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(4) SAPLMA, though effective on its own training dataset, exhibits suboptimal performance when applied to the real-time hallucination. This could be attributed to the model’s overfitting to the specific training data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
