    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://arxiv.org/abs/2311.13230">
        <z:itemType>preprint</z:itemType>
        <dc:publisher>
           <foaf:Organization><foaf:name>arXiv</foaf:name></foaf:Organization>
        </dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Zhang</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Tianhang</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Qiu</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Lin</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Guo</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Qipeng</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Deng</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Cheng</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Zhang</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Yue</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Zhang</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Zheng</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Zhou</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Chenghu</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Wang</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Xinbing</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Fu</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Luoyi</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_365"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_367"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_366"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Artificial Intelligence</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>EUBHD : Enhancing Uncertainty-Based Hallucination Detection with Stronger Focus</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Large Language Models (LLMs) have gained significant popularity for their impressive performance across diverse fields. However, LLMs are prone to hallucinate untruthful or nonsensical outputs that fail to meet user expectations in many real-world applications. Existing works for detecting hallucinations in LLMs either rely on external knowledge for reference retrieval or require sampling multiple responses from the LLM for consistency verification, making these methods costly and inefficient. In this paper, we propose a novel reference-free, uncertainty-based method for detecting hallucinations in LLMs. Our approach imitates human focus in factuality checking from three aspects: 1) focus on the most informative and important keywords in the given text; 2) focus on the unreliable tokens in historical context which may lead to a cascade of hallucinations; and 3) focus on the token properties such as token type and token frequency. Experimental results on relevant datasets demonstrate the effectiveness of our proposed method, which achieves state-of-the-art performance across all the evaluation metrics and eliminates the need for additional information.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-11-22</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2311.13230</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-02 07:48:14</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2311.13230 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2311.13230</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2311.13230</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_365">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Accepted by EMNLP 2023 (main conference)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; oui on peut s’en inspirer. calcule l’uncertainty des mots importants uniquement. Propage l’uncertainty à l’aide du mécanisme d’attention car un peu uncertain peut en créer d’autres en cascades.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;CODE provided&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper proposes a &lt;strong&gt;reference-free, efficient method&lt;/strong&gt; to detect &lt;strong&gt;hallucinations&lt;/strong&gt; (untruthful or nonsensical content) in texts generated by large language models (LLMs), such as GPT-3, &lt;strong&gt;without needing external data or multiple outputs&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Idea: Instead of relying on RAG or multiple model responses, the authors estimate &lt;strong&gt;how “uncertain” a model should be about each token in the generated text&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Idea: Our assumption is that a powerful enough LLM should assign a low probability to tokens that make up hallucinated information, since they deviate from the world knowledge the model has learned during its training stage.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They improve this approach by imitating how &lt;strong&gt;humans focus&lt;/strong&gt; when checking facts — hence the name &lt;em&gt;“Stronger Focus.”&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;How they found the idea:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The example shows how LLMs can&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;confidently generate incorrect information&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(&quot;Mackenzie Caquatto competed at the 2012 Olympics&quot;) despite&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;low initial confidence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;in the critical factual element (&quot;2012&quot;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Incorrect Claim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Generated text states:&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&quot;Mackenzie Caquatto competed at the 2012 Summer Olympics in London.&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Reality&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Mackenzie Caquatto&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;did not compete at the 2012 Olympics&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Probability Analysis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;First &quot;2012&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: The model assigns a&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;low probability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;to &quot;2012&quot; initially (e.g., unsure about the year).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Second &quot;2012&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: The model&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;increases confidence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;when repeating &quot;2012 Summer Olympics&quot; later, despite the factual error.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Attention Mechanism&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Self-Attention Matrix&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;: Shows the model&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;focuses heavily on the first sentence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(blue box) when generating the incorrect claim.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Why This Happens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The model&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;latches onto its own incorrect output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(first sentence) as context, creating a&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;self-reinforcing error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;This leads to&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;overconfidence&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;in the repeated but incorrect claim.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;=&amp;gt;This observation inspired us to introduce a “penalty” for tokens generated with attentions paid to unreliable tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The method detects hallucinations in text generated by a LLM, using a &lt;strong&gt;proxy model&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., LLaMA, OPT) to estimate the &lt;strong&gt;uncertainty&lt;/strong&gt; of each token.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 0: Use a Proxy Language Model&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;In cases where token-level probabilities are inaccessible like GPT-3, they use a &lt;strong&gt;proxy model&lt;/strong&gt; that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Predicts the next token probabilities given a sequence
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Allows access to &lt;strong&gt;attention weights&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;entropy&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;token likelihoods&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This model will &lt;strong&gt;simulate the probability distribution&lt;/strong&gt; over each token to assess how surprising or likely each word is.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Previous work &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Selfcheckgpt: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; has demonstrated the ineffective performance of using a proxy model, but we attribute it to&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;the uncertainty metrics employed. These metrics, such as the average entropy and average loss for all tokens in the sentence, are insufficiently aligned with human evaluation. We believe this issue stems from the inherent disparities in how models and humans perceive and assess information, &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;thus limiting the capability of the uncertainty-based approach for hallucination detection.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Lila: If we have access to next token probability maybe we don’t need a proxy model.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 1: Focus on Informative Keywords&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;entities are the most frequently hallucinated words in text generation. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Understanding factuality in abstractive summarization with FRANK: A benchmark for factuality metrics 2021), (Evaluating the factual consistency of abstractive text summarization 2019)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Goal: Prioritize factual elements like names, dates, places (like a human would do)— where hallucinations are most likely
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;an effective distinction can be achieved by merely utilizing the last token’s contextualized embedding of the final layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;1) Use &lt;strong&gt;SpaCy&lt;/strong&gt; to extract:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Named Entities&lt;/strong&gt;: e.g., PERSON, DATE, GPE, ORG, etc.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Nouns&lt;/strong&gt; not already in the named entity list
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2) Compute&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; token-level hallucination score hi&lt;/span&gt; using:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
pi(ti) is the token probability from the proxy model of generating ti
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Hi is the entropy (uncertainty) of the token distribution
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;EYFI6UEY&quot; width=&quot;243&quot; height=&quot;85&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
3) Aggregate these scores at the &lt;strong&gt;sentence level&lt;/strong&gt;, but only over the &lt;strong&gt;keywords&lt;/strong&gt;:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;M7QTQWY6&quot; width=&quot;347&quot; height=&quot;91&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 2: Focus on Preceding Words (Hallucination Propagation)&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
LLMs can assign high probabilities to hallucinated tokens if the context is misleading (overconfidence).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Hallucinated content &lt;strong&gt;propagates forward&lt;/strong&gt; due to attention mechanisms.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
1) For every keyword token ti, look at its attention to &lt;strong&gt;previous keyword tokens&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2) Compute a &lt;strong&gt;penalty&lt;/strong&gt; pi from preceding tokens:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
atti,j is the attention weight from ti to tj
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;γ∈[0,1] controls how much penalty is propagated&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;Y8LF9Y9Q&quot; width=&quot;236&quot; height=&quot;140&quot;&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;5WAV5VNN&quot; width=&quot;264&quot; height=&quot;73&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This is recursive:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; hallucination score&lt;/span&gt; at token i, &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;h^i​&lt;/span&gt;, includes penalties from previous scores
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Propagation supports &lt;strong&gt;multi-hop&lt;/strong&gt; influence (e.g., hallucinations from early tokens affect later ones)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 3: Focus on Token Properties (Probability Correction)&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Some factual tokens are &lt;strong&gt;underconfident&lt;/strong&gt; due to multiple valid choices
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Rare words tend to get &lt;strong&gt;low probabilities&lt;/strong&gt;, even if they're correct
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two Adjustments:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;a) Entity-Type Constrained Probability&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Insert &lt;strong&gt;entity type prompts&lt;/strong&gt; before named entities: &lt;code&gt;&amp;lt;PERSON&amp;gt; Barack Obama &amp;lt;DATE&amp;gt; 1961&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This nudges the proxy model to narrow its candidate set (like humans would)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The new probability: (Where the candidate set includes tokens matching the entity type (e.g., all plausible dates)&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;V2HIM4QS&quot; width=&quot;254&quot; height=&quot;56&quot;&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;b) &lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;IDF-Based Correction&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Apply &lt;strong&gt;inverse document frequency (IDF)&lt;/strong&gt; from a large corpus (e.g., RedPajama) to re-weight token probabilities:&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;9LXLV79C&quot; width=&quot;224&quot; height=&quot;62&quot;&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This boosts rare but correct tokens, penalizes frequent generic words
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Final Step: Aggregate Scores&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;After all three steps:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use corrected token probabilities p^(t)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Compute hallucination scores for tokens, sentences, and passages
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use these for &lt;strong&gt;binary classification&lt;/strong&gt; (factual vs non-factual) or &lt;strong&gt;ranking&lt;/strong&gt; (for correlation with human judgment)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Datasets:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;WikiBio GPT-3 Dataset&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;XSumFaith&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;FRANK&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Tested on &lt;strong&gt;WikiBio GPT-3&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;XSumFaith&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;FRANK&lt;/strong&gt; datasets
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Outperforms &lt;strong&gt;SelfCheckGPT&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;GPT-3 uncertainty baselines&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Achieves &lt;strong&gt;state-of-the-art&lt;/strong&gt; results in hallucination detection &lt;strong&gt;without additional resources&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Even &lt;strong&gt;smaller models&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., LLaMA-7B) perform competitively using this method
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;BAJI2RU6&quot; width=&quot;830&quot; height=&quot;460&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Compute time:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Table from paper &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(MIND Unsupervised Real-Time Hallucination Detection based on the Internal States of Large Language Models 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;TY2N4D9J&quot; width=&quot;357&quot; height=&quot;206&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;EUBHD = the method of this paper&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The keyword identification and named entity recognition in our approach is based on Spacy, which may introduce some errors as observed in our practice.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A further limitation arises from our assumption that LLM proxies are consistently current with factual knowledge. However, LLMs are not continuously updated post-training, hence they may lack recently emerged factual information.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>INTERESTING</dc:subject>
    </bib:Memo>
