        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_378"/>
        <dc:title>EGH: Embedding and Gradient Say Wrong: A White-Box Method for Hallucination Detection</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>In recent years, large language models (LLMs) have achieved remarkable success in the field of natural language generation. Compared to previous small-scale models, they are capable of generating fluent output based on the provided prefix or prompt. However, one critical challenge — the *hallucination* problem — remains to be resolved. Generally, the community refers to the undetected hallucination scenario where the LLMs generate text unrelated to the input text or facts. In this study, we intend to model the distributional distance between the regular conditional output and the unconditional output, which is generated without a given input text. Based upon Taylor Expansion for this distance at the output probability space, our approach manages to leverage the embedding and first-order gradient information. The resulting approach is plug-and-play that can be easily adapted to any autoregressive LLM. On the hallucination benchmarks HADES and other datasets, our approach achieves state-of-the-art performance.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-11</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>Embedding and Gradient Say Wrong</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2024.emnlp-main.116/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-02 09:56:50</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>1950–1959</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
           <bib:Conference><dc:title>EMNLP 2024</dc:title></bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_379">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; not really relevant because &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;this detection method does nothing for hallucinations where the model doesn't know the information.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; Cannot detect when&amp;nbsp;the content is &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;factually wrong&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; because the model &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;doesn’t know the correct information&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;. Evaluation not comprehensive looks like they discarded models where the method was better.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper introduces a &lt;strong&gt;novel white-box approach&lt;/strong&gt; to detect hallucinations in large language models (LLMs). The method is called &lt;strong&gt;EGH&lt;/strong&gt; — &lt;strong&gt;Embedding and Gradient-based Hallucination detection&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Idea: &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;LLMs hallucinate when their output &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;isn't grounded in the input&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; (e.g., the answer doesn’t depend on the question)&lt;/span&gt;. This method tries to measure: How much does the output actually depend on the input? If the dependence is weak, the answer is likely hallucinated.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Existing hallucination detection methods mostly perform hallucination detection at the token level. However, when the model loses much of the internal information while outputting tokens, it undoubtedly significantly increases the difficulty of hallucination detection &lt;/span&gt;=&amp;gt; &lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;When generating tokens sequentially, LLMs&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;discard or compress internal states&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., attention maps, hidden-layer activations) from earlier tokens. This loss of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;token-level internal data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;makes it harder to trace how hallucinations emerge during generation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Related work:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Some methods utilize uncertainty metrics at the token level to determine hallucination &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Bartscore: Evaluating generated text as text generation 2021), (Gptscore: Evaluate as you desire 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;ZXIMTU4T&quot; width=&quot;769&quot; height=&quot;245&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 1: Two Input Modes&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For a given prompt (like a question &lt;code&gt;Q&lt;/code&gt;) and its model-generated answer &lt;code&gt;A&lt;/code&gt;, they create two versions:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Conditional input&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;[Q, A]&lt;/code&gt; → normal generation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unconditional input&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;code&gt;[0s, A]&lt;/code&gt; → same answer &lt;code&gt;A&lt;/code&gt;, but no question (zero-padded)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Then, they compare the model’s internal behavior under both inputs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 2: Extract Two White-Box Features&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;1) Embedding Difference (E):&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They extract embeddings for &lt;code&gt;A&lt;/code&gt; in both cases:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;E(A|Q)&lt;/code&gt; = embeddings with the real question
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;E(A|0)&lt;/code&gt; = embeddings with zero-padding (no real question)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Then compute: E = E(A ∣ Q)−E( A ∣ 0)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This captures &lt;strong&gt;how the presence of Q affects internal token representations&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2) Gradient (G)&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They compute the &lt;strong&gt;KL divergence&lt;/strong&gt; between:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;P(A|Q)&lt;/code&gt; — the probability of &lt;code&gt;A&lt;/code&gt; when Q is present
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;P(A|0)&lt;/code&gt; — the probability of &lt;code&gt;A&lt;/code&gt; without Q
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Then, backpropagate the gradient of this divergence &lt;strong&gt;through the embedding layer&lt;/strong&gt;: G=∇D([0,A])
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This tells us &lt;strong&gt;how sensitive the model is to the input&lt;/strong&gt; when it’s not there — the less sensitive, the more likely the output is hallucinated.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 3: Combine Features and Classify&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They combine the two signals: y_hal=f(λE+(1−λ)G)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
f is a small neural network (MLP)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
λ is a tunable weight (default 0.8)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The result is a &lt;strong&gt;hallucination label&lt;/strong&gt;: 1 = hallucinated, 0 = not.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;W8Z96M93&quot; width=&quot;853&quot; height=&quot;452&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;u&gt;Datasets:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;HaluEval&lt;/strong&gt; (QA, dialogue, summarization)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SelfCheckGPT&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;HADES&lt;/strong&gt; (token-level hallucination detection)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;EGH outperforms all baselines&lt;/strong&gt;, including both:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Black-box&lt;/strong&gt; methods (e.g., prompting ChatGPT to self-check)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Other white-box&lt;/strong&gt; methods like SAPLMA
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Achieves &lt;strong&gt;97% accuracy&lt;/strong&gt; in QA hallucination detection
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Generalizes&lt;/strong&gt; well even when trained on one dataset and tested on another
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hallucinations often happen when the model &lt;strong&gt;generates text that isn’t grounded in the prompt&lt;/strong&gt;. By comparing conditional vs unconditional behavior internally, EGH can &lt;strong&gt;catch these disconnections&lt;/strong&gt; — even if the text sounds fluent.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;2D7JCMDG&quot; width=&quot;822&quot; height=&quot;394&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Requires &lt;strong&gt;white-box access&lt;/strong&gt; (gradients, embeddings) → not applicable to closed models like GPT-4 via API
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Slower&lt;/strong&gt; than black-box methods due to gradient computation
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;No external knowledge&lt;/strong&gt; used — so it struggles with fact-checking against the real world
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;this detection method does nothing for hallucinations where the model doesn't know the information.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Cannot detect when &amp;nbsp;the content is &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;factually wrong&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; because the model &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;doesn’t know the correct information&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
