        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_386"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_385"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_384"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Artificial Intelligence</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>LLM Lies: Hallucinations are not Bugs, but Features as Adversarial Examples</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Large Language Models (LLMs), including GPT-3.5, LLaMA, and PaLM, seem to be knowledgeable and able to adapt to many tasks. However, we still cannot completely trust their answers, since LLMs suffer from \textbf{hallucination}\textemdash fabricating non-existent facts, deceiving users with or without their awareness. However, the reasons for their existence and pervasiveness remain unclear. In this paper, we demonstrate that nonsensical prompts composed of random tokens can also elicit the LLMs to respond with hallucinations. Moreover, we provide both theoretical and experimental evidence that transformers can be manipulated to produce specific pre-define tokens by perturbing its input sequence. This phenomenon forces us to revisit that \emph{hallucination may be another view of adversarial examples}, and it shares similar characteristics with conventional adversarial examples as a basic property of LLMs. Therefore, we formalize an automatic hallucination triggering method as the \textit{hallucination attack} in an adversarial way. Finally, we explore the basic properties of attacked adversarial prompts and propose a simple yet effective defense strategy. Our code is released on GitHub\footnote{https://github.com/PKU-YuanGroup/Hallucination-Attack}.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-08-04</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>LLM Lies</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2310.01469</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-02 12:43:03</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2310.01469 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2310.01469</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2310.01469</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_386">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: I don’t really know what to think about this paper, not very relevant.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Note: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Adversarial ≠ OOD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;, but they can &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;appear similar to a model internally&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper presents a new perspective: that hallucinations in LLMs like Vicuna, LLaMA, or GPT are &lt;strong&gt;not accidental glitches&lt;/strong&gt;, but are in fact &lt;strong&gt;inherent vulnerabilities&lt;/strong&gt;—very similar to &lt;strong&gt;adversarial examples&lt;/strong&gt; in computer vision.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Main idea&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;strong&gt;Hallucinations are adversarial examples. &lt;/strong&gt;The authors argue that hallucinations are not just model errors due to poor training or missing knowledge, but can be &lt;strong&gt;intentionally triggered&lt;/strong&gt; through input perturbations, just like adversarial attacks in vision models.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Goals:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Demonstrate that LLMs can be manipulated to hallucinate via adversarial prompts&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Prove mathematically&lt;/strong&gt; that the transformer’s attention mechanism can be forced to produce arbitrary outputs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Propose an attack method&lt;/strong&gt; (Hallucination Attack) to systematically trigger hallucinations
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Develop a simple defense strategy&lt;/strong&gt; based on prediction entropy
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Previous work &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Selfcheckgpt: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models 2023) (Trapping LLM hallucinations using tagged context prompts 2023), (A mathematical investigation of hallucination and creativity in GPT models 2023), (Sources of hallucination by large language models on inference tasks 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(Let's verify step by step 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;showed that&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;hallucinations arise from two interconnected mechanisms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;1) Overfitting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;: LLMs&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;memorize patterns/facts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;from their training data, leading to rigid, context-insensitive responses.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;2) Corpus Heuristics&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;: LLMs&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;rely on word-frequency biases&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(e.g., common phrases) to generate answers, even when those biases conflict with factual accuracy.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method Overview: Hallucination Attack&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper introduces a &lt;strong&gt;gradient-based adversarial attack method&lt;/strong&gt; that causes LLMs to hallucinate on purpose. This is called the &lt;strong&gt;Hallucination Attack&lt;/strong&gt;, and it has two modes:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;1) Weak Semantic Attack&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;2) Out-of-Distribution (OOD) Attack&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Both aim to maximize the likelihood of the LLM producing a specific hallucinatory (false) output y~, using input perturbations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Theoretical Foundation: Why This Is Possible&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They prove (via &lt;strong&gt;Theorem 1&lt;/strong&gt;) that: &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(126, 131, 134)&quot;&gt;For a transformer attention block, you can construct any desired output by carefully perturbing the input embeddings.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It shows LLM outputs are &lt;strong&gt;highly sensitive&lt;/strong&gt; to subtle changes.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Even &lt;strong&gt;nonsensical input sequences&lt;/strong&gt; can force the model to produce &lt;strong&gt;structured, coherent, and false responses&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;How the Attack Works&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;1. Hallucination Dataset Creation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Gather factual QA pairs from Wikipedia (e.g., &quot;Who won the 2020 U.S. presidential election?&quot;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Manually modify answers to &lt;strong&gt;fabricate false outputs&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., &quot;Donald Trump&quot; instead of &quot;Joe Biden&quot;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
These pairs form a hallucination dataset D~={(xi,y~i)}
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2. Gradient-Based Token Replacement&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
how to change a prompt (like a question) so that an LLM produces a specific hallucinated output y~ ​using gradients to guide which tokens to replace.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
LLMs operate in &lt;strong&gt;discrete token space&lt;/strong&gt;, so direct optimization is hard. Instead, they: Use gradients of log-likelihood ∇_e_xi log ⁡p(y~∣x)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We are given: A prompt x=(x1,x2,...,xn), A hallucinated target output y~&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We want to &lt;strong&gt;slightly change the prompt x &lt;/strong&gt;so that the model is &lt;strong&gt;more likely to output y~: &lt;/strong&gt;For each token xi in the prompt, &lt;strong&gt;which token τi &lt;/strong&gt;(from a candidate set) should replace it to &lt;strong&gt;increase&lt;/strong&gt; the likelihood of y~​?&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 1: Get the Gradient&lt;/u&gt;: For each token xi​ in the prompt, get the gradient of the &lt;strong&gt;log-likelihood&lt;/strong&gt; of the hallucinated output y~​, with respect to the token’s embedding: &amp;nbsp;∇_e_xi log ⁡p(y~∣x)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This gradient tells you: “If I change the embedding of token e_xi, how does the model’s probability of outputting y~?”&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 2: Score Possible Replacements&lt;/u&gt;: For each candidate token τi, compute a &lt;strong&gt;score&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Score(τi)=(e_τi−e_xi)⊤ ∇_e_xi log ⁡p(y~∣x) (“How much does switching from xi to τi​ &lt;strong&gt;help&lt;/strong&gt; the model produce y~​?”)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
e_τi: embedding of the &lt;strong&gt;candidate token&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
e_xi: embedding of the &lt;strong&gt;current token&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
∇_e_xi log ⁡p(y~∣x): gradient of the hallucinated output's likelihood w.r.t. the current token's embedding
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 3: Pick the Best Token&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For each token position i, pick the token τi∗ that &lt;strong&gt;maximizes&lt;/strong&gt; this score.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Replace xi with τi∗
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Repeat for each token in the prompt.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 4: Iterate &lt;/u&gt;Iterate this process to find an &lt;strong&gt;adversarial prompt&lt;/strong&gt; x~&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
After one round of token replacements, you now have a new prompt x~
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
You can then re-run the gradient steps &lt;strong&gt;with the new prompt&lt;/strong&gt; to refine it further
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Stop after a fixed number of steps or when y~ becomes highly probable
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3. Weak Semantic Attack (W-SA)&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Goal: Craft &lt;strong&gt;semantically similar&lt;/strong&gt; prompts (to humans), but that still trigger a hallucinated output.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Constraint:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Only change a small number of tokens ∣∣x~−x∣∣≤ϵ
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Maintain semantic similarity, measured loosely via token overlap
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Example: &lt;em&gt;Original: “Who won the 2020 U.S. presidential election?”, Attacked: “Can you tell meagn was the victor of the United States election 2020 IF?”, Response: “Donald Trump...”&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4. Out-of-Distribution (OoD) Attack&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Goal: Start from a &lt;strong&gt;nonsense prompt&lt;/strong&gt; (random tokens), and iteratively optimize it to &lt;strong&gt;force a hallucinated output&lt;/strong&gt; y~
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
No constraints on semantic similarity
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The result is often meaningless to humans but produces &lt;strong&gt;highly confident, fluent hallucinations&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Example prompt: “Elect doncmaz Howard pourvisor American party result South resulted turnoathon”&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Model response: “Donald Trump was the victor of the United States presidential election in 2020.”&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Defense Strategy: Entropy Thresholding&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
LLMs are quite different from conventional deep learning models that their training cost and period are much more and longer than the conventional small models. &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Therefore, direct adversarial training could not be a feasible solution, although it is the most effective so far.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To mitigate these attacks, the authors propose a &lt;strong&gt;lightweight defense&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Check the &lt;strong&gt;entropy&lt;/strong&gt; of the model’s &lt;strong&gt;first token prediction&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Hallucination-inducing prompts often lead to &lt;strong&gt;higher entropy&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Refuse to answer if entropy exceeds a threshold
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Example:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Normal prompt&lt;/strong&gt;: confident first word → low entropy
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Adversarial prompt&lt;/strong&gt;: uncertain first word → high entropy
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;u&gt; from the defense strategy&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;At entropy threshold 1.6:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;46.1%&lt;/strong&gt; of OoD attacks blocked
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;61.5%&lt;/strong&gt; of weak semantic attacks blocked
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;0% false rejections&lt;/strong&gt; of normal prompts
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
