        <dc:title>Knowing What LLMs DO NOT Know: A Simple Yet Effective Self-Detection Method</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown great potential in Natural Language Processing (NLP) tasks. However, recent literature reveals that LLMs generate nonfactual responses intermittently, which impedes the LLMs' reliability for further utilization. In this paper, we propose a novel self-detection method to detect which questions that a LLM does not know that are prone to generate nonfactual results. Specifically, we first diversify the textual expressions for a given question and collect the corresponding answers. Then we examine the divergencies between the generated answers to identify the questions that the model may generate falsehoods. All of the above steps can be accomplished by prompting the LLMs themselves without referring to any other external resources. We conduct comprehensive experiments and demonstrate the effectiveness of our method on recently released LLMs, e.g., Vicuna, ChatGPT, and GPT-4.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-03-21</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>Knowing What LLMs DO NOT Know</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2310.17918</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-02 14:53:34</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2310.17918 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2310.17918</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2310.17918</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_393">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: Accepted by NAACL 2024&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: article intéressant, méthode qui semble fonctionner pas mal. On peut peut_être s’en inspirer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This paper tackles a key challenge: LLMs &lt;strong&gt;don’t know when they don’t know&lt;/strong&gt; — they often generate confident but incorrect (hallucinated) answers.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Detecting non-factuality&lt;/strong&gt; as that a model &lt;strong&gt;does not know&lt;/strong&gt; which knowledge is related to the question or does not understand the queried question, outputting the nonfactual response.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We propose a novel self-detection method to detect which questions an LLM does not know.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The authors propose a &lt;strong&gt;self-detection method&lt;/strong&gt; to identify such cases using &lt;strong&gt;only the LLM itself&lt;/strong&gt;, without relying on external tools, retraining, or ground-truth databases.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Why this approach?&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;We attribute the non-factuality of an LLM to the generative characteristics which sample the most possible tokens sequentially. It means even if the LLM does not know the exact knowledge related to the question or even does not understand the question, it still generates plausible responses.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Consequently, if an LLM returns contradicting responses to the semantically equivalent questions, the LLM does not know the knowledge for the question. Besides, if the textual verbalization of a question is not representative in the LLM, i.e., atypical, it would be hard to understand resulting in a lower-quality response&lt;/em&gt; &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;EQLBTE44&quot; width=&quot;788&quot; height=&quot;234&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Goal&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: Detect &lt;strong&gt;non-factual (hallucinated)&lt;/strong&gt; responses by identifying:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The proposed method combines &lt;strong&gt;two components&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;1) Consistency-Based Detection: &lt;/strong&gt;Does the model give &lt;strong&gt;consistent answers&lt;/strong&gt; to differently phrased versions of the same question?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;2) Verbalization-Based Detection: &lt;/strong&gt;Is the phrasing of the question &lt;strong&gt;typical&lt;/strong&gt; (i.e., familiar and likely well-understood by the model)? how familiar the LLM is with the way it's written
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The intuition: If the LLM gives &lt;strong&gt;unstable&lt;/strong&gt; answers to reworded versions of a question, or if the question is &lt;strong&gt;strangely worded or rare&lt;/strong&gt;, the model likely doesn’t know the correct answer.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Component 1: Consistency-Based Detection&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 1: Generate Rephrasings&lt;/u&gt;: &lt;/em&gt;Given a question q, generate multiple &lt;strong&gt;semantically equivalent versions&lt;/strong&gt; Q(q)={q1,q2,...,qn} using:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;LLM prompting&lt;/strong&gt; for open-domain questions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rule-based rewording&lt;/strong&gt; for math/logic tasks
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 2: Generate Responses&lt;/u&gt;: &lt;/em&gt;Ask the LLM to answer each qi using &lt;strong&gt;greedy decoding&lt;/strong&gt; (no randomness).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 3: Measure Consistenc&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;u&gt;y&lt;/u&gt;: For each pair of answers (ri,rj) check if they are &lt;strong&gt;semantically consistent&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use string matching for exact answers (e.g., multiple-choice)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use the LLM itself to evaluate answer similarity (e.g., “Same” or “Contradicted”)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Metrics&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;Consistency Score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: How often rewordings lead to the same answer&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For each question q, the model generates a set of &lt;strong&gt;paraphrased versions&lt;/strong&gt; {q1,q2,...,qn}
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The model is then asked each version, and returns answers {a1,a2,...,an}.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;strong&gt;consistency score&lt;/strong&gt; is computed as the &lt;strong&gt;fraction of answers that agree&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;Entropy Score (=another “consistency score”)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: Measures the spread/diversity of clustered answers&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
high entropy = More diverse = more uncertainty = more likely the model doesn’t know
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
From the paraphrased questions, collect the &lt;strong&gt;set of answers&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Cluster &lt;strong&gt;similar answers&lt;/strong&gt; together (using the &lt;strong&gt;consistency score&lt;/strong&gt; previously calculated).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Compute the &lt;strong&gt;empirical probability distribution&lt;/strong&gt; over the clusters.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use the entropy formula: &amp;nbsp;H(p)=−∑_i pi log(⁡pi) where pi is the proportion of responses in cluster i.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Component 2: Verbalization-Based Detection&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This measures how &quot;typical&quot; the question is — how &lt;strong&gt;familiar&lt;/strong&gt; the LLM is with the phrasing.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;Atypically score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Calculate the &lt;strong&gt;negative log-likelihood (NLL)&lt;/strong&gt; of the question under the model:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;NLL = A(q)=−∑_t=1^n log⁡ P(xt ∣ x&amp;lt;t)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
If NLL is &lt;strong&gt;low&lt;/strong&gt; → the sequence is &lt;strong&gt;expected&lt;/strong&gt; by the model (familiar).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
If NLL is &lt;strong&gt;high&lt;/strong&gt; → the sequence is &lt;strong&gt;unexpected&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;rare&lt;/strong&gt; under the model's learned distribution.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
P(xt ​∣ x&amp;lt;t​) is the probability assigned by the model to token xt​ given its preceding tokens. This is exactly what LLMs compute during generation — it's a natural metric for them.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;Normalize atypically score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: Normalize it by question length N(q), So longer questions don't get penalized just because they have more tokens: &amp;nbsp;Atypicality=A(q) / N(q)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Intuition:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Low atypicality&lt;/strong&gt; → familiar phrasing → model more likely to know the answer
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;High atypicality&lt;/strong&gt; → unfamiliar question → more chance of hallucination
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Note&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; (Lila): &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;NLL&lt;/strong&gt; is related but different than &lt;strong&gt;Uncertainty Metrics:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Uncertainty Metrics&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt; measure how &lt;strong&gt;confident&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;uncertain&lt;/strong&gt; the model is about its predictions. Common examples:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Entropy&lt;/strong&gt; of the output distribution: H(p)=−∑_i pi log(⁡pi)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Higher entropy = more uncertainty
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
pi = The probability assigned by the model to the i-th possible output token.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Final Detection Score&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The final detection score is a &lt;strong&gt;linear&lt;/strong&gt; combination of:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;Entropy of response clusters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;Normalized atypicality score&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Datasets&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Factoid QA&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., FaVIQ, ComQA)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Commonsense Reasoning&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., ARC-challenge, CommonsenseQA)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Arithmetic Reasoning&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., GSM-8K, SVAMP)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Models&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
GPT-4
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ChatGPT (GPT-3.5)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Vicuna-13B
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
LLaMA2-13B
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For each question in the datasets, we have a &lt;strong&gt;golden answer&lt;/strong&gt;. 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For &lt;strong&gt;factoid question&lt;/strong&gt; answering tasks, we &lt;strong&gt;prompt GPT-4 to verify the correctness of the response&lt;/strong&gt; by comparing it with the golden answer similar to what we described before. 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For arithmetic and commonsense reasoning questions, we check whether the final answer exactly matches the golden answer, while the final answer is extracted using &lt;strong&gt;regular expressions&lt;/strong&gt;. If the extraction fails, &lt;strong&gt;we prompt GPT-4&lt;/strong&gt; to assess whether the answer is correct.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Baselines&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
1). &lt;strong&gt;Token-level probability&lt;/strong&gt; (TokenProbs), proposed in&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(Selfcheckgpt: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;, measures the response’s likelihood and the average of the token probabilities is used as the confidence score; 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
2). &lt;strong&gt;Perplexity&lt;/strong&gt;, the reciprocal of the (normalized) language model probability, is used to indicate the uncertainty &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(Prompting gpt-3 to be reliable 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;; 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
3). &lt;strong&gt;Self-consistency of answers&lt;/strong&gt; (ConsistAnswers) is calculated as the consistency of the sampled answers while the answers are sampled using a high-temperature value (0.7) leading to 10 different predictions &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(Prompting gpt-3 to be reliable 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
4). &lt;strong&gt;SelfCheckGPT&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Selfcheckgpt: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; combines the averages of the main response’s BERTScore with the most similar sentence of each drawn sample and the token-level probability.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;HGASYRKK&quot; width=&quot;646&quot; height=&quot;518&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Self-Detection &lt;strong&gt;outperformed&lt;/strong&gt; other methods like:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SelfCheckGPT
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Token probability
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Perplexity-based methods
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Achieved higher PR-AUC in most datasets (especially in &lt;strong&gt;commonsense&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;math reasoning&lt;/strong&gt;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;We cannot compare PR-AUC scores across models because the underlying ground-truth labels (what counts as “known” or “unknown”) differ from model to model. Only comparisons within the same model are valid, since only then are the ground-truth definitions and class balances the same.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;GIR9AVG5&quot; width=&quot;373&quot; height=&quot;457&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Ensembling the methods also improves performance. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Other results:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
the LLM has &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;relatively poorer memorization of unpopular knowledge.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For arithmetic reasoning and commonsense reasoning questions,&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; if the solution requires more reasoning steps,&lt;/span&gt; or&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; contains different arithmetic operations simultaneously&lt;/span&gt;, the model tends to confuse the order of operations. This leads to&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; incorrect answers&lt;/span&gt;. 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; commonsense reasoning tasks,&lt;/span&gt; if the &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;solution requires two or more reasoning steps, the model is more likely to make mistakes.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
the &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;unknown questions correlate with a higher score, i.e., higher atypicality.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;When they increase the number of paraphrased questions (from 10 to 20 to 30), the model’s performance improves a little. Why? If you only ask a few paraphrased questions, sometimes the model might get the right answer by chance, even if it doesn’t really know the answer.&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Ablation Study&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Removing &lt;strong&gt;consistency&lt;/strong&gt; drops performance more than removing &lt;strong&gt;atypicality&lt;/strong&gt; — meaning answer divergence is most important.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Combining their method with prior tools like &lt;strong&gt;SelfCheckGPT&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;TokenProbs&lt;/strong&gt; yields even stronger results.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Related work:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Language models (mostly) know what they know 2022&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Prompting gpt-3 to be reliable 2023&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Self-consistency improves chain of thought reasoning in language models 2022&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Selfcheckgpt: Zero-resource black-box hallucination detection for generative large language models 2023&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Semantic uncertainty: Linguistic invariances for uncertainty estimation in natural language generation 2023&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;=&amp;gt; However, the performance of these works is &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;limited&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; as LLMs tend to be &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;overconfident&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;about their own outputs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; and these work would be less effective after the models are trained more aligned.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The current paper is a unified version of these papers:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Can llms express their uncertainty? an empirical evaluation of confidence elicitation in llms 2023&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;SAC3: Reliable hallucination detection in black-box language models via semantic-aware cross-check consistency 2023&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Lm vs lm: Detecting factual errors via cross examination 2023&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Only rely on the model itself. 
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
If a model gives a &lt;strong&gt;consistently wrong&lt;/strong&gt; answer, this method won’t detect it.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Relies on &lt;strong&gt;paraphrase generation&lt;/strong&gt;, which can be limited by the model’s own abilities.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Doesn’t use external facts (so cannot check truth directly, only infer knowledge gaps)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
