        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_401"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_400"/>
        <dc:title>The Curious Case of Hallucinatory (Un)answerability: Finding Truths in the Hidden States of Over-Confident Large Language Models</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Large language models (LLMs) have been shown to possess impressive capabilities, while also raising crucial concerns about the faithfulness of their responses. A primary issue arising in this context is the management of (un)answerable queries by LLMs, which often results in hallucinatory behavior due to overconfidence. In this paper, we explore the behavior of LLMs when presented with (un)answerable queries. We ask: do models represent the fact that the question is (un)answerable when generating a hallucinatory answer? Our results show strong indications that such models encode the answerability of an input query, with the representation of the first decoded token often being a strong indicator. These findings shed new light on the spatial organization within the latent representations of LLMs, unveiling previously unexplored facets of these models. Moreover, they pave the way for the development of improved decoding techniques with better adherence to factual generation, particularly in scenarios where query (un)answerability is a concern.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-12</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>The Curious Case of Hallucinatory (Un)answerability</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2023.emnlp-main.220/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-05 08:29:05</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>3607–3625</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
           <bib:Conference><dc:title>EMNLP 2023</dc:title></bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_401">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: article super intéressant. On peut s’en inspirer je pense.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;CODE provided&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;One of the settings in which LLMs are notoriously prone to hallucinate is when presented with (un)answerable questions&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The paper investigates the question:&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Do large language models (LLMs) “know” when a question is unanswerable — even if they still hallucinate an answer?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The authors argue: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Yes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, LLMs often &lt;strong&gt;encode the (un)answerability of questions internally&lt;/strong&gt;, even if they outwardly respond with overconfident and incorrect (hallucinated) answers.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They propose a multi-part method to:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Detect&lt;/strong&gt; when LLMs face (un)answerable questions
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Reveal&lt;/strong&gt; internal representations related to this capability
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Mitigate hallucinations&lt;/strong&gt; through prompt design and decoding changes
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method Overview&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper uses &lt;strong&gt;three main strategies&lt;/strong&gt; to uncover and leverage this hidden answerability signal:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
1. Prompt Manipulation: &lt;em&gt;Can we coax the model to acknowledge that a question might be unanswerable?&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
2. Beam Relaxation: &lt;em&gt;Even if the top prediction is wrong, is a correct “unanswerable” answer hiding in the beam?&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
3. Probing and Subspace Erasure: &lt;em&gt;Can we &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;decode&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; (un)answerability directly from hidden states? And does erasing that representation change behavior?&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;1. Prompt Manipulation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The idea: &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Explicitly suggest to the model that some questions may be unanswerable.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two prompt variants:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Regular Prompt&lt;/strong&gt;: &quot;Answer the following question based on the context.&quot;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hint Prompt&lt;/strong&gt;: Adds an instruction like “If the question can’t be answered, reply 'unanswerable'.”
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Few-shot version&lt;/strong&gt;: In-context examples also include one (un)answerable query.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Prompting with a “hint” massively boosts model ability to say &quot;I don’t know&quot; when appropriate (up to +80 F1 points on some datasets).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;It also &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;improves QA accuracy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; on real answerable questions.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2. Beam Relaxation&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Beam search normally selects the &lt;strong&gt;top-scoring output&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;But: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Maybe the model produces a better answer deeper in the beam — e.g., “I don’t know” — but it's not ranked first&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So they propose:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Decode with &lt;strong&gt;larger beam sizes&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Search within the beam&lt;/strong&gt; for a reply that admits unanswerability (e.g., &quot;Answer not in context&quot;, &quot;IDK&quot;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
If found, replace the hallucinated top response with the more appropriate one.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Beam search often &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;contains a correct abstention&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; somewhere in the list.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;The more the beam size increases, the more the detection is improved&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Suggests models internally “know” the question is hard — they just prefer to bluff.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;This observation underscores the notion that beneath the facade of overconfidence expressed by these models, the models do encode their inability to respond to certain queries. Importantly, we find that this approach has very little negative impact on the answerable questions, with only a slight degradation in the exact match and F1 scores&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;54CILZ6R&quot; width=&quot;323&quot; height=&quot;431&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;N5Q3WL85&quot; width=&quot;417&quot; height=&quot;388&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3. Probing the Answerability Subspace&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This is the most novel and technical part. Goal:&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Can we extract the answerability signal from the LLM’s hidden states?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Steps:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;a. Linear Probe&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (very simple classifier)&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Is there a &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;linearly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; separable signal in the LLM’s hidden activations that corresponds to the answerability of the question?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Train a &lt;strong&gt;linear classifier (logistic regression)&lt;/strong&gt; to predict whether a question is (un)answerable from the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;hidden state of the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;first&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; generated token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt; More specifically:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Run the LLM &amp;nbsp;on QA inputs with format: &lt;strong&gt;[question + context] → answer&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;They also have ground trough answers labeled with yi = Answerable (ground-truth answer is present in the context) / Unanswerable (no correct answer in the context). These are specific datasets where questions are unanswerable and cannot be addressed from the given context&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
After prompting the model, record the &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;hidden state hi vector at the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;FIRST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;output token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;em&gt;Why the first token&lt;/em&gt;? T&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;his vector captures the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;initial generation decision&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; and reflects how the model interprets the input. It's compact and easy to compare across examples. &lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Lila: Other papers &amp;nbsp;generally use the last generated token - &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;final hidden state)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Using the hidden vectors as features, train a &lt;strong&gt;linear probe&lt;/strong&gt; to predict yi. The classifier learns:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;y^=sigmoid(w⊤h+b).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; Achieves &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&amp;gt;85% F1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; — showing that answerability is &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;linearly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; encoded/separable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; in the final-layer representation.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;b. Cross-dataset Generalization&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Probes trained on one dataset (e.g., SQuAD) work well on others (e.g., NQ, MuSiQue): The existence of the answerability subspace is largely independent of the specific QA dataset used.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Suggests the model encodes &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;generalizable abstract answerability knowledge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
While performance deteriorates, the F1 scores are still well above the F1 scores we calculated over the uncontextualized first layer (baseline).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;c. Visualization&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Apply &lt;strong&gt;PCA&lt;/strong&gt; to visualize the hidden representations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Answerable vs. unanswerable questions form &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;separable clusters&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Even hallucinated unanswerables (pink dots) tend to lie &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;between&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; the two classes — indicating internal uncertainty.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;it can be observed that (un)answerable queries, which were correctly identified as such by the&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;model (red dots), are distinctly separate from the answerable queries (blue dots). This separation becomes especially pronounced in the context where the prompt incorporated a hint.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Importantly, we find that (un)answerable questions, which the models failed to recognize as such and instead generated a hallucinated response (pink dots), appear to reside within a separate linear subspace&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;This finding demonstrates that, notwithstanding the overconfidence exhibited by these models, they intrinsically possess the capacity to distinguish (un)answerable queries. This intrinsic capability is particularly evident given that the subspace corresponding to hallucinated (un)answerable questions (pink) seems to be positioned between that of the answerable queries (blue) and that of correctly identified (un)answerable queries (red). This positioning is suggestive of the models’ inherent uncertainty.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;97P46KVS&quot; width=&quot;493&quot; height=&quot;515&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;2X36ECQF&quot; width=&quot;536&quot; height=&quot;320&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;X7N7UTDE&quot; width=&quot;406&quot; height=&quot;224&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;4. Erasing the Subspace&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Goal: &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Test if the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;(un)answerability information in the hidden states&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; of the model is actually &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;used by the model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; to make decisions (like abstaining), or is it just &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;passively present&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;To test &lt;strong&gt;causality&lt;/strong&gt;, they apply &lt;strong&gt;concept erasure&lt;/strong&gt;: a technique to &lt;strong&gt;erase&lt;/strong&gt; that information from the model’s hidden states and then observe how the model’s behavior changes.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Use &lt;strong&gt;LEACE&lt;/strong&gt; (a linear erasure method) to remove the answerability signal from the hidden states.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;What Is a “Subspace”&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;? When you train a &lt;strong&gt;linear probe&lt;/strong&gt;, you find a &lt;strong&gt;direction in hidden state space&lt;/strong&gt; that separates two classes (e.g., answerable vs. unanswerable). This direction defines a &lt;strong&gt;subspace&lt;/strong&gt; along which answerability is encoded.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;What Is Concept Erasure? &lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;“Erasing” means &lt;strong&gt;removing&lt;/strong&gt; the component of the hidden state that lies in the subspace that encodes the concept. After erasure, the hidden state &lt;strong&gt;no longer contains that information&lt;/strong&gt;. This technique is used to study whether a model’s behavior &lt;strong&gt;depends on that concept&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Then re-run decoding.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;After erasure:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;QA performance and abstention detection drop sharply.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Proves that the subspace &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;is behaviorally relevant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; — not just a coincidental encoding.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;This shows that the model isn’t just passively encoding answerability — it’s actually &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;using&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; the hidden sates when deciding whether to answer. &lt;/span&gt;This is important because &lt;strong&gt;many feature-probing studies don’t show causality&lt;/strong&gt; — just correlation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Related work:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(78, 179, 28)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(170, 170, 170, 0.5)&quot;&gt;The related work cited in this paper is quite different from the related work of other papers on hallucination. I put the interesting related paper in the section “Hall - internal structure Transformers”&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Datasets&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SQuAD v2.0&lt;/strong&gt; — classic reading comprehension with unanswerable questions.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Natural Questions (NQ)&lt;/strong&gt; — real-world search queries.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;MuSiQue&lt;/strong&gt; — multi-hop QA with intentionally unanswerable cases.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;7NR4RQ27&quot; width=&quot;424&quot; height=&quot;169&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;SL73KMHV&quot; width=&quot;648&quot; height=&quot;529&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They do not examine the relation between the ability to encode (un)answerability and model scale.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Our focus on linear probing and linear erasure stems from the availability of existing methods from this family, but deep LMs are highly nonlinear and may encode the information we are interested in in a nonlinear manner. As such, our results should only be interpreted as a lower bound for the identification of (un)answerability.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Lastly, our experiments focused on (un)answerability in a given context. Future work should also explore the phenomenon in the open-domain setting.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Lila&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;: &amp;nbsp;the LLM can still know the answer even if the answer is not in the context provided in the question?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>INTERESTING</dc:subject>
    </bib:Memo>
