    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://arxiv.org/abs/2303.09435">
        <z:itemType>preprint</z:itemType>
        <dc:publisher>
           <foaf:Organization><foaf:name>arXiv</foaf:name></foaf:Organization>
        </dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Din</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Alexander Yom</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Karidi</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Taelin</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Choshen</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Leshem</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Geva</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Mor</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_423"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_422"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_421"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Jump to Conclusions: Short-Cutting Transformers With Linear Transformations</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Transformer-based language models create hidden representations of their inputs at every layer, but only use final-layer representations for prediction. This obscures the internal decision-making process of the model and the utility of its intermediate representations. One way to elucidate this is to cast the hidden representations as final representations, bypassing the transformer computation in-between. In this work, we suggest a simple method for such casting, using linear transformations. This approximation far exceeds the prevailing practice of inspecting hidden representations from all layers, in the space of the final layer. Moreover, in the context of language modeling, our method produces more accurate predictions from hidden layers, across various model scales, architectures, and data distributions. This allows &quot;peeking&quot; into intermediate representations, showing that GPT-2 and BERT often predict the final output already in early layers. We then demonstrate the practicality of our method to recent early exit strategies, showing that when aiming, for example, at retention of 95% accuracy, our approach saves additional 7.9% layers for GPT-2 and 5.4% layers for BERT. Last, we extend our method to linearly approximate sub-modules, finding that attention is most tolerant to this change. Our code and learned mappings are publicly available at https://github.com/sashayd/mat.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-06-18</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>Jump to Conclusions</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2303.09435</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-05 09:53:08</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2303.09435 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2303.09435</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2303.09435</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_423">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Found in the related work of this paper:&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;The Curious Case of Hallucinatory (Un)answerability: Finding Truths in the Hidden States of Over-Confident Large Language Models 2023&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;/.\ I didn’t read this paper yet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt; - summary generated with AI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;The paper&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&quot;Jump to Conclusions: Short-Cutting Transformers With Linear Transformations&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;investigates how information flows through transformer-based language models (like GPT-2 and BERT) and proposes a new, efficient way to “peek” into and use the intermediate representations (hidden states) produced at earlier layers of these models.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;What Is the Problem?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Transformers process input through many layers, creating hidden representations at each step.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Normally, only the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;final layer’s output&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;is used for making predictions (e.g., generating text or classifying).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;This hides what’s happening inside the model and whether earlier layers already &quot;know&quot; enough to predict the output.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Inspecting these intermediate layers is challenging because their representations aren’t directly compatible with the final layer’s output space.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;What Does the Paper Propose?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;The authors introduce a&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;simple linear transformation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;that can map the hidden states from any intermediate layer to the space of the final layer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;By learning these linear mappings, you can&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;bypass the remaining layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;and see how well the model could perform if it &quot;jumped to conclusions&quot; earlier.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;This approach is called&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&quot;short-cutting&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;or &quot;jumping to conclusions.&quot;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Key Findings&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Early layers often already contain enough information&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;to make accurate predictions-sometimes almost as good as the final layer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Their linear transformation method&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;outperforms previous approaches&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;(which just looked at intermediate layers &quot;as is&quot;) in approximating the final output.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;This method enables&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;early exit strategies&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;: you can stop computation early (saving time and resources) if an earlier layer is already confident enough. For example, you can retain 95% accuracy while skipping about 8% of layers in GPT-2 and 5% in BERT.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;The method also shows that&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;attention sub-modules&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;in transformers are especially robust to this kind of shortcutting.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;h2 id=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: inherit&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Why Is This Important?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Efficiency&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;: You can make transformer inference faster and cheaper by exiting early when possible.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Interpretability&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;: This method provides a new way to understand what different layers in a transformer are doing and how information develops as it moves through the model.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Generalization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;: The technique works across various model sizes, architectures, and data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
