        <dc:title>CCS: Discovering Latent Knowledge in Language Models Without Supervision</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Existing techniques for training language models can be misaligned with the truth: if we train models with imitation learning, they may reproduce errors that humans make; if we train them to generate text that humans rate highly, they may output errors that human evaluators can't detect. We propose circumventing this issue by directly finding latent knowledge inside the internal activations of a language model in a purely unsupervised way. Specifically, we introduce a method for accurately answering yes-no questions given only unlabeled model activations. It works by finding a direction in activation space that satisfies logical consistency properties, such as that a statement and its negation have opposite truth values. We show that despite using no supervision and no model outputs, our method can recover diverse knowledge represented in large language models: across 6 models and 10 question-answering datasets, it outperforms zero-shot accuracy by 4\% on average. We also find that it cuts prompt sensitivity in half and continues to maintain high accuracy even when models are prompted to generate incorrect answers. Our results provide an initial step toward discovering what language models know, distinct from what they say, even when we don't have access to explicit ground truth labels.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-03-02</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03827</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-05 12:30:46</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2212.03827 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2212.03827</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2212.03827</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_434">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: ICLR 2023&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; only works for &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;binary classification tasks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;Relevant&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; because it shows like the paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;“The Curious Case of Hallucinatory (Un)answerability: Finding Truths in the Hidden States of Over-Confident Large Language Models 2023”&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;that truth and false are linearly separable in the embedding space. Relevant for some results.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;CODE provided&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Language models (LMs) often produce answers that are &lt;strong&gt;fluent but false&lt;/strong&gt;, especially when:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Trained on human-written text (which includes errors),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Rewarded for sounding convincing (even if inaccurate),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Prompted poorly.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
However, LMs may &lt;strong&gt;internally encode the correct knowledge&lt;/strong&gt;, even if their &lt;strong&gt;outputs lie or hallucinate&lt;/strong&gt;. &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Can we extract this “latent truth” from a model’s &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;hidden states&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;, even when its output is incorrect?&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We implement this idea by introducing &lt;strong&gt;Contrast-Consistent Search (CCS)&lt;/strong&gt;, a method that learns a&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;linear projection&lt;/strong&gt; of the &lt;strong&gt;hidden states&lt;/strong&gt; that is &lt;strong&gt;consistent across negations&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method: Contrast-Consistent Search (CCS)&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Goal&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;: &lt;/strong&gt;Unsupervised method to recover whether the answer to a &lt;strong&gt;yes-no question&lt;/strong&gt; is “Yes” or “No” — using &lt;strong&gt;internal model activations only&lt;/strong&gt;, not the output.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;High-Level Steps of CCS&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1) For each question&lt;/strong&gt; qi​, create two natural language statements:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
xi+: the statement assuming the answer is “Yes”
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
xi−​: the statement assuming the answer is “No”
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Example&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;: Question: &quot;Is water wet?&quot; &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;x+: &amp;nbsp; &quot;Water is wet.&quot;, &amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp;x−: &quot;Water is not wet.&quot;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For datasets that already contain statements with binary labels, like natural language inference (NLI) or sentiment classification, they can directly use the premise/hypothesis or sentence and construct the other variant via negation or polarity flipping.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;2) Extract hidden states&lt;/strong&gt; ϕ(xi+) and ϕ(xi−) using the pretrained model.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;We extract the hidden states corresponding to the last token in the last layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;3) Normalize&lt;/strong&gt; the features to remove superficial differences (e.g., the words “Yes” vs. “No”).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;where μ+ and μ− are the mean vectors for the “Yes” and “No” statements, respectively.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;VZQXJU3C&quot; width=&quot;388&quot; height=&quot;50&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;4) Learn a linear probe&lt;/strong&gt; (small model) p(ϕ(x))∈[0,1] that maps each hidden state to a probability of truth. Probe model: &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;JAI794ID&quot; width=&quot;198&quot; height=&quot;46&quot;&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;5) Train the probe&lt;/strong&gt; using an &lt;strong&gt;unsupervised loss&lt;/strong&gt; with two parts:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Consistency Loss&lt;/strong&gt;: Ensure &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;QM3ACJZB&quot; width=&quot;159&quot; height=&quot;35&quot;&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Confidence Loss&lt;/strong&gt;: Encourage confident predictions (push away from 0.5): &amp;nbsp;min (p(xi+), p(xi-) )
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Unsupervised objective:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;JQ3UEWPX&quot; width=&quot;524&quot; height=&quot;70&quot;&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;6) Inference&lt;/strong&gt;: Compute the final prediction as:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;4TLCBG7D&quot; width=&quot;279&quot; height=&quot;51&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;7) Why This Works:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The assumption is that &lt;strong&gt;truth has a logical structure&lt;/strong&gt;: a statement and its negation should have &lt;strong&gt;opposite probabilities&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
By enforcing this consistency in &lt;strong&gt;representation space&lt;/strong&gt; — not in generated text — CCS identifies &lt;strong&gt;directions correlated with truthfulness&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;8) Contrastive Representation Clustering (CRC). &lt;/strong&gt;We now show that directions correlated with the truth may be “salient” in a different way: by&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; showing that we can also find such directions using either (1) PCA or (2) clustering&lt;/span&gt;. The main difference between the representations ϕ(xi+) and &amp;nbsp;ϕ(xi-) should relate to truth. Consequently, we can take the differences in (normalized) hidden states, {ϕ(xi+) - ϕ(xi-)}i=1..n and&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;cluster them. We call this method Contrastive Representation Clustering (CRC). Clustering can be&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;achieved by&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Top Principal Component (TPC)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Bimodal Salience Search (BSS)&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;These methods also identify truth directions from contrast pairs — with &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;competitive results&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; but lower performance than CCS.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This indicates both that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(1) &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;representations of truth often lie in a high-variance direction in the contrastive representation space &amp;nbsp;{ϕ(xi+) - ϕ(xi-)}i=1..n&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;(2) true and false examples are often well-clustered in this same contrastive space.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;UD6NBKGD&quot; width=&quot;813&quot; height=&quot;485&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Models and Datasets&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;6 models:&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
GPT-J,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
T5,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
UnifiedQA,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
RoBERTa,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
DeBERTa
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;10 datasets:&lt;/strong&gt; We convert each dataset to a yes-no question-answering task or a binary classification task.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
sentiment classification: IMDB and Amazon
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
topic classification: AG-News and DBpedia-14
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
NLI: RTE, QNL
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
story completion: COPA, Story-Cloze
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
question answering: BoolQ
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
common sense reasoning: PIQA.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Baselines:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero-shot:&lt;/strong&gt; works by predicting the answer with the highest log probability according to the language model, averaged across the tokens that make up that label.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Calibrated zero-shot&lt;/strong&gt; works by balancing zero-shot predictions to be 50/50 for each answer
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CCS&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Logistic Regression&lt;/strong&gt; we train on the training split for each dataset using ( &amp;nbsp;̃φ(x+), &amp;nbsp;̃φ(x−)) as the covariates, then evaluate on the corresponding test split. We treat LR as a ceiling since it uses labeled data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;There is a &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;direction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; in &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;embedding space&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; along which “truthful” and “false” statements are &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;separated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;, since the probe identified a &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;hyperplane &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;that acts as a decision boundary between the truth and false &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;hidden representations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Even if an LLM outputs the wrong answer due to prompting issues or training biases, its &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;internal representations may still differentiate truth from falsehood&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;. CCS taps into these &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;latent semantic features&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;These truth directions are &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;not handcrafted&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; — they are &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;learned&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;They &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;generalize across tasks and prompts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;, which suggests that truth is a &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;robust feature&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; encoded in many layers of pretrained models. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Models may have a task-agnostic representation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; of the truth.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Additionally, we try deliberately prompting models to make incorrect outputs, which should intuitively change what models say but which shouldn’t affect their latent knowledge. We find that this causes zero-shot accuracy to drop by up to 9.5% without decreasing the accuracy of CCS.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
CCS &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;sometimes works best using the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;hidden states in the middle layers&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;of a network (&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;depending on the dataset&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;) &lt;/span&gt;and can work even when model outputs aren’t very informative, suggesting that it can leverage different features from those used by the outputs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Outperforms zero-shot prompting&lt;/strong&gt; on average by &lt;strong&gt;4%&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;More robust to misleading prompts&lt;/strong&gt;: even when output text is wrong, CCS remains accurate.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Transfers across tasks&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;: trained on sentiment, works on reasoning.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Works with small data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;: as few as 16 examples to train the linear probe can produce strong accuracy. We find that while CCS benefits from more data, it can often do well with very limited data.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Mid-layer features&lt;/strong&gt; sometimes outperform final-layer ones — meaning CCS doesn't just mimic the output layer.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;CCS works on &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;any binary classification task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Do not generalize well according to the paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;A Survey on Hallucination in Large Language Models: Principles, Taxonomy, Challenges, and Open Questions 2025&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;We provide empirical results that show that these methods fail to generalize in very basic ways. =&amp;gt; according to this paper:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;(Still No Lie Detector for Language Models: Probing Empirical and Conceptual Roadblocks 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>INTERESTING</dc:subject>
    </bib:Memo>
