        <dc:title>SAPLMA: The Internal State of an LLM Knows When It's Lying</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>While Large Language Models (LLMs) have shown exceptional performance in various tasks, one of their most prominent drawbacks is generating inaccurate or false information with a confident tone. In this paper, we provide evidence that the LLM's internal state can be used to reveal the truthfulness of statements. This includes both statements provided to the LLM, and statements that the LLM itself generates. Our approach is to train a classifier that outputs the probability that a statement is truthful, based on the hidden layer activations of the LLM as it reads or generates the statement. Experiments demonstrate that given a set of test sentences, of which half are true and half false, our trained classifier achieves an average of 71\% to 83\% accuracy labeling which sentences are true versus false, depending on the LLM base model. Furthermore, we explore the relationship between our classifier's performance and approaches based on the probability assigned to the sentence by the LLM. We show that while LLM-assigned sentence probability is related to sentence truthfulness, this probability is also dependent on sentence length and the frequencies of words in the sentence, resulting in our trained classifier providing a more reliable approach to detecting truthfulness, highlighting its potential to enhance the reliability of LLM-generated content and its practical applicability in real-world scenarios.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-10-17</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2304.13734</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-05 14:21:45</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2304.13734 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2304.13734</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2304.13734</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_449">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: proves once again that the internal states of a LLM know when its lying or not. Other than that, the method is not really useful. Discussion about which layer to choose =&amp;gt; depends on model.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Claim&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;: Even when a large language model (LLM) like GPT or LLaMA generates false information, it may still &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;“internally know”&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; that what it’s saying is untrue — and this can be detected from its &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;hidden activations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They introduce a method called &lt;strong&gt;SAPLMA (Statement Accuracy Prediction based on Language Model Activations)&lt;/strong&gt; to &lt;strong&gt;extract this internal knowledge&lt;/strong&gt; and determine if a statement is &lt;strong&gt;true or false&lt;/strong&gt;, based solely on the model's own hidden states.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The authors hypothesize that&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;LLMs must internally “represent” whether a sentence is true or false, since this influences future token predictions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Hypothesis&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;: LLM must have some internal notion as to whether a sentence is true or false, as this information is required for generating (or predicting) following tokens. &lt;em&gt;For example, consider an LLM generating the following false information “The sun orbits the Earth.&quot; After stating this incorrect fact, the LLM is more likely to attempt to correct itself by saying that this is a misconception from the past. But after stating a true fact, for example “The Earth orbits the sun,&quot; it is more likely to focus on other planets that orbit the sun.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;So they ask:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Can we extract that internal belief?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Can we detect when an LLM is &lt;strong&gt;lying&lt;/strong&gt;, even if it doesn’t admit it?
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3 reasons on why the LLM lies:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;1)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt; The LLM generates one token at a time and each token depends on the previous one. Committing to a word at a time may lead the LLM to be required to complete a sentence that it simply does not know how to complete.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Ex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;: The statement begins with the common words &quot;Pluto is the&quot;, then, since Pluto used to be the smallest planet the word &quot;smallest&quot; may be a very plausible choice. Once the sentence is &quot;Pluto is the smallest&quot;, completing it correctly is very challenging, and when prompted to complete the sentence, GPT-4 (March 23rd version) completes it incorrectly: “Pluto is the smallest dwarf planet in our solar system.”&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;Ex&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;: , when describing a city, it may predict that the next words should describe the city’s population. Therefore, it may include in a sentence &quot;Tiztoutine has a population of&quot;, but the population size is not present in the dataset, so it must complete the sentence with a pure guess.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;2)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;The second reason for an LLM to provide false information is that at times, there may be many ways to complete a sentence correctly (each correct answer splits the probability mass so no single correct answer gets a huge score), but fewer ways to complete it incorrectly. Therefore, it might be that a single incorrect completion may have a higher likelihood than any of the correct completions (when considered separately).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(121, 83, 227)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(95, 178, 54, 0.5)&quot;&gt;3)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;Finally, since it is common for an LLM to not use the maximal probability for the next word, but to sample according to the distribution over the words, it may sample words that result in false information.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Related work&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Contrast-Consistent Search (CCS):&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Discovering latent knowledge in language models without supervision 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; However, CCS requires rephrasing a statement into a question, evaluating the LLM on two different version of the prompt, and requires training data from the same dataset (topic) as the test set. These limitations render it &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;unsuitable for running in practice on statements generated by an LLM&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;. In addition,&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CCS increases the accuracy by only approximately 4% over the 0-shot LLM query, while our approach&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;demonstrates a nearly 20% increase over the 0-shot LLM.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SAPLMA is a &lt;strong&gt;classifier&lt;/strong&gt; trained to detect whether a statement is true or false, using &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;only the internal hidden states&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; of the LLM as it processes or generates the sentence.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;No use of:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The LLM's output token probabilities,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Any fine-tuning of the LLM,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Task-specific supervision.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;1. Dataset: True–False Statements&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They create a balanced dataset (6 topics: Cities, Inventions, Animals, Companies, Chemical Elements, Scientific Facts), with:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
True facts (e.g., “Oranjestad is a city in Aruba”),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Matched false versions (e.g., “Oranjestad is a city in Canada”).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Also includes a second dataset of &lt;strong&gt;statements generated by the LLM itself&lt;/strong&gt;, labeled as true or false by human annotators.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The dataset must cover several disjoint topics, such that a classifier can be trained on the LLM’s activations of some topics while being tested on another.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2. Extract Hidden States&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use &lt;strong&gt;OPT-6.7b&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;LLAMA2-7b&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Record the &lt;strong&gt;activations from specific layers&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., layer 20 of 32).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Try 5 layers: last, 28th, 24th, 20th, and middle (16th).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Token&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;: Only the final hidden state (activation vector) of the last token in the statement is used.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3. Train a Classifier&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Use a &lt;strong&gt;simple feedforward neural network&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
3 hidden layers (256, 128, 64 units),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
ReLU activations + sigmoid output.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Input: hidden state of the LLM when it processes the sentence.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Output: probability the statement is true.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Importantly:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Trained &lt;strong&gt;on some topics&lt;/strong&gt;, tested &lt;strong&gt;on held-out/OOD topics&lt;/strong&gt; (no overlap)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For each topic in the true-false dataset, we train the classifier using only the activation values obtained from all other topics and test its accuracy on the current topic. The classifier is trained on 5 epochs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This way, the classifier is required to determine which sentences the LLM “believes” are true and which it “believes” are false, in a general setting, and not specifically with respect to the topic being tested.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This tests &lt;strong&gt;generalization&lt;/strong&gt;, not memorization.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;On True-False Dataset (6,000+ sentences):&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SAPLMA achieves &lt;strong&gt;60%–83% accuracy&lt;/strong&gt;, depending on model and topic.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Best layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; for accuracy differs with model:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
OPT-6.7b → layer 20 (accuracy: ~71%)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
LLAMA2-7b → layer 16 (accuracy: ~83%)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Compared to Baselines:
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;table&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Method&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Average Accuracy&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;SAPLMA&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;71–83%&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Few-shot prompting (3-shot/5-shot)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;~53%&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;BERT embeddings&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;~54%&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Raw sentence probability&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;p&gt;~56%&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;On LLM-Generated Sentences:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SAPLMA reaches ~71% accuracy (after threshold tuning),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Baselines perform at chance (≈50%)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;General results:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;It shows that &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;internal hidden states&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; of LLMs carry information about the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;factuality&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; of a sentence.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;It proves that hallucinations are not necessarily due to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;lack of internal knowledge&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;, but to &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;failures in output expression&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; or &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;decoding preferences&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;The optimal layer &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;varies by model&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;, suggesting internal truth encoding is architecture-dependent.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It trains a classifier to &lt;strong&gt;extract this truth signal&lt;/strong&gt; from those hidden states.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It works even when:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The LLM &lt;strong&gt;generates a hallucination&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The sentence is syntactically valid and semantically plausible.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We show that SAPLMA, which leverages the LLM’s internal states, results in a better performance than prompting the LLM to explicitly state whether a statement is true or false.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Why Not Just Use Sentence Probability?&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;XABRCUI3&quot; width=&quot;773&quot; height=&quot;253&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They show that &lt;strong&gt;sentence likelihood&lt;/strong&gt; is a weak signal because:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It's influenced by &lt;strong&gt;token frequency&lt;/strong&gt;, syntax, and length,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
False but common statements (e.g., “The Earth is flat”) can get &lt;strong&gt;high probability&lt;/strong&gt;,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
True but unusual or awkwardly phrased facts get &lt;strong&gt;low probability&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SAPLMA, in contrast, is &lt;strong&gt;more semantically grounded&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Examples why SAPLMA perfomed better than sentence probability&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Example 1: Typo Resilience&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Statement: &lt;em&gt;“Kevin Durant is basketeer”&lt;/em&gt; → typo in &quot;basketball player&quot;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Despite very &lt;strong&gt;low sentence probability&lt;/strong&gt; due to the typo, &lt;strong&gt;SAPLMA correctly classifies it as true&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Example 2: Implicit Truth&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Statement: &lt;em&gt;“Jennifer Aniston is a female person”&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
A basic fact, but &lt;strong&gt;unlikely to appear explicitly&lt;/strong&gt; in training data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Hence, the LLM assigns it &lt;strong&gt;very low probability&lt;/strong&gt;, even lower than &lt;em&gt;“Jennifer Aniston is not an actress”&lt;/em&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SAPLMA still classifies it as true&lt;/strong&gt;, although with lower confidence.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
These examples highlight that &lt;strong&gt;probability alone fails as a truth detector&lt;/strong&gt; — especially in edge cases involving rare phrasing, typos, or implicit facts.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Best layers vary per model → may require tuning.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Currently uses final state of full sentence; &lt;strong&gt;might improve by analyzing per-token states&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Could be extended to &lt;strong&gt;detect uncertainty&lt;/strong&gt;, not just binary truth/false.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SAPLMA may inherit &lt;strong&gt;biases&lt;/strong&gt; from the LLM.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For generated sentences, &lt;strong&gt;Layer 28&lt;/strong&gt; outperformed Layer 20 in SAPLMA, which was &lt;strong&gt;unexpected&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Difference was &lt;strong&gt;statistically significant&lt;/strong&gt; (p &amp;lt; 0.05), prompting future work on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Multi-layer fusion&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Token-level activation analysis&lt;/strong&gt;, not just sentence-level
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In their experiments, each sentence was processed &lt;strong&gt;independently&lt;/strong&gt;. But real-world LLM outputs often include &lt;strong&gt;long, multi-sentence generations&lt;/strong&gt; — where activations evolve over time and mix truthful and hallucinated content. Proposed Solution: Use a &lt;strong&gt;“discrete derivative”&lt;/strong&gt; approach: (1) Subtract activation vectors of the &lt;strong&gt;previous sentence&lt;/strong&gt; from those of the &lt;strong&gt;current sentence&lt;/strong&gt;. (2) This isolates the &lt;strong&gt;change in activations&lt;/strong&gt; specific to the new statement, enabling per-statement truth detection. Importantly, &lt;strong&gt;training must match this approach&lt;/strong&gt; (i.e., train with activation differences).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;We provide empirical results that show that these methods fail to generalize in very basic ways. =&amp;gt; according to this paper:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;(Still No Lie Detector for Language Models: Probing Empirical and Conceptual Roadblocks 2024)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:relation rdf:resource="http://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03827"/>
        <dc:subject>INTERESTING</dc:subject>
    </bib:Memo>
