        <dc:title>Relative representations enable zero-shot latent space communication</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Neural networks embed the geometric structure of a data manifold lying in a high-dimensional space into latent representations. Ideally, the distribution of the data points in the latent space should depend only on the task, the data, the loss, and other architecture-specific constraints. However, factors such as the random weights initialization, training hyperparameters, or other sources of randomness in the training phase may induce incoherent latent spaces that hinder any form of reuse. Nevertheless, we empirically observe that, under the same data and modeling choices, the angles between the encodings within distinct latent spaces do not change. In this work, we propose the latent similarity between each sample and a fixed set of anchors as an alternative data representation, demonstrating that it can enforce the desired invariances without any additional training. We show how neural architectures can leverage these relative representations to guarantee, in practice, invariance to latent isometries and rescalings, effectively enabling latent space communication: from zero-shot model stitching to latent space comparison between diverse settings. We extensively validate the generalization capability of our approach on different datasets, spanning various modalities (images, text, graphs), tasks (e.g., classification, reconstruction) and architectures (e.g., CNNs, GCNs, transformers).</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-03-07</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2209.15430</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-06 07:55:42</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2209.15430 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2209.15430</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2209.15430</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_751">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;Comment: ICLR 2023 notable top 5%, 26 pages, 11 figures, 18 tables&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: interestin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;g: “&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;we empirically observe that, under the same data and modeling choices, the angles between the encodings within distinct latent spaces do not change”.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This paper introduces a novel approach to handling inconsistencies in latent representations across different neural networks or training instances.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Neural networks (NNs) are known to produce different latent spaces—even when trained on the same data and architecture—due to randomness (e.g., initialization, data order, etc.). &lt;/span&gt;This variance causes practical issues:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It's hard to compare or reuse components from different NNs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Tasks like &lt;strong&gt;model stitching&lt;/strong&gt; (reusing encoder/decoder from different models) are usually not feasible without retraining.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;Comparing latent spaces across multiple trainings becomes doable with this method. &lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
However,&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; the authors observed that while &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;absolute coordinates&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; in the latent space vary, the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;angles (cosine similarities) between representations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; remain relatively stable across different models.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The core assumption is: &lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;The angles between latent vectors are preserved across different training runs due to the underlying data structure and task&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;. This assumption holds empirically across multiple domains and is what allows the relative representation to function consistently.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;What is model stitching?&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;Model stitching&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; refers to the process of taking parts of different neural networks—typically trained independently—and combining them into a single, functioning pipeline &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;without retraining&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;. The typical use case is stitching an &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;encoder&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; from one model with a &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;decoder&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; from another. &lt;u&gt;Why is this challenging&lt;/u&gt;? Each model learns a &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;latent space&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;—the internal representation of data. However, even if two models are trained with the same data and architecture, &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;the learned latent spaces differ&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; due to randomness (e.g., initialization, data shuffling).&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;TR4XLJAY&quot; width=&quot;819&quot; height=&quot;313&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;1) Relative vs. Absolute Representations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Absolute Representation:&lt;/strong&gt; A latent vector output by a model (e.g., an embedding vector).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Relative Representation:&lt;/strong&gt; A vector of similarity scores (e.g., cosine similarities) between a sample and a fixed set of &lt;strong&gt;anchors&lt;/strong&gt; in latent space.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;=&amp;gt; This shift captures &lt;strong&gt;geometric relationships&lt;/strong&gt; rather than exact positions.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2) How It Works&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;56MEF4AK&quot; width=&quot;561&quot; height=&quot;293&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3) Why It Works&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Cosine similarity is:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Invariant&lt;/strong&gt; to isometric transformations like rotation, reflection, and scaling.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Therefore, different embeddings that are geometrically rotated but semantically similar yield the same relative representations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4) How Are Anchor Embeddings Chosen?&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Anchors are the foundation of the relative representation. Each data point is represented in terms of its &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;cosine similarity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;to these anchors in latent space.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The cosine similarity is invariant to rotations, reflections, and rescaling. While this is not true for translations, NNs commonly employ normalization techniques to center the latent spaces. Under this assumption, cosine similarity guarantees a relative representation r_x(i) invariant to angle-preserving transformations.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Goal of Anchors&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;To define a &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;coordinate system&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; over the data manifold that remains stable across latent space transformations.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;The authors discuss multiple ways of choosing anchors&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;a) &lt;strong&gt;In-domain Anchors&lt;/strong&gt; (Standard Case)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Randomly sample points from the training set.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Ensure &lt;strong&gt;diversity&lt;/strong&gt;, especially across classes for classification tasks.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;E.g., For MNIST classification, pick samples from all digits to make sure each class is well represented.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;b) &lt;strong&gt;Parallel Anchors&lt;/strong&gt; (Cross-domain/Cross-lingual)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Used when two domains (e.g., English and French) have paired data.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Choose corresponding pairs of data points (e.g., translated sentences).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;Enables stitching models across languages.&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;c) &lt;strong&gt;Out-of-Domain (OOD) Anchors&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use data that &lt;strong&gt;doesn’t come&lt;/strong&gt; from the task or training distribution.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Useful in &lt;strong&gt;domain adaptation&lt;/strong&gt; where no parallel or labeled data is available.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;em&gt;E.g., Use general sentences from Wikipedia to stitch models trained on Amazon reviews&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;u&gt;Important Factors in Anchor Selection:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Number of Anchors&lt;/strong&gt;: More anchors generally improve expressivity, but add computational cost.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Too few = poor coverage.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Too many = overfit noise, instability.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Diversity&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Anchors should capture the variance in the data.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For classification, sample uniformly from each class.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Stability&lt;/strong&gt;: The same anchors must be used across models being stitched.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;TIP2S3T2&quot; width=&quot;809&quot; height=&quot;275&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;1) Model Stitching&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They perform &lt;strong&gt;zero-shot model stitching&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use an encoder from one training instance.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use a decoder from another.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Without retraining, the stitched model works when relative representations are used.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;2) Model Similarity as a Performance Proxy&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They show that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Similarity between two relative latent spaces correlates with performance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;If two models produce &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;similar relative representations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; (i.e. their latent embeddings, when expressed relative to a shared anchor set, are close), &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;they tend to have similar performance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
More precisely, &lt;strong&gt;better-performing models tend to produce relative representations closer to a strong reference model&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Implies that &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;you can compare models by comparing their latent spaces without looking at their accuracy or predictions.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;This can be used as a &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;differentiable metric&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; to estimate model quality without labels.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;Because &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;cosine similarity is differentiable&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;, you can define a&lt;/span&gt; &lt;strong&gt;loss function or &lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;evaluation metric&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt; based on how close a model’s relative latent space is to a strong reference model.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This gives you a &lt;strong&gt;proxy for model quality&lt;/strong&gt; without needing labels or ground truth.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;3) Robustness Across Modalities and Architectures&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The method generalizes across:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Data types: images, text, graphs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Tasks: classification, reconstruction
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Architectures: CNNs, Transformers, GCNs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;4) Anchors selection&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Even &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;random anchors work surprisingly well&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; if chosen from diverse data.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Performance is robust to noisy anchors (e.g., machine-translated text).&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Cross-architecture and cross-domain stitching work, showing anchor generality.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Anchor selection&lt;/strong&gt; is critical:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use class-representative points for classification tasks.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Can be out-of-domain (OOD) for tasks like domain adaptation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Can be parallel (cross-modal/cross-lingual pairs).&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Anchor number and choice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; affect performance.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Expressivity&lt;/strong&gt; of relative representations may vary.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Future directions:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Explore&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;geodesic-based distances or other distances&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; that can enforce additional invariances&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;in the relative representation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Learnable anchor sets.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Layer-wise or multi-level stitching.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Lila: What is the relation between this paper and Hallucination detection?&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Lila: &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;While hallucination detection isn’t explicitly studied, the methods proposed in this paper could be &lt;strong&gt;used as tools&lt;/strong&gt; for it.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;The paper shows that models trained on the same task/data should have similar relative latent representations, even if their internal embeddings differ due to randomness.&lt;/span&gt; So if a model generates an embedding (or output) that doesn't align with the expected relative latent space, it might indicate:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
That the input is OOD (out-of-distribution),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
That the output is &lt;strong&gt;incoherent&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;ungrounded&lt;/strong&gt;, or &lt;strong&gt;hallucinatory&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;That could be flagged as &lt;strong&gt;potential hallucination&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They say: &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;we empirically observe that, under the same data and modeling choices, the angles between the encodings within distinct latent spaces do not change =&amp;gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;If a new input (say, at inference time) yields a latent vector whose &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;angles to the anchors&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt; are significantly different from what you'd expect based on training data, this could be an OOD data&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>INTERESTING</dc:subject>
    </bib:Memo>
