        <dc:title>Still No Lie Detector for Language Models: Probing Empirical and Conceptual Roadblocks</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>We consider the questions of whether or not large language models (LLMs) have beliefs, and, if they do, how we might measure them. First, we evaluate two existing approaches, one due to Azaria and Mitchell (2023) and the other to Burns et al. (2022). We provide empirical results that show that these methods fail to generalize in very basic ways. We then argue that, even if LLMs have beliefs, these methods are unlikely to be successful for conceptual reasons. Thus, there is still no lie-detector for LLMs. After describing our empirical results we take a step back and consider whether or not we should expect LLMs to have something like beliefs in the first place. We consider some recent arguments aiming to show that LLMs cannot have beliefs. We show that these arguments are misguided. We provide a more productive framing of questions surrounding the status of beliefs in LLMs, and highlight the empirical nature of the problem. We conclude by suggesting some concrete paths for future work.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-02-17</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>Still No Lie Detector for Language Models</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2307.00175</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-06 08:49:02</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2307.00175 [cs]</dc:description>
    </bib:Article>
    <bib:Journal rdf:about="urn:issn:0031-8116,%201573-0883">
        <dc:title>Philosophical Studies</dc:title>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.1007/s11098-023-02094-3</dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:alternative>Philos Stud</dcterms:alternative>
        <dc:identifier>ISSN 0031-8116, 1573-0883</dc:identifier>
    </bib:Journal>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_761">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: I don’t really know what to think about this paper. It mainly says that the method SAPLMA and CSS do not work. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
One strategy for addressing hallucinations is to find a way to read the beliefs of an LLM directly off its internal state.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We evaluate two existing approaches:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SAPLMA&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(The internal state of an LLM knows when it's lying 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;CSS&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Discovering latent knowledge in language models without supervision 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We provide empirical results that show that &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;these methods fail to generalize in very basic ways.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We then argue that, even if LLMs have beliefs, these methods are unlikely to be successful for conceptual reasons. Thus, there is still no lie-detector for LLMs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We consider whether or not we should expect LLMs to have something like beliefs in the first place. We consider some recent arguments aiming to show that LLMs cannot have beliefs. We show that these arguments are misguided.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We provide a more productive framing of questions surrounding the status of beliefs in LLMs, and highlight the empirical nature of the problem.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;S&lt;u&gt;ome important sentences:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;we will be focusing on autoregressive,&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; decoder-only models&lt;/span&gt; such as OpenAI’s GPT series and Meta’s&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;LLaMA series.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;In practice, we focus on the embedding associated with the last token from a late layer&lt;/span&gt;. This is due&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;to the fact that in autoregressive, decoder-only models , information flows forward. Therefore, if the LLM is processing a statement like &lt;em&gt;The earth is round&lt;/em&gt;, the embeddings associated with the initial token &lt;em&gt;The&lt;/em&gt; will not receive any information from the subsequent tokens. However, the embedding for the final word &lt;em&gt;round&lt;/em&gt; has received information from all previous tokens. Thus, if the LLM computes and stores a judgement about the truth of the statement &lt;em&gt;The earth is round,&lt;/em&gt; this information will be captured in the embedding associated&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;with &lt;em&gt;round&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The sentences in the dataset all ended with a period (i.e., full-stop) as the final token. We ran some initial tests to see if probes did better on the embedding for the period or for the penultimate token. We found it did not make much of a difference, so we&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; did our full analysis using the embeddings for the penultimate tokens&lt;/span&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;use relatively late layers&lt;/span&gt; because it seems more likely that the LLM will try to determine whether a statement is true or false after first processing lower-level semantic and syntactic information in earlier layers.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;How a probe works:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;CZIYHLAC&quot; width=&quot;633&quot; height=&quot;439&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Redo the experiments of the paper SAPLMA&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They redid the experiments of the paper &lt;strong&gt;SAPLMA&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(The internal state of an LLM knows when it's lying 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We implemented a reconstruction of the &amp;nbsp;method with several modifications:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We constructed the probes for LLaMA 30b
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We utilized an additional dataset named Capitals consisting of 10,000 examples, which was provided by the paper. It has substantial overlap with the Cities dataset, which explains some of the test accuracy.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We trained probes on 3 specific layers: the last layer (layer -1), layer 56 (layer -4), and layer 52 (layer -8).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
We took the best of ten probes (by binary classification accuracy) for each dataset and each layer instead of the best of three.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Similar to the findings of the paper, our reconstruction resulted in generally &lt;strong&gt;impressive performance.&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However: &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;probes in this setting often learn features that correlate with truth in the training set, but do not necessarily generalize well to broader contexts&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They tested the same datasets but modifying by putting the negation of some statements. =&amp;gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; accuracy of five of six of these probes was worse than chance.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Then, trained probes on all positive variants of the negated statements they were tested on =&amp;gt; &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;performance was still surprisingly poor.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;It’s likely the original &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;probes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; are not finding representations of truth within the language model embeddings.&lt;/span&gt; Instead, it seems they’re learning some other feature that correlates well with truth on the training sets but that does not correlate with truth in even mildly more general contexts.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They say that&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;using supervised learning is not the solution&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; (even if we use more data to train probe) because supervised methods do not handle well OOD anyway. &amp;nbsp;The probe most directly is learning to predict the label, not the actual truth-value.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;+a lot of other reasons in the paper stating why probes are not good for detecting the internal beliefs of an LLM.&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Redo the experiments of the paper CSS&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They redid the experiments of the paper &lt;strong&gt;CSS&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Discovering latent knowledge in language models without supervision 2022)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
In this paper the probe is trained without labels.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The loss function in the CSS methods does not take actual accuracy into account. It merely penalizes probes for lack of confidence and (one type of) probabilistic incoherence.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
the only decoder-only model tested in the study, was less impressive, with an accuracy rate of only 62.1% across all datasets. This is notably lower than the peak accuracy of 84.8% achieved by the encoder-decoder model UnifiedQA.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
They did the same tests as above for the paper SAPLMA
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
their accuracy was in effect no better than chance—it ranged from 50% to 57% depending on the training run.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;This low accuracy persisted for both linear probes and simple MLP probes across several model layers.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;5 Do LLMs even have beliefs at all?&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The authors address arguments claiming that large language models (LLMs) cannot have beliefs because they only predict the next token and do not reason with propositions or world-models. The authors argue these objections are misguided. They suggest that, according to leading theories of belief and decision-making, it is plausible that LLMs could have beliefs, since having beliefs may actually help them make better predictions&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Papers that state that LLMs do not have beliefs:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;On the dangers of stochastic parrots: Can language models be too big? 2021&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
(&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;Talking about large language models 2024&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It is a mistake to infer from the fact that the LLM outputs tokens that are likely to follows its inputs that the LLM must not have beliefs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Conclusion:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;nbsp;This leads us to the conclusion that, whether or not LLMs have beliefs, is largely an empirical matter.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Solutions proposed:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;instead of just inputting a sentence like, &lt;em&gt;Rome is the name of a country&lt;/em&gt;, we might input the prompt, &lt;em&gt;I want you to think hard about the following sentence: “Rome is the name of a country.”&lt;/em&gt; Perhaps refined versions of such prompting will make the representations of truth more perspicuous and easier for probes to find. The conceptual problems plaguing both types of probing techniques would still exist. However, the thought is that&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;if the representation of truth is especially perspicuous, the probes might land upon such a representation naturally. This is a highly empirical question.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The authors note that using prompts with clear truth values is useful for supervised learning, but it limits dataset size and may not capture situations where the language model is uncertain. To address this, they propose systematically generating prompts about probabilistic scenarios (e.g., drawing colored balls from an urn) where the truth of statements can be labeled with known probabilities. This approach allows for easier dataset creation and enables probing the model’s beliefs or uncertainty in a more nuanced way.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:relation rdf:resource="http://arxiv.org/abs/2212.03827"/>
        <dc:relation rdf:resource="http://arxiv.org/abs/2304.13734"/>
