        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Zero-Resource Hallucination Prevention for Large Language Models</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>The prevalent use of large language models (LLMs) in various domains has drawn attention to the issue of &quot;hallucination,&quot; which refers to instances where LLMs generate factually inaccurate or ungrounded information. Existing techniques for hallucination detection in language assistants rely on intricate fuzzy, specific free-language-based chain of thought (CoT) techniques or parameter-based methods that suffer from interpretability issues. Additionally, the methods that identify hallucinations post-generation could not prevent their occurrence and suffer from inconsistent performance due to the influence of the instruction format and model style. In this paper, we introduce a novel pre-detection self-evaluation technique, referred to as SELF-FAMILIARITY, which focuses on evaluating the model's familiarity with the concepts present in the input instruction and withholding the generation of response in case of unfamiliar concepts. This approach emulates the human ability to refrain from responding to unfamiliar topics, thus reducing hallucinations. We validate SELF-FAMILIARITY across four different large language models, demonstrating consistently superior performance compared to existing techniques. Our findings propose a significant shift towards preemptive strategies for hallucination mitigation in LLM assistants, promising improvements in reliability, applicability, and interpretability.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2023-10-08</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2309.02654</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-06 13:08:53</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2309.02654 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2309.02654</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2309.02654</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_772">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;My comments:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: #ffd40080&quot;&gt;/.\ I didn’t read the paper, this is a summary generated by AI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🎯 Objective&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper tackles the issue of &lt;strong&gt;hallucination&lt;/strong&gt; in large language models (LLMs) — instances where LLMs generate incorrect, fabricated, or ungrounded information. Unlike prior work that focuses on &lt;strong&gt;post-generation detection&lt;/strong&gt;, this paper introduces a &lt;strong&gt;preemptive method&lt;/strong&gt; to &lt;strong&gt;prevent&lt;/strong&gt; hallucinations &lt;strong&gt;before the model generates any response&lt;/strong&gt;, and crucially, without relying on external resources.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🚀 Proposed Method: &lt;strong&gt;SELF-FAMILIARITY&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The core idea is to &lt;strong&gt;simulate human-like self-assessment&lt;/strong&gt;: if an LLM is unfamiliar with certain concepts in a user instruction, it should &lt;strong&gt;avoid responding&lt;/strong&gt; to reduce the chance of hallucination.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The method follows three key steps:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;1. 🧠 Concept Extraction&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Goal&lt;/strong&gt;: Identify key concepts in the user instruction to isolate the meaningful parts that the model needs to understand.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Uses &lt;strong&gt;Named Entity Recognition (NER)&lt;/strong&gt; to extract concept entities.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Applies &lt;strong&gt;Concept Grouping&lt;/strong&gt; to fuse split or partial concepts (e.g., [&quot;2023&quot;, &quot;United States&quot;, &quot;debt-ceiling crisis&quot;] → &quot;2023 United States debt-ceiling crisis&quot;).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Applies &lt;strong&gt;Concept Filtering&lt;/strong&gt; to discard “common concepts” (e.g., &quot;age&quot;, &quot;year&quot;) using a word frequency list from Wiktionary.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;2. 🎲 Concept Guessing (Core Self-Evaluation Step)&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Goal&lt;/strong&gt;: Assess whether the model is “familiar” with each extracted concept.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;It mimics a “Charades-style” game:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Step 1: Concept Explanation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;The model is prompted to &lt;strong&gt;explain a concept&lt;/strong&gt; in its own words.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This explanation is then &lt;strong&gt;masked&lt;/strong&gt; to prevent cheating (e.g., copying the concept verbatim).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Step 2: Concept Inference&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;The model is prompted to &lt;strong&gt;guess the masked concept&lt;/strong&gt; based on the explanation it previously gave.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Uses &lt;strong&gt;Constrained Beam Search&lt;/strong&gt; to force the model to regenerate the correct concept.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;strong&gt;highest log probability score&lt;/strong&gt; of successful regeneration is treated as the &lt;strong&gt;familiarity score&lt;/strong&gt; for that concept.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h3&gt;3. 📊 Aggregation&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Goal&lt;/strong&gt;: Compute a final familiarity score at the &lt;strong&gt;instruction level&lt;/strong&gt;, combining all individual concept scores.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Concept Frequency Score&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Weights each concept by how rare it is (less common concepts contribute more to the risk of hallucination).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Weighted Aggregation&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br&gt;Combines individual familiarity scores, giving more importance to rare and informative concepts.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;If the final familiarity score is &lt;strong&gt;below a threshold&lt;/strong&gt;, the system &lt;strong&gt;refuses to answer&lt;/strong&gt; the prompt — effectively &lt;strong&gt;preventing hallucinations&lt;/strong&gt; from being generated.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🧪 Experiments&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Dataset&lt;/strong&gt;: The authors build a new &lt;strong&gt;Concept-7 dataset&lt;/strong&gt; with:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
192 basic concepts.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
180 test concepts from &lt;strong&gt;7 expert domains&lt;/strong&gt; (Medical, Finance, Music, Art, Legal, Physics, History).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Includes &lt;strong&gt;fabricated fictional concepts&lt;/strong&gt; to simulate zero-familiarity.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Models Tested&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Vicuna-13b-v1.3
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Falcon-7b-instruct
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
MPT-7b-instruct
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Alpaca-7b
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Baselines Compared&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Greedy-Perplexity
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Avg/Min Log Prob
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Forward Inference (Yes/No)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
BERTScore &amp;amp; SentenceScore sampling
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SelfCheckGPT-like methods
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;📈 Results&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;SELF-FAMILIARITY consistently outperforms all baselines&lt;/strong&gt; in AUC, accuracy, F1, and Pearson correlation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
It shows &lt;strong&gt;robust performance across different model architectures&lt;/strong&gt;, unlike baselines that often fail on certain models.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Human evaluation and ablation studies confirm its effectiveness and interpretability.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;🧑‍⚕️ Case Study: &quot;Beyfortus&quot; Drug&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Without SELF-FAMILIARITY: the model hallucinates that Beyfortus treats diabetes.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
With SELF-FAMILIARITY: the system detects low familiarity, &lt;strong&gt;refuses to answer&lt;/strong&gt;, and recommends searching for reliable info.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Result: with external support, the model provides the &lt;strong&gt;correct&lt;/strong&gt; use of Beyfortus for RSV prevention.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;h2&gt;✅ Strengths&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Zero-resource&lt;/strong&gt;: Requires no external knowledge base or search engine.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Model-agnostic&lt;/strong&gt;: Works across different LLMs.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Preemptive&lt;/strong&gt;: Prevents hallucinations instead of reacting after they occur.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Interpretable&lt;/strong&gt;: Pinpoints which concept the model is unfamiliar with.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Flexible&lt;/strong&gt;: Can complement post-detection correction methods.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
    </bib:Memo>
