        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_774"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_775"/>
        <dc:title>CED: Comparing Embedding Differences for Detecting Out-of-Distribution and Hallucinated Text</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Detecting out-of-distribution (OOD) samples is crucial for ensuring the safety and robustness of models deployed in real-world scenarios. While most studies on OOD detection focus on fine-tuned models trained on in-distribution (ID) data, detecting OOD in pre-trained models is also important due to computational limitations and the widespread use of open-source pre-trained models. However, in the same domain shift setting, the OOD detection performance of pre-trained models is insufficient because both ID and OOD samples originate from the same domain, leading to a high overlap in their embeddings. To address this issue, we introduce a new method called CED, a training-free OOD detection technique designed to enhance the distinction between ID and OOD datasets. We theoretically validate that specific auxiliary and oracle samples that satisfy certain conditions improve this distinction. Motivated by our theoretical analysis, CED enhances the differentiation by utilizing these specially designed auxiliary and oracle samples. As a result, CED significantly improves the ability of pre-trained models to distinguish between ID and OOD samples in text classification and hallucination detection tasks. Furthermore, we verify that CED is a plug-and-play method compatible with various backbone networks, such as RoBERTa, Llama, and OpenAI Embedding.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2024-11</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>CED</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>ACLWeb</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
                <rdf:value>https://aclanthology.org/2024.findings-emnlp.874/</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-07 08:51:12</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <bib:pages>14866–14882</bib:pages>
        <bib:presentedAt>
           <bib:Conference><dc:title>Findings 2024</dc:title></bib:Conference>
        </bib:presentedAt>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_774">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments: Méthode un peu compliquée, je pense pas trop que ça va servir &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;MAIS &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;c’est le seul papier qui teste du OOD sur de l’Hallucination et ils ont de bons résultats pas si mauvais alors ça ouvre la voie pour tester d’autres méthodes OOD sur de l’hallucination &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;This paper does not have any citation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;. But I chose it because it is the &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;ONLY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; one that frames a little bit OOD detection as Hallucination detection&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Introduction:&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper proposes &lt;strong&gt;CED&lt;/strong&gt; (Comparing Embedding Differences), a &lt;strong&gt;training-free&lt;/strong&gt; method designed to detect:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Out-of-Distribution (OOD) inputs&lt;/strong&gt; in classification tasks (e.g., intent classification, topic classification),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Hallucinated generations&lt;/strong&gt; in text generation tasks (e.g., summarization, QA).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Most existing OOD methods rely on:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Classifier confidence (softmax probabilities),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Mahalanobis distances to class centers,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Generative likelihoods (log-probs),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Trained detectors or classifiers.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;However, &lt;strong&gt;these techniques suffer&lt;/strong&gt; when:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
OOD examples lie &lt;strong&gt;close to ID examples&lt;/strong&gt; in embedding space (&quot;hard OOD&quot; or “near-OOD”)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The detector needs &lt;strong&gt;additional training&lt;/strong&gt; or domain-specific adaptation.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;CED is designed to &lt;strong&gt;address these weaknesses&lt;/strong&gt; by:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Comparing how a test sample interacts with &lt;strong&gt;similar (oracle)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;dissimilar (auxiliary)&lt;/strong&gt; ID examples,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Measuring these interactions &lt;strong&gt;in embedding space&lt;/strong&gt;, using pretrained models only.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The paper states: &lt;em&gt;“An ID sample and an OOD sample can appear close to each other in the embedding space (especially hard OOD), but their &lt;/em&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;relationship with the rest of the ID data&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;em&gt; can still differ.”&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Rather than just measure how far a sample is from a class center (as Mahalanobis-based detectors do), CED asks:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;How does this sample relate to the rest of the ID distribution?&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Do its relationships (differences in embeddings) behave like typical ID samples or not?&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This relational approach is what sets CED apart.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Method&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 1: Feature Extraction from a Pretrained Model&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Given a &lt;strong&gt;test sample&lt;/strong&gt; x_t​, extract its feature representation f(x_t) using a &lt;strong&gt;frozen, pretrained model&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., RoBERTa, LLaMA-3, OpenAI embeddings).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This could be:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;strong&gt;[CLS] token embedding&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., RoBERTa),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The &lt;strong&gt;average of token embeddings&lt;/strong&gt; (OpenAI),
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Or the &lt;strong&gt;last-token hidden state&lt;/strong&gt; (LLaMA).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 2: Constructing Comparison Sets&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;a) Oracle Samples&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt; x_o1,x_o2,...,x_oM&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These are:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Samples that belong to the &lt;strong&gt;same predicted class&lt;/strong&gt; as x_t​, as inferred from a basic classifier (e.g., Mahalanobis nearest-class).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Among those, you choose the &lt;strong&gt;closest M neighbors&lt;/strong&gt; to x_t in the embedding space.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Goal: Find &lt;strong&gt;typical ID examples&lt;/strong&gt; that are similar to the &lt;strong&gt;test sample.&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;(b) &lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Auxiliary Samples&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt; x_a1,x_a2,...,x_aN&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;These are:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Most dissimilar examples&lt;/strong&gt; from the dataset (e.g., farthest away from x_t​ in embedding space).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Also used: sampling from classes &lt;strong&gt;different from the predicted one&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Goal: Create a &lt;strong&gt;contrast set&lt;/strong&gt; to stress-test the sample's embedding behavior.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 3: Combination and Relational Embedding Differences&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Compute &lt;strong&gt;how the sample and oracles relate to the auxiliaries&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;For each auxiliary x_aj&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;1) Create &lt;strong&gt;concatenated pairs&lt;/strong&gt; (or interactions):&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;math&quot;&gt;$\hat{x}_{taj}=[x_t,x_{aj}]$&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;math&quot;&gt;$\hat{x}_{oiaj}=[x_{oi},x_{aj}]$&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;2) Pass each combined pair through the embedding model:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span class=&quot;math&quot;&gt;$f(\hat{x}_{taj}), f(\hat{x}_{oiaj})$&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;3) Compute &lt;strong&gt;embedding differences&lt;/strong&gt; between:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
x_t and its oracles
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Combined pairs vs. oracle+aux pairs
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The idea is:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For &lt;strong&gt;ID inputs&lt;/strong&gt;, these differences should remain &lt;strong&gt;stable&lt;/strong&gt; — predictable and consistent.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
For &lt;strong&gt;OOD or hallucinated inputs&lt;/strong&gt;, the interaction with ID samples becomes &lt;strong&gt;less stable&lt;/strong&gt;, producing a &lt;strong&gt;larger relational gap&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Step 4: CED Scoring Function&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The final &lt;strong&gt;CED score&lt;/strong&gt; aggregates the distance metrics from the above steps: &lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;7LPMJ9NK&quot; width=&quot;665&quot; height=&quot;72&quot;&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Where:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
h(⋅): distance metric (Mahalanobis, KNN, Cosine)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
α,β: weight hyperparameters
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Interpretation&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The first term: similarity between test and oracle.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The second term: how the test+aux combo differs from oracle+aux.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
High scores = &lt;strong&gt;less typical behavior&lt;/strong&gt; → likely OOD or hallucinated.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Theoretical Justification&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The paper proves that:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
When OOD points are close to ID (i.e., hard OOD), auxiliary samples chosen in the &lt;strong&gt;direction opposite to the oracle&lt;/strong&gt; yield the &lt;strong&gt;largest embedding distortions&lt;/strong&gt; — making them easier to detect.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Under certain assumptions (linear separability, Gaussian class distributions), CED offers &lt;strong&gt;higher separation margins&lt;/strong&gt; than vanilla Mahalanobis detection.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;They validate this theory with:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Synthetic 1D and 2D examples&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., two-class Gaussians)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Real-world embeddings&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., RoBERTa, LLaMA-3)
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Application to Hallucination Detection&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
The authors extend CED beyond classification to &lt;strong&gt;detect hallucinations&lt;/strong&gt; in generative text (e.g., summaries, QA answers).
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Process:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Treat a &lt;strong&gt;generated output&lt;/strong&gt; as the test sample.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Use &lt;strong&gt;Wikipedia articles&lt;/strong&gt; (filtered and de-overlapped) as in-distribution data to construct oracles and auxiliaries.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Compute CED scores based on the &lt;strong&gt;embedding behavior of generations&lt;/strong&gt; when paired with ID texts.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hallucinations tend to:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Drift in embedding space,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Show &lt;strong&gt;less alignment&lt;/strong&gt; with ID relational patterns,
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Produce &lt;strong&gt;higher CED scores&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;CED (especially with cosine + LLaMA-3 embeddings) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;outperforms all zero-shot baselines&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;, including:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
SelfCheckGPT
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Predictive Entropy
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
GPT-4 HDM hallucination detector
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;SCG-NLI&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;OOD Detection results (classification task):&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Datasets&lt;/strong&gt;: SST-2, 20News, DBPedia, AGNews, etc.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Base models&lt;/strong&gt;: RoBERTa, OpenAI text embeddings, LLaMA-3
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Metrics: FPR@95, AUROC&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;UGHDEVLZ&quot; width=&quot;765&quot; height=&quot;548&quot;&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;u&gt;Hallucination Detection results&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; data-attachment-key=&quot;2I7IIVL8&quot; width=&quot;742&quot; height=&quot;311&quot;&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Datasets: XSUM, NarrativeQA, BioASQ
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Metric: Correlation with human hallucination labels
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;hr&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;Limitations&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Currently applied only to &lt;strong&gt;English language tasks&lt;/strong&gt;.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Embedding quality is crucial — performance may vary across model architectures.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Relies on proper sampling of &lt;strong&gt;oracle and auxiliary&lt;/strong&gt; sets.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Assumes clean in-distribution data is available.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>INTERESTING</dc:subject>
    </bib:Memo>
