        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_800"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_799"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_798"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Artificial Intelligence</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Layer by Layer: Uncovering Hidden Representations in Language Models</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>From extracting features to generating text, the outputs of large language models (LLMs) typically rely on their final layers, following the conventional wisdom that earlier layers capture only low-level cues. However, our analysis shows that intermediate layers can encode even richer representations, often improving performance on a wide range of downstream tasks. To explain and quantify these hidden-layer properties, we propose a unified framework of representation quality metrics based on information theory, geometry, and invariance to input perturbations. Our framework highlights how each model layer balances information compression and signal preservation, revealing why mid-depth embeddings can exceed the last layer's performance. Through extensive experiments on 32 text-embedding tasks and comparisons across model architectures (transformers, state-space models) and domains (language, vision), we demonstrate that intermediate layers consistently provide stronger features. These findings challenge the standard focus on final-layer embeddings and open new directions for model analysis and optimization, including strategic use of mid-layer representations for more robust and accurate AI systems.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2025-02-04</dc:date>
        <z:shortTitle>Layer by Layer</z:shortTitle>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2502.02013</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-09 07:48:59</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2502.02013 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2502.02013</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2502.02013</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_800">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;/.\ I didn’t read this paper yet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; - summary generated with AI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Layer by Layer: Uncovering Hidden Representations in Language Models&lt;/strong&gt; – &lt;em&gt;Oscar Skean, Md Rifat Arefin, Dan Zhao, Niket Patel, Jalal Naghiyev, Yann LeCun, Ravid Shwartz-Ziv (2025, arXiv)&lt;/em&gt;.&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Focus:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;Contextual hidden states at each layer – information content &amp;amp; quality.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Summary:&lt;/strong&gt; A comprehensive &lt;strong&gt;layer-wise analysis&lt;/strong&gt; of transformer LLMs,&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; showing that &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;intermediate layers often produce representations more useful than the final layer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt; for downstream tasks&lt;/span&gt;. The authors introduce information-theoretic and geometric metrics to quantify each layer’s representation quality. They find that &lt;strong&gt;mid-layer embeddings can encode richer semantic features and even outperform final-layer embeddings on many tasks&lt;/strong&gt;, challenging the assumption that only final states are task-optimal. Each layer balances &lt;strong&gt;information compression vs. signal preservation&lt;/strong&gt;, and mid-depth layers strike an optimal trade-off, sometimes retaining task-relevant details that later layers compress away.&lt;br&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Techniques:&lt;/strong&gt; They evaluate hidden-state embeddings from &lt;strong&gt;every layer&lt;/strong&gt; on 32 tasks (MTEB benchmark) and use metrics like representation similarity and invariance. Geometric analyses (e.g. measuring layer embedding spread and clustering) reveal how lower layers capture general features, mid-layers achieve high separability for concepts, and very deep layers become too specialized. These findings suggest using intermediate representations can yield &lt;em&gt;more robust text embeddings&lt;/em&gt; and provide insight into how &lt;strong&gt;semantic information is gradually transformed and sometimes lost&lt;/strong&gt; across a model’s depth.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;Early layers tend to capture basic syntactic patterns, middle layers refine these into more contextually rich features, and the final layers may become overly specialized to the model’s training objective&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
Also this paper&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(VisBERT: Hidden-State Visualizations for Transformers 2020)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; states that: &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;Early layers cluster tokens by lexical or syntactic roles, while middle layers might bring together tokens related to the question’s topic, and later layers isolate the specific answer candidates.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
This paper&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 119, 0)&quot;&gt;(Eliciting Latent Predictions from Transformers with the Tuned Lens 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt; found that: transformers perform iterative inference: &lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;early layers make rough guesses, and later layers systematically refine or correct these guesses.&lt;/span&gt; For example, on a reasoning task, an early layer might lean towards a plausible but incorrect token, while successive layers adjust the distribution toward the correct answer as more context or computation is integrated.
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This paper&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(An Attempt to Unraveling Token Prediction Refinement and Identifying Essential Layers of LLMs 2025)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;iidentifies&amp;nbsp;specific layers that are most critical&amp;nbsp;for incorporating relevant information and finalizing the correct prediction. Their findings show that:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The&amp;nbsp;middle layers&amp;nbsp;often serve as key “refinement layers” where the model first predicts the correct token.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;The&amp;nbsp;gap between the first correct prediction layer and the final correct prediction layer&amp;nbsp;varies depending on the position of relevant information in the input.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;When relevant information is in the middle of the context, the model requires more layers to refine and finalize the prediction, forming an inverted U-shaped pattern in terms of refinement effort across layers.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Thus, the paper highlights that&amp;nbsp;some middle-to-late layers are essential&amp;nbsp;for integrating and refining informtion, while early and very late layers are less critical for final prediction accuracy.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>INTERESTING</dc:subject>
    </bib:Memo>
