    <rdf:Description rdf:about="http://arxiv.org/abs/2503.21073">
        <z:itemType>preprint</z:itemType>
        <dc:publisher>
           <foaf:Organization><foaf:name>arXiv</foaf:name></foaf:Organization>
        </dc:publisher>
        <bib:authors>
            <rdf:Seq>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Lee</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Andrew</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Weber</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Melanie</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Viégas</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Fernanda</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
                <rdf:li>
                    <foaf:Person>
                        <foaf:surname>Wattenberg</foaf:surname>
                        <foaf:givenName>Martin</foaf:givenName>
                    </foaf:Person>
                </rdf:li>
            </rdf:Seq>
        </bib:authors>
        <dcterms:isReferencedBy rdf:resource="#item_804"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_803"/>
        <link:link rdf:resource="#item_802"/>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Machine Learning</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:subject>
            <z:AutomaticTag>
               <rdf:value>Computer Science - Computation and Language</rdf:value>
            </z:AutomaticTag>
        </dc:subject>
        <dc:title>Shared Global and Local Geometry of Language Model Embeddings</dc:title>
        <dcterms:abstract>Researchers have recently suggested that models share common representations. In our work, we find that token embeddings of language models exhibit common geometric structure. First, we find ``global'' similarities: token embeddings often share similar relative orientations. Next, we characterize local geometry in two ways: (1) by using Locally Linear Embeddings, and (2) by defining a simple measure for the intrinsic dimension of each token embedding. Our intrinsic dimension demonstrates that token embeddings lie on a lower dimensional manifold. We qualitatively show that tokens with lower intrinsic dimensions often have semantically coherent clusters, while those with higher intrinsic dimensions do not. Both characterizations allow us to find similarities in the local geometry of token embeddings. Perhaps most surprisingly, we find that alignment in token embeddings persists through the hidden states of language models, allowing us to develop an application for interpretability. Namely, we introduce Emb2Emb, a simple method to transfer steering vectors from one language model to another, despite the two models having different dimensions.</dcterms:abstract>
        <dc:date>2025-04-24</dc:date>
        <z:libraryCatalog>arXiv.org</z:libraryCatalog>
        <dc:identifier>
            <dcterms:URI>
               <rdf:value>http://arxiv.org/abs/2503.21073</rdf:value>
            </dcterms:URI>
        </dc:identifier>
        <dcterms:dateSubmitted>2025-05-09 07:59:21</dcterms:dateSubmitted>
        <dc:description>arXiv:2503.21073 [cs]</dc:description>
        <dc:identifier>DOI 10.48550/arXiv.2503.21073</dc:identifier>
        <prism:number>arXiv:2503.21073</prism:number>
    </rdf:Description>
    <bib:Memo rdf:about="#item_804">
        <rdf:value>&lt;div data-schema-version=&quot;9&quot;&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;My comments:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt;/.\ I didn’t read this paper yet&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: rgba(255, 212, 0, 0.5)&quot;&gt; - summary generated with AI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Global similarity:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;The study finds that token embeddings (the vectors representing words/tokens) from different models within the same family tend to have &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;similar relative orientations&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; in their high-dimensional spaces. This means that &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;the geometric relationships between tokens&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt; (like the angle between &quot;cat&quot; and &quot;dog&quot;) &lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: rgb(255, 32, 32)&quot;&gt;are preserved across models, even if their absolute coordinates or dimensions differ. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;For example, if “cat” and “dog” are close together in one model, they’ll be close together in the other model too, and the “direction” from “cat” to “dog” is about the same. So, the “shape” of the word space is shared, even if the actual coordinates are not.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;=&amp;gt; &lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;Lila: This is also what the paper&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff7700&quot;&gt;(Relative representations enable zero-shot latent space communication 2023)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/em&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;shows&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #7953e3&quot;&gt;. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Local Geometry: Structure in the Neighborhood: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Now, zoom in on a single word, like “apple.” The authors look at which words are closest to “apple” in this space-maybe “fruit,” “banana,” and “pear.” They use a method (Locally Linear Embeddings, or LLE) to see if you can rebuild the “apple” vector by mixing together its neighbors. They find that not only are the neighbors similar across models, but the way you combine them to get “apple” is also similar. So, the tiny “neighborhood” around each word looks almost the same in different models.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Intrinsic Dimension: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;This is a way of asking: “How complicated is the space around each word?” For most words, you only need a few directions to describe all the ways they can vary-meaning the space is actually much simpler than it looks at first. Words with simple, clear meanings (like colors or animals) tend to cluster together in these low-dimensional spaces, while more complex or ambiguous words don’t.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;Persistence Through Hidden States: &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: oklch(0.304 0.04 213.681)&quot;&gt;As the model processes a sentence, it transforms these word vectors at each step (layer). The surprising finding is that this geometric similarity-the way words are arranged and related-sticks around, not just at the start, but through all the layers of the model. The model keeps “thinking about” words in similar ways as it reads. &lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;color: #ff2020&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;background-color: oklch(0.99 0.004 106.471)&quot;&gt;his means that the way information is represented remains similar at different stages of computation. &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;</rdf:value>
        <dc:subject>INTERESTING</dc:subject>
    </bib:Memo>
